    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG004:links:Link:00CBFDYYCR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG009",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG004:links:Link:00CBFDYYCR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYD0000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "329"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG004",
    "text": "`List`-typed fields (this project's actual containment shape — order is semantically meaningful)\n  give plain `triple()` no shortcut in *either* direction: reverse lookup finds nothing at all;\n  forward lookup binds to TerminusDB's internal `Cons` cell, not the member value, requiring an\n  explicit `rdf:first`/`rdf:rest` walk ([§3](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/3. The concrete design]]>)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG005:links:Link:00CBFDYYD8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG005:links:Link:00CBFDYYD8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYD8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "150"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG005",
    "text": "`@abstract` classes don't materialize as GraphQL interfaces — any field typed to one loses\n  subclass-specific data without the `_json` escape hatch ([§2](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/2. What confirmed this isn't incidental]]>)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG006:links:Link:00CBFDYYDR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG003",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG006:links:Link:00CBFDYYDR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYDR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "416"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG006",
    "text": "Node-by-node (\"paired\") tree reconstruction, benchmarked live across all three APIs, lost to\n  bulk fetch by 47–240x — not because any one API is badly built, but because the heterogeneous-\n  node problem (no API can generically ask \"whatever fields this type has\" without knowing the\n  type first) forces per-type fragment queries plus client-side stitching, work TerminusDB's bulk\n  path already does server-side ([§2](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/2. What confirmed this isn't incidental]]>)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG007:links:Link:00CBFDYYE8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG009",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG007:links:Link:00CBFDYYE8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYE8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "326"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG007",
    "text": "Two real upstream bugs, filed and confirmed live: the cross-store `bundle`/`unbundle`\n  incompatibility (`terminusdb/terminusdb#2509`) and the `List`-typed `@unfold` gap\n  (`terminusdb/terminusdb#2512`) — both already cited in `Aperas-design.md`'s Phase 4 contingency,\n  now with the second one's root cause traced precisely ([§3](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/3. The concrete design]]>): property-level unfold never reaches\n  into the `Cons` chain the way class-level `@unfoldable` does).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG008",
    "text": "A real server-side panic (byte-index string truncation landing inside a multi-byte UTF-8\n  character), reproduced twice against real content, not a one-off.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. The concrete design",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXNR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXP0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG009",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00T"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00B",
    "text": "**Engine: Oxigraph**, not a from-scratch build. A mature, embeddable (in-process, Rust) RDF\n  store with genuine SPO/POS/OSP multi-index storage — the original Phase 4 sketch called for \"a\n  small, embeddable Rust graph engine... no general Datalog/Prolog solver, no auto-generated\n  GraphQL schema\"; Oxigraph is a real, off-the-shelf instance of exactly that shape, not a gap\n  ApeironNgn needs to fill from zero.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00C",
    "text": "**Ordered containment: reified triples, not `rdf:List`.** Every child carries its own `parent`\n  and `siblingIndex` as direct, bounded-degree triples (`(child, parent, P)`, `(child,\n  siblingIndex, N)`) instead of a `Cons`-chain collection. This isn't a workaround for TerminusDB's\n  specific `List` bug — it sidesteps the entire class of problem RDF's native list encoding has:\n  no random access, no reverse index on membership, because a list is fundamentally a linked\n  structure rather than a set of ordinary triples. Both `parent` (in-degree ≤ 1, structurally, for\n  a tree) and `siblingIndex` are cheap in *either* direction on Oxigraph's ordinary indexes; order\n  is recovered by sorting an already-fetched set, not by traversing a chain.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00D",
    "text": "**Bulk/tree fetch: self-implemented, paired, level-wise.** A recursive reverse-index walk —\n  fetch a node's own fields, reverse-query its children via the `parent` index, sort by\n  `siblingIndex`, recurse — the same \"control structure paired with data structure\" model examined\n  earlier against TerminusDB, except now viable: each hop is an in-process lookup, not an HTTP\n  round trip, so the 47–240x penalty node-by-node paid under TerminusDB doesn't apply here. This is\n  real, new engineering surface — TerminusDB's bulk GraphQL path did this stitching for free;\n  ApeironNgn has to build it, deliberately, as the trade for owning the shape.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00E",
    "text": "**Schema: the OOP class itself**, not a declarative schema language. The class enforces shape on\n  every read/write; an unmatched read/write either fails hard or fills the gap, per the class's own\n  policy — no second, independently-evolving schema representation for the class to drift from.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00F:links:Link:00CBFDYYEG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00V",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00F:links:Link:00CBFDYYEG000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYER000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "942"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00F",
    "text": "**Field shape: declared once, per concrete class — not inferred from data.** A `Set`-typed field\n  holding exactly one value must stay distinguishable from an `Optional` field that happens to be\n  set — a plain match-count check can't tell them apart. The fix: a small table each concrete class\n  owns, declaring per field its **cardinality** (`one` / `optional` / `set` / the special\n  `orderedContainment` used only by `children`) and, for node-shaped values, its **storage kind**\n  (`reference`, a bare id — `links`/`parent`/`root`; or `embed`, a full nested object — `props`,\n  since `Prop`/`StringProp` are `@subdocument`). `fieldValue` consults this table\n  for known fields, falling back to match-count inference only for a field the table doesn't cover\n  (fine for ad hoc exploration, not relied on for anything round-tripped). Deliberately not derived\n  from TerminusDB's `schema.json` — already shown to drift from the live schema ([§4](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence]]>)'s `Link` fix,\n  the `ordered`/`start` field discrepancy) — authored directly against the concrete classes\n  ApeironNgn actually has, the same \"no second, independently-evolving schema representation\"\n  stance as the bullet above, extended from *presence* to *shape*. It's also the direct\n  prerequisite for JSON-LD export/dehydrate (rehydration's inverse, not yet built): serializing a\n  node back to a document needs exactly this same fact — bare value vs. array, id string vs.\n  embedded object — which exists nowhere queryable in the engine today. Concretely, for two of the\n  five concrete classes:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00H"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00G",
    "text": "```ts\n  BlockNode: {\n    blockId: { cardinality: 'one' },                              // literal\n    type:    { cardinality: 'one' },                               // literal\n    title:   { cardinality: 'one' },                               // literal\n    text:    { cardinality: 'optional' },                          // literal\n    parent:  { cardinality: 'optional', storageKind: 'reference' }, // -> BaseNode\n    children:{ cardinality: 'orderedContainment', storageKind: 'reference' }, // -> BlockNode\n    links:   { cardinality: 'set', storageKind: 'reference' },      // -> Link (bare id)\n    props:   { cardinality: 'set', storageKind: 'embed' },          // -> Prop (full object)\n  }\n  Link: {\n    target:    { cardinality: 'one', storageKind: 'reference' },    // -> BaseNode\n    predicate: { cardinality: 'one' },                              // literal\n  }\n  ```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00F",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
