    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00H",
    "text": "So a `BlockNode` with one `links` entry serializes as `\"links\": [\"Link/xyz\"]` (array, bare id),\n  never `\"links\": \"Link/xyz\"`; one `props` entry serializes as `\"props\": [{\"@id\": ..., \"@type\":\n  \"StringProp\", \"key\": ..., \"value\": ...}]` (array, full object); `title` always serializes as a\n  bare string, never `[\"…\"]`, regardless of how the underlying `match()` call happens to shape its\n  result. `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` follow the same pattern for their own scalar fields\n  (`one`/`optional`), each inheriting `links`/`props`/`title`/`text` from `BaseNode`/`TreeNode` as\n  before; `ArtifactNode` alone also inherits `type`/`parent`/`children` from `BlockNode` ([§4](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence]]>) [Step 7](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence/Step 7: `ArtifactNode extends BlockNode`, the root block retired outright, `text` a copied abstract for both — implemented, verified]]>)\n  — originally a separate `root` reference field; `ArtifactNode` was later merged with its own root\n  document outright, `FolderNode` deliberately left out of that merge — see [Step 7](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence/Step 7: `ArtifactNode extends BlockNode`, the root block retired outright, `text` a copied abstract for both — implemented, verified]]>) for why).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00F",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00J:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXPG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00J:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXPG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00J",
    "text": "**Schema = class — implemented and live-verified, not just designed.** One real class per\n  concrete type, not the single generic function `wrapNode` used to be:\n  `web/src/lib/apeironNgn/shape.ts` (the `SHAPE` tables), `classes.ts` (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`, `Link`, `StringProp`, each carrying only its own `static SHAPE`), and `node.ts`'s\n  `wrap(store, id)` (replacing `wrapNode`), branching into three parts:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00P"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00K",
    "text": "**Constructor / registry lookup.** `CLASS_BY_KIND`, keyed by the id prefix (`\"BlockNode\"` →\n    `BlockNode`, etc., via `vocab.ts`'s `nodeKindFromId`) — the one generic dispatch point left,\n    keyed directly off the id scheme `snowflake.ts` already mints ids by. One real wrinkle found\n    implementing this: a `props` subdocument's own id is shaped `${parentId}/props/StringProp/\n    <snowflake>` — the *owning* node's class prefix leads the string, so a naive leading-prefix\n    check would misclassify every `StringProp` as whatever its parent happens to be.\n    `nodeKindFromId` checks for `/props/ClassName/` first, falling back to the leading prefix\n    otherwise — live-verified against a real `props`/`links` read (below).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00M",
    "text": "**`get` trap**, reading through the class's own `SHAPE`, not match-count inference. A known\n    field decodes per its declared cardinality; an unknown field returns `undefined` (safe for ad\n    hoc exploration, not relied on for anything round-tripped). Live-verified: a synthetic\n    `BlockNode` with exactly one `props` entry and one `links` entry — the exact case that used to\n    misbehave — now reads back as real one-element arrays (`Array.isArray` true), each entry\n    correctly resolved to its own `key`/`value` or `target`/`predicate` fields.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00N",
    "text": "**`set` trap — the first real write path into the `Store`.** Validates the assigned value\n    against `SHAPE`, then translates to `store.delete`/`store.add` calls. Live-verified for every\n    shape: a literal `one` field, an `optional` reference, a `set` of existing references, a `set`\n    of `embed`-kind entries (minting a fresh `StringProp` id and writing its own literal fields),\n    `orderedContainment` reindexing (replacing an existing `children` set correctly detaches the\n    dropped members — no stray `__parent`/`__siblingIndex` quads left behind), an unknown field\n    throwing, and clearing a required field throwing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00P:links:Link:00CBFDYYGR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00V",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00P:links:Link:00CBFDYYGR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYGR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "34"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00P:links:Link:00CBFDYYH0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00P:links:Link:00CBFDYYH0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYH0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "497"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00P",
    "text": "**Mere schema, deliberately — see [§4](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence]]>)'s rollout step 3.** A class here is field access and\n    shape enforcement only, no behavior. That's what makes migrating a free function easy —\n    pointing `project.ts`'s serializer at a class instance instead of a generic `wrap()` result is\n    a one-line change, not a simultaneous rewrite into method form. But leaving it there forever\n    would contradict [§1](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/1. The actual reason, stated first, not derived from a benchmark]]>)'s own mental model (\"ties data structure directly with control\n    structure\") — folding migrated functions into methods is its own later rollout step, not a\n    permanent aside.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00Q",
    "text": "**Query: `a.b.c` as literal property access, not a separate query language** — implemented as a\n  `Proxy` per node (`web/src/lib/apeironNgn/node.ts`), live-verified against the entire real\n  `AperasKG/Apeiron/` mirror (`apeironNgnSmokeTest.ts`: every artifact's tree, node-for-node,\n  against the raw JSON-LD as ground truth). The earlier framing of this as needing\n  `valueOf`/`Symbol.toPrimitive`-style deferred-forcing machinery, isomorphic to a Gremlin\n  traversal builder, was written while it was still an open question whether Oxigraph's Node\n  binding would be sync or async — it's confirmed sync (`oxigraph` skill), so there's no I/O gap\n  for a Promise-like wrapper to hide, and no such wrapper exists in the implementation. The\n  laziness that actually matters survives anyway, at the right granularity for free: `.b` returns\n  a fresh Proxy naming its neighbor via the one triple needed to identify it — none of *that*\n  neighbor's own fields are read until something asks for them — so `a.b.c` costs exactly two\n  `store.match()` calls, not a subtree's worth. Getting this granularity right was still the part\n  that most needed care; it just didn't need the mechanism originally assumed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00R",
    "text": "**Versioning: git-native, already built, not new work.** Per the existing Phase 4 plan\n  (`Aperas-design.md`): `AperasKG/Apeiron/` already lives inside a real git repository, so a\n  JSON-LD write followed by an ordinary `git commit` *is* the version control — no bespoke\n  commit-layer to reimplement.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00S",
    "text": "**Concurrency: explicitly not supported, by design, not by omission.** Each Aperas instance owns\n  its own local store — no shared mutable database, no locking, no transaction isolation to build.\n  Cross-instance sync happens via `git merge` on the underlying JSON-LD, the same way code merges.\n  `reconcile.ts`'s Gestalt (Ratcliff/Obershelp) matcher — already built, already live-verified, used\n  today for re-ingestion after a local edit — is structurally the same problem as cross-instance\n  merge-conflict resolution on structured content, a reuse candidate rather than new mechanism to\n  invent.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG00T",
    "text": "**Search: still needs a secondary index, regardless of engine.** Regex/free-text search has no\n  index-servable shortcut in general, under TerminusDB or Oxigraph alike — this isn't a cost the\n  switch avoids, just one that stays constant either way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
