  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01S:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXSR002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01S:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXSR003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG01S",
    "text": "**`key`, derived, not stored.** `blockId`/`artifactId`/`folderId` are three separately-named\n  literal fields today, but each is always identical to its own id's local part by construction\n  (`writeBlockTree` sets `n.blockId = node.blockId` for the very id it just `wrap()`ped as\n  `` `BlockNode/${node.blockId}` ``, same pattern for `artifactId`/`folderId`) — the old\n  TerminusDB schema's own `@key: {Lexical, @fields: [blockId]}` directive already said as much:\n  the field's whole purpose was generating the id, not carrying independent information. `TreeNode`\n  replaces all three with one derived getter, `get key() { return this.id.slice(this.id.indexOf('/')",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01T"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG01T",
    "text": "1); }` — never its own triple, so nothing to keep in sync and nothing for `writeField` to\n  validate. Every current reader of `blockId`/`artifactId`/`folderId` switches to `.key`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01V:links:Link:00CBFDYYNR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG009",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01V:links:Link:00CBFDYYNR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYNR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "139"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG01V",
    "text": "**`children`: already dissolved into `parent`/`siblingIndex` — not a new change, confirmed.**\n  This was already true before this session ([§3](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/3. The concrete design]]>) \"Ordered containment: reified triples, not\n  `rdf:List`\") — `children` has never been a stored triple in ApeironNgn; `node.ts`'s `childrenOf`\n  already derives it by reverse-querying `PARENT_PRED`, sorted by `SIBLING_INDEX_PRED`. Restated\n  here only because it's the working precedent for `key` above: both are derived, on-the-fly\n  properties on top of the real stored facts (`parent`/`siblingIndex` triples; the id string\n  itself), not independently-stored data that could drift from them.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG01W",
    "text": "**`Link` becomes a subdocument, sibling to `Prop`/`StringProp`, not a top-level node.** Today\n  `Link` is addressable (`Link/<snowflake>`, its own `Link.jsonld` file, `BASE_NODE_FIELDS` spread\n  into `LINK_SHAPE` alongside `target`/`predicate`) purely because it inherited from `BaseEdge` ->\n  `BaseLink` -> `BaseNode` in the old TerminusDB schema. Once `Assertion`/`BaseEdge` are gone (next\n  bullet), nothing still needs `Link` to be independently addressable or to carry `links`/`props`/\n  `tombstonedAt`/`holder`/`unfolded` of its own — it becomes exactly `Prop`'s shape of thing: `{\n  target, predicate }` only, minted as `${parentId}/links/Link/<snowflake>` (mirroring `props`'\n  `${parentId}/props/StringProp/<snowflake>`), `BASE_NODE_FIELDS.links`'s `storageKind` flipped\n  from `reference` to `embed`. Checked live against the actual generic machinery, not assumed: both\n  `dehydrate.ts`'s `decodeField`/`serializeDoc` (branches on `spec.storageKind === 'embed'`, already\n  fully generic — not `props`-specific) and `store.ts`'s `encodeDoc` (recurses into any nested\n  object carrying its own `@id`, also not `props`-specific) already do exactly the right thing for\n  an embedded `Link` with zero new code — only `dehydrate.ts`'s `DEHYDRATE_CLASSES` and `store.ts`'s\n  `INSTANCE_FILES` need `'Link'` removed (no more standalone `Link.jsonld`). This also directly\n  removes the \"create then attach\" workaround `kgLinkNgn.ts`/`artifacts.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks`\n  both currently need (today's generic `mintEmbedded`-via-`writeField` auto-mint always shapes a\n  fresh id as `.../props/<type>/...`, wrong for a top-level `Link` — moot once `Link` really is\n  `storageKind: 'embed'`, since the existing generic embed-mint path becomes correct for it too).\n  **Decided: `Link` sheds `BASE_NODE_FIELDS` entirely** — its shape becomes exactly `{ target,\n  predicate }`, nothing else, matching `Prop`'s own minimal `{ key }`/`{ key, value }` shape. A\n  side effect worth stating plainly: since `StringProp` already didn't spread `BASE_NODE_FIELDS`\n  either, `BaseNode` (and everything it carries — `links`/`props`/`tombstonedAt`/`holder`/\n  `unfolded`) ends up used only by `TreeNode` and its descendants once this lands; nothing else in\n  the hierarchy extends it. Kept as its own level anyway (per the earlier instruction that\n  `BaseNode` stays parent of `TreeNode`) — it still documents a real conceptual boundary\n  (\"participates in the links/props/lifecycle system\") separate from `TreeNode`'s (\"has a\n  title and a position in the tree\"), even though only one branch uses it today.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG01X",
    "text": "**`Assertion`/`BaseEdge` are not migrated in any form — the capability is subsumed, not\n  ported.** Previously \"permanently out of scope\" meant \"no `kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/\n  `kg:unassert` port\"; this finalizes it one step further — there is no ApeironNgn class for\n  `Assertion`/`BaseEdge` at all, ever, because the thing they were for (recording a claim linking\n  two nodes, with justification) is already fully expressible as ordinary artifact content: a\n  `BlockNode` holding the claim in prose, with a wikilink resolving to a real `Link` at ingest time\n  (the same mechanism `kg:ingest`'s `resolveBlockLinks` already uses for every other in-text\n  wikilink) — e.g. \"Through the analysis of this investigation, we conclude that\n  `[X](../../reportX/sectionY)` is wrong.\" This is strictly more capable than the old model, not a\n  reduced substitute: the justification *is* the surrounding prose (not a separate, easy-to-neglect\n  field), and it projects to/from a real Markdown artifact for free (`kg:project`/`kg:ingest`\n  already round-trip any `BlockNode` with a wikilink) — `Assertion` never had that, which is the\n  concrete problem this resolves, not just a naming preference. Checked live: the real\n  `AperasKG/Apeiron/Assertion.jsonld` has zero documents today (the basic-assertion-authoring work\n  was only ever exercised by a synthetic edge in `verifyPhase0.ts`'s test harness, per\n  `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md` §0) — nothing to migrate or convert, so finalizing this\n  costs nothing. This also closes a real latent gap noticed while checking: `store.ts`'s\n  `INSTANCE_FILES` still reads `Assertion.jsonld` on every rehydrate (its fields land as raw quads,\n  since `encodeDoc` doesn't consult any class registry) while `dehydrate.ts`'s `DEHYDRATE_CLASSES`\n  never writes it back out — today, any real `Assertion` doc would silently vanish on the next\n  dehydrate. Removing `'Assertion'` from `INSTANCE_FILES` (alongside dropping it from\n  `ID_PREFIX_RE`/`KIND_RE` in `vocab.ts`) closes the gap by removing the read path entirely, rather\n  than by adding the write path back.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01Y:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXT8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01Y:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXT8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG01Y",
    "text": "**Classification — as actually implemented.** A function folds onto a class only when a single\nalready-identified node is a natural `this` for it; a function that searches across the whole\nstore, or sweeps every node of a kind, stays free (it has no one node to be `this` until *after*\nit runs). Each level gets only the methods whose governing field(s) — or whose need for a\ntitle/position in the tree — actually live at that level; a subclass doesn't restate a fold its\nparent already covers. One correction from the original proposal, caught while implementing:\n`collectBlockNodes` folds onto `TreeNode`, not `BlockNode` — the starting node passed to it can be\nany tree-positioned kind (an `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` root, not just a `BlockNode`), so it needs\n`treeChildren`'s polymorphism the same way `renderTree`/`toPath`/`findChild` do; putting it on\n`BlockNode` alone would have made it uncallable from the common `kg:title`/`kg:link` entry point.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01Z",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG028",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG02E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG02K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG02S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG02X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG02Y"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01Z:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXTG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01Z:props:StringProp:00CBFDYXTG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG01Z",
    "text": "**`BaseNode` (`links`/`props`/`tombstonedAt`/`holder`/`unfolded` — used only by `TreeNode` and\n  below now that `Link`/`StringProp` don't extend it):**",
