    "title": "00CBFDYXMG037",
    "text": "**Mirror format consequence, decided.** `dehydrateToJsonLd` no longer writes `blockId`/\n`artifactId`/`folderId` into `AperasKG/Apeiron/*.jsonld` at all now (derived-not-stored, per\n`key`) — confirmed via a structural diff against the real mirror before this was decided. That\nfield is also what the *old* TerminusDB schema's `@key: {Lexical, @fields: [blockId]}` uses to\nassign a document's own key on import, so a mirror dehydrated by the new code is no longer\nre-importable via the old `kg:import`. Decided to accept this rather than have `dehydrate.ts`\nspecial-case emitting the field output-only: consistent with `kg:export`/`kg:import` already being\nslated for retirement ([§4](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence]]>) point 2's \"not really migrated so much as retired\"), not something this\nmigration needs to keep working through.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG038",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG038",
    "text": "**Sequencing, as it actually happened** (same one-at-a-time / diff-before-next discipline as\nstep 2): the hierarchy refactor first (verified via a scratch-copy rehydrate → mutate → dehydrate\n→ re-rehydrate round trip, `danglingRefs: []`, zero semantic diff besides harmless `Set`-order\nnoise in `props`/`links` — the same kind already accepted for the original dehydrate work); then\n`BaseNode` (`fold`/`unfold`/`addLink`, verified live: a fresh embedded `Link` mints and decodes\ncorrectly); then `TreeNode` and all three `treeChildren`/`appendChild` overrides together (verified\nagainst the real corpus: `renderTree`/`kg:tree`'s full 1048-line output and `kg:path`/`kg:resolve`\nincluding a real 3-hop deep path all diffed byte-identical against TerminusDB; `findChild` +\n`appendChild` verified end-to-end via a real `--create-holder` run against a scratch copy — correct\nmulti-hop descent, correct holder attachment, immediately re-resolvable, and confirmed the\n`ArtifactNode`-already-has-a-root guard rejects *before* minting, leaving no orphan node behind);\nthen `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`'s own methods (verified via a real `kg:track`+\n`kg:ingest` sweep against a scratch copy of the actual corpus — genuine, non-manufactured drift on\n4 real artifacts, correct reconciliation stats, zero dangling references on round-trip); then the\n`resolve.ts`/`resolveCreate.ts` family's `descend` switched to call `.findChild()`/`.appendChild()`\ninternally, verified as part of the same `--create-holder` run above.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG01M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG039",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Step 4: real-corpus validation, then archive the TerminusDB-backed half — implemented, verified",
    "text": "**First real-corpus run of `kg:track:ngn`/`kg:ingest:ngn`, and non-dry-run `kg:project:ngn`.**\nEverything above through step 3 had only ever been verified against scratch copies. Ran the real\npipeline for the first time against the actual `AperasKG/artifacts/` corpus: reverted the working\ntree to the last committed mirror as ground truth, ran `kg:track:ngn`+`kg:ingest:ngn` for real, and\nconfirmed every one of the 13 artifacts with no genuine content change came back byte-identical\n(same block ids, same content — the critical idempotency property, and a stronger result than the\nTerminusDB-backed full-sweep `kg:ingest` gets on the same corpus, see `Aperas-dev-status.md`'s\nappendix). The 5 artifacts that did change all reconciled sanely; one (`Aperas-core-ontology-\ndesign.md`) reconciled 6 added/6 removed blocks despite an unchanged file hash, traced to the\nastParser/mapping rules having evolved since it was last really ingested, not a defect. Non-dry-run\n`kg:project:ngn` was then implemented (mirroring `kgCli.ts`'s `project` write branch: writes to the\nartifact's own file, or the folder's `README.md`) and used for real to project that reconciled tree\nback to `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` — confirmed the only differences from the original file\nwere canonical formatting normalization (blank-line list spacing, `*`→`-` bullets), zero content\nlost, closing out the unexplained reconciliation delta.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00V",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG03A",
    "text": "**Found in the process, outside the rollout plan's own scope: `AperasKG/.githooks/post-commit`/\n`post-index-change` were still invoking the TerminusDB-backed `npm run kg:track`, not\n`kg:track:ngn`.** Not a deliberate deferral like `kg:export`/`kg:import`'s retirement — a plain\noversight, since the hooks live outside `web/src/lib/kgCli.ts` entirely and call `kg:track` as a\nhardcoded string, so migrating the underlying script never touched them. Fixed: both hooks (and\n`.githooks/README.md`) now call `kg:track:ngn`; re-ran `post-index-change` directly afterward and\nconfirmed it completes with no TerminusDB connection at all. Separately moved `post-commit` to\n`pre-commit` (staging the mirror sync's own output via `git add`) so it lands in the same commit\nas the artifact change it reflects, instead of trailing it as a separate leftover working-tree\nchange right after the commit completes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG039",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG03B",
    "text": "**`verifyPhase0.ts` replaced by `verifyApeironNgn.ts`.** `client.ts`/`crud.ts`/`woql.ts`/\n`graphql.ts`/`versionControl.ts` (and `export.ts`'s `kg:export`/`kg:import`) are all abandoned\nalong with TerminusDB itself, per the same decision as their retirement above — so the project's\nmain correctness harness needed an ApeironNgn-only replacement, not a `--db`-gated branch onto a\ndead dependency. New `web/src/lib/verifyApeironNgn.ts` (`npm run verify:ngn`) ports everything\n`verifyPhase0.ts` covered except Assertion/WOQL (the model doesn't have `Assertion` anymore) and\ntemporal commit management (ordinary `git branch`/`commit`/`diff` on `AperasKG/Apeiron/` already\ncovers that, nothing ApeironNgn-specific needed). Runs entirely against an in-memory `Store`\nrehydrated from the real mirror — never dehydrates back to it, so the real `AperasKG/Apeiron/`\nfiles are untouched by every run (confirmed live: ran it twice back-to-back, zero git diff both\ntimes); the one dehydrate/rehydrate check uses its own scratch directory. The demo artifact/folder\nstill have to live under a real (if disposable) `__verify_apeironngn_demo/` subfolder of\n`AperasKG/artifacts/` — `getArtifactsDir()`/`ingestFolderTree` have no directory-override param,\nunlike `rehydrateStore`/`dehydrateToJsonLd` — cleaned up in a `finally` regardless of pass/fail.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG039",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG03C",
    "text": "**Real bug found on its very first run, not by inspection this time: `mintEmbedded` (`node.ts`)\nstored a freshly-minted `Link.target` as a literal, not a node reference.** Its per-field loop\nchecked `typeof v === 'string'` before consulting the field's own declared `storageKind`, so\n`BaseNode.addLink(predicate, target)` — whose normal calling convention passes `target` as a bare\nid *string* — always fell into the literal branch even though `LINK_SHAPE` declares `target` as\n`storageKind: 'reference'`. Every freshly-minted link's target decoded back as a plain string on\nread, not a wrapped node, so `.target.id` (and any reverse-traversal through it) silently read\n`undefined`. Reproduced live by `verifyApeironNgn.ts`'s own link-extraction check (§5b) on its\nfirst run against a real self-link. Fixed by checking `shape[k]?.storageKind === 'reference'`\nfirst, regardless of whether `v` is a string or an object (`idOf` already handles both) — falling\nthrough to the literal branch only when the field isn't a reference. Re-verified: the same check\npasses after the fix.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG039",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03D:links:Link:00CBFDYYRR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00V",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03D:links:Link:00CBFDYYRR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYRR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "569"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG03D",
    "text": "**TDB-backed code renamed and archived; ApeironNgn scripts promoted to the plain command names.**\nWith every `kg:*` command's real-corpus write path now verified (above) and `verify.ts` covering\nwhat `verifyPhase0.ts` used to, the TDB-backed half no longer needed first-class command names —\nrenamed and archived rather than deleted outright, kept for reference for some time before final\nremoval (some mechanism may only exist in code, not written up in a doc). Two more files turned\nout to be mixed TDB/engine-agnostic, the same way `artifacts.ts`/`project.ts` were ([§4](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence]]>) rollout\nstep 2): `folders.ts` (`getFolderRecord`/`ingestFolderTree` split out to `foldersTdb.ts`) and\n`nodeRef.ts` (the whole `client`-based deep-path resolver split out to `nodeRefTdb.ts`, leaving\nonly `slugify`/`tokenize`/`pathToNameTokens`/`Token` — what `apeironNgn/node.ts`/`resolveCreate.ts`/\n`resolve.ts` actually import — behind). `bench-tree-fetch-strategies.ts` (a TDB-vs-ApeironNgn\nfetch-strategy comparison, not wired into any npm script) archived too, once its TDB half had\nnothing live left to compare against.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG039",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG03E",
    "text": "Renaming scheme: every TDB-backed file gets a `Tdb` suffix (`kgCli.ts` → `kgCliTdb.ts`,\n`client.ts` → `clientTdb.ts`, etc.); every ApeironNgn `kgXxxNgn.ts` drops the `Ngn` suffix\n(`kgTreeNgn.ts` → `kgTree.ts`, ..., `verifyApeironNgn.ts` → `verify.ts`) and its npm command\nloses `:ngn` (`kg:tree:ngn` → `kg:tree`) to take over the plain name. Every TDB npm command got a\nmatching `:tdb` suffix for the final regression pass (`kg:track:tdb`, `verify:tdb`, ...) — both\n`verify` and `verify:tdb` were run for real after the rename (the latter against live\nTerminusDB, full pass including Assertion/WOQL and branch/commit management) before archiving,\nthen the `:tdb` npm script entries were deleted entirely (not left pointing at `.archive/`) once\nthe files actually moved, matching the earlier decision to drop rather than keep them reachable\nvia `npm run`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG039",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG03F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMG03F",
    "text": "Final location: `.archive/scripts/` (`restore.sh`/`tdb-log.sh`/`tdb-doc.sh`/`README.md` — no code\ndependencies, unaffected either way) and `web/.archive/src/lib/` for the renamed `*Tdb.ts` files\nplus `bench-tree-fetch-strategies.ts`. `web/.archive/`, not a repo-root `.archive/web/`,\ndeliberately: the first attempt (mirroring the full `web/src/lib/...` path under a repo-root\n`.archive/`) left the archive syntactically self-contained (`./artifacts`, `./reconcile`, etc. all\ncopied in alongside, not reached-back-into) but *unrunnable* — Node resolves an external package\n(`terminusdb`) by walking up from the importing file looking for `node_modules`, and a repo-root\n`.archive/web/src/lib/` never passes through the real `web/node_modules` on that walk. Nesting one\nlevel shallower inside `web/` itself (`web/.archive/src/lib/`) fixes that — confirmed live,\n`npx tsx web/.archive/src/lib/kgCliTdb.ts tree ...` actually runs — while staying outside\n`tsconfig.app.json`'s `include: [\"src\"]`, so it's still never swept into routine type-checking.\nEvery shared dependency the archived files need (`artifacts.ts`, `project.ts`, `folders.ts`,\n`nodeRef.ts`, `reconcile.ts`, `props.ts`, `astParser.ts`, `snowflake.ts`, `lineReader.ts`) is\ncopied in alongside them, not reached-back-into — a deliberate frozen snapshot, since those live\nfiles are free to keep evolving and nothing should silently break the archive later. The one\nexception: `bench-tree-fetch-strategies.ts`'s `apeironNgn/store.ts`/`apeironNgn/node.ts` imports\nreach back into the real `web/src/lib/apeironNgn/` rather than being copied — unlike the TDB\nmodules, that code isn't abandoned, it's the live implementation, so freezing a copy of it would\njust go stale instead of staying accurate.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG039",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR000",
    "text": "Not done as part of this pass, flagged as a follow-up rather than silently left inconsistent:\n`Aperas-architecture.md` still describes the now-archived TDB implementation as the current\nsystem spec (directory layout, module table, `kg:track`/`kg:ingest`/`kg:export` description, etc.)\n— unlike this doc's own rollout narrative (correctly historical, left as-is), that one presents\nitself as current-state architecture and needs an actual rewrite to describe ApeironNgn, not just\na note. Deferred by explicit choice, not an oversight.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG039",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Step 5: shared service process — implemented, verified",
    "text": "Every `kg:xxx` script today starts a fresh process on each invocation: `rehydrateStore()` (a full\nparse of `BlockNode.jsonld`/`ArtifactNode.jsonld`/`FolderNode.jsonld` into a brand-new `Store`),\nfollowed, for mutating ops, by an unconditional full-rewrite `dehydrateToJsonLd(store)` before\nexit. Cost scales with the whole mirror's size on every single call, including the\n`pre-commit`/`post-index-change` git hooks, which fire on every commit, branch switch, and reset.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00V",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR007",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR002",
    "text": "This step replaces that with a single long-running service process\n(`web/src/lib/apeironNgn/service.ts`) holding one `Store` in memory across invocations. Every\n`kg:xxx` script becomes a thin client: parse argv, `ensureServiceRunning()` (auto-start if nothing\nis listening), send one request, print the result, exit. Only `service.ts` calls\n`rehydrateStore()`/`dehydrateToJsonLd()` — the CLI scripts no longer do. No fallback \"standalone,\nno service\" mode is planned: auto-start already makes the service transparent on a cold\ninvocation, so a fallback would just duplicate the old rehydrate/dehydrate logic for no benefit.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR003",
    "text": "**Transport and locking.** The service listens on a Unix domain socket (`node:net`, no new\ndependency) at `web/.run/apeironngn.sock`. \"No concurrency\" is enforced two ways: a file lock\n(`web/.run/apeironngn.lock`, holding `{pid, socketPath, startedAt, status}`) guarantees only one\nservice process ever owns the mirror directory, and an in-process promise-chain queue inside that\none service guarantees its own concurrent socket connections are handled strictly one at a time,\nregardless of transport concurrency:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR004",
    "text": "```ts\nlet queue: Promise<unknown> = Promise.resolve();\nfunction enqueue<T>(fn: () => T | Promise<T>): Promise<T> {\n  const result = queue.then(fn, fn);\n  queue = result.then(() => undefined, () => undefined);\n  return result;\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR005",
    "text": "**Auto-start and the lock race.** A client checks liveness by connecting to the socket and sending\n`{op:'ping'}` (more reliable than trusting a PID alone, since PIDs get reused); a failed connect\nplus an existing lock file means either a live-but-slow-to-answer service or a stale lock left by a\ncrashed one — `process.kill(pid, 0)` (throws `ESRCH` if dead) combined with a `status:'starting'`\ntimestamp older than ~10s disambiguates, unlinking both lock and socket files if stale. The\nwould-be starter claims the lock via `fs.openSync(lockPath, 'wx')` — atomic exclusive create;\n`EEXIST` means someone else is already starting it, so this client polls-connect with backoff\ninstead of spawning a second copy. The winner spawns `service.ts` directly via the local `tsx`\nbinary (`node_modules/.bin/tsx`, `tsx` a devDependency — see \"Toolchain: dropping `npx`\" below),\ndetached and unref'd, and the spawned process itself overwrites the lock with its own pid and\n`status:'ready'` once `listen()` succeeds — the lock always reflects the actual owning process, not\nthe process that happened to start it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR006",
    "text": "**Dirty-tracking and flush.** A `dirty` flag is set by any mutating op handler after it runs; a\n`setInterval(..., 10_000)` flushes via the unchanged `dehydrateToJsonLd(store)` if dirty, then\nclears the flag. `flush` is a field on the request payload rather than a separate message\n(`{op:'track', paths, flush: true}`); when true, the flush happens synchronously before the\nresponse is sent, so the calling CLI process — and the `pre-commit` hook's `git add` right after it\n— only proceeds once the mirror is actually on disk. `--flush` is parsed out of argv the same way\neach script already parses its other flags (`kgTree.ts`'s `--no-holders`/`--unfolded`, etc.). One\nbehavior change this implies: a plain `kg:track` invocation without `--flush` now marks the store\ndirty and returns, with the on-disk mirror catching up within 10s rather than synchronously — the\nintended effect of centralizing dehydrate, not a bug.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR007",
    "text": "**Invoking any flag through `npm run`.** Every `--flag` across the whole `kg:xxx` suite\n(`--flush`/`--reload`/`--discard`/`--clobber`/`--recursive`/`--view`/etc.) needs npm's own `--`\nseparator to actually reach the script when run as `npm run kg:xxx`: `npm run kg:xxx --flush`\n(no `--`) has npm's own CLI parser consume `--flush` itself — silently, with no forwarded flag and\noften no visible warning at all (a *recognized* npm option like `--force` at least prints\n\"npm warn using --force\"; an unrecognized one like `--clobber` doesn't print anything, and just\nvanishes) — while a plain, unprefixed positional word (a path, a ref) passes through untouched\neither way. The correct invocation is always `npm run kg:xxx -- --flush` (`--` before the flag), or\nskip `npm run` entirely and call the script directly:\n`node_modules/.bin/tsx src/lib/kgTrack.ts --flush`. This isn't specific to any one flag or op — it's\na property of `npm run` itself, so no flag name choice avoids it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR008",
    "text": "**Toolchain: dropping `npx`.** Every `kg:xxx` script and the service's own auto-start both ran\n`npx tsx <entry>` originally. `tsx` isn't a local devDependency, so each call pays `npx`'s own\npackage-resolution overhead on top of actually running `tsx` — measured at ~1s of the ~1.45s a\nbare `npm run kg:tree` took, even with `tsx` already cached and fully offline (`npx --offline`\nstill took ~1s; the cached `tsx` binary called directly took ~0.3s). Fixed by adding `tsx` as a\ndevDependency and calling it directly — `package.json`'s `kg:xxx` scripts read `tsx <entry>` (no\n`npx`; `npm run` resolves the local `node_modules/.bin` automatically) and `serviceClient.ts`'s\n`spawnService` calls `node_modules/.bin/tsx` explicitly (used for auto-start, and — since a code\nchange requires restarting the service by hand to take effect, see the workflow note in \"Manual\nkill target\" below — for every manual restart during development too). Brought `npm run kg:tree`\ndown to ~0.6s, unrelated to and stacking with the rehydrate-avoidance win this whole step is about.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR009",
    "text": "**Wire protocol.** Newline-delimited JSON, one request per connection (client connects, writes one\nline, reads one line, closes) — `JSON.stringify` always escapes an embedded `\\n`, so this framing\nneeds no parser beyond a string split.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00A",
    "text": "```ts\ntype ServiceRequest =\n  | { op: 'ping' }\n  | { op: 'reload'; discard: boolean }                             // reload the store from disk\n  | { op: 'flush'; clobber: boolean }                               // force an immediate dehydrate\n  | { op: 'track'; paths: string[]; flush: boolean; reload: boolean }\n  | { op: 'ingest'; paths: string[]; flush: boolean; reload: boolean; track: boolean }\n  | { op: 'unfold'; ref: string; viewRef?: string; flush: boolean; reload: boolean }\n  | { op: 'fold'; ref: string; viewRef?: string; flush: boolean; reload: boolean }\n  | { op: 'resolve'; paths: string[]; base?: string; createHolder: boolean; titles?: string[]; flush: boolean; reload: boolean }\n  | { op: 'titleCandidates'; pathArg: string; recursive: boolean; reload: boolean } // read-only, lists kg:title's prompt targets\n  | { op: 'setBlockTitle'; blockId: string; title: string; flush: boolean }\n  | { op: 'linkCandidates'; pathArg: string; recursive: boolean; all: boolean; reload: boolean } // read-only, lists kg:link's prompt targets\n  | { op: 'addBlockLink'; blockId: string; targetRef: string; flush: boolean }  // targetRef resolved server-side\n  | { op: 'project'; path: string; reload: boolean }               // read-only; result carries rendered markdown\n  | { op: 'tree'; pathArg: string; maxDepth?: number; noHolders: boolean; viewRef?: string; reload: boolean }\n  | { op: 'path'; idArg: string; reload: boolean };\n\ntype ServiceResponse = { ok: true; result: unknown } | { ok: false; error: string };\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00B",
    "text": "**Per-script refactor.** Each `kg:xxx.ts` currently calls `main()` unconditionally at module\nbottom; the refactor adds the same self-invocation guard `verify.ts` already uses\n(`if (process.argv[1]?.endsWith('kgTrack.ts')) main();`) so `service.ts` can import each script's\noperation function without triggering its CLI side effects. `kgTrack.ts`/`kgIngest.ts`/\n`kgUnfold.ts`/`kgFold.ts` are one-shot batch mutators (rehydrate → one operation → dehydrate once)\nand split cleanly into an exported `run*(store, ...args)` plus a thin client `main()`.\n`kgResolve.ts` only dehydrates inside its `--create-holder` branch — plain lookup mode is\nread-only. `kgTitle.ts` and `kgLink.ts` are interactive (readline against stdin), dehydrating after\nevery accepted answer rather than once at the end, so they need granular per-answer ops instead of\none request per invocation: a read-only `titleCandidates`/`linkCandidates` op lists what to prompt\nfor in one round trip, then each accepted answer is its own `setBlockTitle`/`addBlockLink` round\ntrip. `kg:link`'s target resolution (`resolveDeepPath` on the raw answer text) moves server-side\ntoo — `addBlockLink` takes the unresolved `targetRef` and reports back whether it resolved, so an\ninvalid answer can be re-prompted without the readline loop needing its own store access.\n`kgProject.ts`/\n`kgTree.ts`/`kgPath.ts` never call `dehydrateToJsonLd` at all; they route through the service\npurely for the rehydrate-avoidance win. `kgProject.ts`'s rendered `.md` write stays client-side\n(the service returns the markdown string; the CLI wrapper writes the file) so the service's own\ndisk-write footprint stays limited to exactly the 3 mirror files.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00C",
    "text": "**Idle shutdown.** The service exits after 30 minutes with no requests handled (any `op`, including\n`ping`, resets the idle timer — in practice a `ping` is always immediately followed by real work,\nso it's a fine proxy for \"someone's using it\"). On fire it goes through the same graceful-shutdown\npath as `SIGTERM`/`SIGINT`: flush if dirty, unlink lock and socket, exit. The next `kg:xxx`\ninvocation (or git hook) auto-starts a fresh service exactly like any other cold start.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00D",
    "text": "**Disk locations.** `web/.run/apeironngn.sock` and `web/.run/apeironngn.lock`, resolved relative to\n`__dirname` the same way `getApeironExportDir()` already is — not `cwd`. Added to `web/.gitignore`\n(`.run/`). Deliberately not under `AperasKG/` — that directory's contents are otherwise all\ngit-tracked mirror data.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00E",
    "text": "**Git hook.** `AperasKG/.githooks/pre-commit`'s `npm run kg:track -- $relative_paths` gains\n`--flush`, so the mirror is guaranteed on disk before the hook's `git add`. `post-index-change`\ndoesn't `git add` anything after `kg:track` (it only exists to keep the mirror's hash-comparison\ncache warm), so it stays flush-less, relying on the 10s timer.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00F",
    "text": "**Failure handling.** A hard crash (SIGKILL/OOM) loses up to 10s of unflushed mutations —\nacceptable, since the one path that must not lose data (commits) always goes through `--flush`,\nwhich blocks until the write is confirmed on disk. A clean stop (`SIGTERM`/`SIGINT`) stops\naccepting new connections, finishes the in-flight request, flushes if dirty, and unlinks the lock\nand socket files before exiting, so a deliberate stop never leaves a stale lock or drops recent\nchanges.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00G",
    "text": "**Manual kill target.** Calling the local `tsx` binary directly (no `npx`) keeps this a two-PID\nchain, not the four-PID one an `npx tsx` spawn would produce: `node_modules/.bin/tsx` (a small\nloader process) spawns the actual runtime — `node --require .../preflight.cjs --import\n.../loader.mjs service.ts` — as its one child, confirmed via `ps -o pid,ppid,pgid,sid`. The\n`SIGTERM`/`SIGINT` handler above lives only in that inner `node` process (the one actually running\n`service.ts`'s code); the `tsx` wrapper PID above it installs no handler of its own. Linux does not\ncascade a signal from parent to child automatically — `kill -TERM <pid>` (a bare, positive PID)\nreaches exactly that one process, nothing downstream of it — so killing the wrapper PID this way\nleaves the real service running, orphaned, still holding the lock/socket, while only the wrapper\nexits.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00H",
    "text": "There are two correct ways to stop it. Either signal the inner `node` PID directly (cross-check\n`web/.run/apeironngn.lock`'s own `pid` field if unsure which PID that is — the service always keeps\nthat field pointed at itself; see \"Auto-start and the lock race\" above) — or signal the whole\nprocess group at once with a *negative* PID, `kill -TERM -<pgid>`: `detached: true` at spawn time\nmade the `tsx` wrapper the leader of a fresh process group, and the `node` process spawned\ndownstream of it inherited that same group without re-detaching, so both PIDs share one PGID\n(numerically equal to the wrapper PID) and `kill -TERM -<pgid>` delivers the signal to both\nsimultaneously — the leaf process runs its normal graceful shutdown, the wrapper just dies on the\nsame signal (no handler, default terminate, harmless). The minus sign is what selects the group\ninstead of a single PID; leaving it off is the wrapper-PID mistake above. Find both PIDs (and\nconfirm they share a PGID) with `ps -o pid,pgid,cmd -C node | grep apeironNgn/service`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00J",
    "text": "**Restart-on-code-change, a standing rule.** The service holds `apeironNgn/*.ts` in memory from the\nmoment it starts — editing any of those files has no effect on an already-running service, same as\nany other long-lived process. There's no file-watcher/hot-reload; a code change only takes effect\nonce the service is killed (one of the two correct ways above) and a `kg:xxx` invocation auto-starts\na fresh one. Forgetting this reads as a baffling \"the fix isn't working\" until the stale process is\nthe thing actually still running.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00K",
    "text": "**Verified live**, against the real `AperasKG/Apeiron/` mirror (not a scratch copy — every op here\nis idempotent on unchanged content, so a no-op re-track/re-flush was safe to run for real):\ncold start from a removed `.run/` (service auto-spawns, lock ends at `status:'ready'` with the\nservice's own pid, correct output); warm reuse immediately after (no second rehydrate — the same\npid answers, an order of magnitude faster than the cold call); two and three simultaneous cold\ninvocations racing on an empty `.run/` (exactly one service ends up running each time, every\ninvocation still gets correct output); two simultaneous mutating `--flush` calls (both land, all 6\nmirror files stay valid JSON, zero diff since nothing actually changed); the 10s auto-flush\n(mutate without `--flush`, mirror mtime provably unchanged immediately after, flips ~10-15s later,\nzero content diff); `--flush` writing synchronously before the CLI process exits; a hard `kill -9`\nfollowed by a fresh invocation (dead lock detected, clean restart, correct output — the crash\nscenario the \"starting\" grace window and `status:'ready'` liveness check exist for); a graceful\n`SIGTERM` after a real (reversible) mutation with no flush yet, confirming it flushes-then-exits\nwhen dirty and skips the write when not (used deliberately to discard a test mutation without\ntouching disk, then re-verified the next cold rehydrate reflects the correct on-disk state).\n`kgTitle.ts`/`kgLink.ts`'s interactive round trips and the git hook's actual `git commit` path\nwere checked by direct invocation and code inspection rather than a live end-to-end run — the\nunderlying mechanics (`--flush` argv parsing, `kg:track -- <paths> --flush`) are already covered\nby the tests above.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Step 6: ingest ordering, `--track`, link-resolution reporting, and root-document addressing — implemented, verified",
    "text": "`kg:ingest --track` folds `kg:track`'s own refresh into the same call: `kg:ingest`'s change\ndetection is `ingestedHash === fileHash` (has the *tracked* hash moved since last ingestion), a\ndifferent question from whether the file on disk has actually changed — without a fresh track\nfirst, an on-disk edit to an already-tracked artifact is invisible to a plain `kg:ingest`.\n`kg:ingest <path> --track` is `kg:track <path> && kg:ingest <path>` in one call (`kgIngest.ts`'s\n`runIngest` runs `runTrack` first when the flag is set).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00V",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00Y",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00Z"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00N",
    "text": "**Link-resolution outcome is its own report line, separate from reconciliation.**\nReconciliation's `changed` count reflects a block's own authored content (text/structure) only — a\nwikilink's *resolution outcome* (dangling → resolved, or the reverse) can flip purely because\nsomething *else* in the graph changed between ingestions, which reconciliation can't see (it runs\nbefore `resolveBlockLinks` does, on the tree as freshly parsed, without knowing yet what any link\nwill resolve to). `artifacts.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks` now returns its own `LinkResolutionStats`\n(`{ resolved, dangling, changed }`), diffing each block's freshly-resolved link targets against its\n*previous* resolved targets (`ArtifactNode.ingestFromDisk` collects those before reconciliation\noverwrites anything, threading them through as `oldLinkTargets`). `kgIngest.ts` prints it as its own\nline under the reconciliation summary: `Links: N resolved, M dangling, K changed.`",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00P",
    "text": "**Ingest ordering: track and rebuild the folder tree before parsing any content or resolving any\nlink.** `kgIngest.ts`'s `runIngest` used to rebuild the `FolderNode` tree (`ingestFolderTree`) only\n*after* every target artifact had already been tracked, parsed into its fractal `BlockNode` tree,\nand had its wikilinks resolved. `ingestFolderTree` only ever needs artifacts to be *tracked* (have a\n`path`) — never their parsed content — so nothing actually depended on that ordering. The cost of\ngetting it backwards: `resolveBlockLinks` resolves a wikilink relative to its own block's ancestry,\nwhich for a brand-new artifact climbs back up into the artifact's own *not-yet-attached* place in\nthe folder tree — a tree-based lookup at that point can't find a real, already-tracked path it\nhasn't reached yet, and (worse) `--create-holder`'s fallback then mints a duplicate holder chain for\nsomething that already exists. Fixed by reordering: `runIngest` now tracks every explicit path (and\nruns `ingestFolderTree`) *before* calling `ingestArtifacts`/`ingestAllArtifacts` — the tree is\nalways consistent by the time any content gets parsed or any link resolved, for every artifact in\nthe batch, including ones newly tracked in the very same call. `ingestArtifacts` itself no longer\ntracks inline (that guarantee is now the caller's job, made explicit); any direct caller of\n`trackArtifact`/`ingestArtifact` outside `runIngest` (`verify.ts`'s own demo harness included) needs\nto rebuild the folder tree between the two for the same reason.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00Q",
    "text": "**Folder/file path segments match the same way headings always did.** The deep-path grammar's\nfolder/file tier used to be its own whole-string search (`resolveArtifactOrFolderPrefix`, since\nremoved): join the leading NAME tokens, look the joined string up as one literal `path` value. That\ngave folder/file segments only exact matching, never the prefix tolerance headings already had via\n`TreeNode.findChild`. With the ingest-ordering fix above guaranteeing the tree is always consistent\nby the time `resolve.ts`/`resolveCreate.ts` run, `findChild`'s exact-then-prefix slug matching was\ngeneralized (it matched `BlockNode` children only before) to match a `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode`\nchild the same way — so `t` now prefix-matches a real `test.md`/`test/` the same way it already\nprefix-matched a `# Test` heading, and multi-segment paths resolve hop-by-hop through `treeChildren`\nuniformly at every tier, not just within one document.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00R:props:StringProp:00CBFDYY00000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00R:props:StringProp:00CBFDYY08000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00R",
    "text": "**An artifact and its root document are the same logical node for addressing.** Every artifact's\ncontent lives under a synthetic root `BlockNode` (`type: 'root'`, title `\"Document Root\"`) —\nreal, still visible in `kg:tree`'s rendering and still `ArtifactNode.root` in storage, but never\nmeant to be a real addressing boundary of its own: an artifact only ever has the one document, so\n\"the artifact\" and \"its root document\" name the same thing. The deep-path grammar used to treat the\nroot block as an ordinary intermediate segment regardless — a heading `H` directly under an\nartifact's root resolved as `<artifact>/document-root/<heading>`, and `..` from `H` landed on the\nsynthetic root block itself, not the artifact. Both corrected to treat the root block as invisible:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00W"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00S",
    "text": "`ArtifactNode.findChild(text)` (`node.ts`) delegates straight to `this.root.findChild(text)`\n    instead of matching `text` against `[root]`'s own title — so `<artifact>/<heading>` resolves in\n    one hop, `<artifact>/document-root/<heading>` no longer resolves at all (the segment doesn't\n    exist to match against).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00T",
    "text": "`ArtifactNode.appendChild(childId)` delegates to `this.root.appendChild(childId)` (resetting\n    `childId`'s own `.parent` to `this.root`, overriding whatever the caller set) when a root\n    already exists, rather than throwing — attaching a new top-level heading directly under an\n    artifact is the *normal* case now (creating one via `--create-holder` no longer needs an\n    explicit `document-root` hop first), not an error; only becoming the very first root (no\n    existing one to delegate to) still sets `this.root` directly, unchanged from before.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00V",
    "text": "`TreeNode.toPath()` skips emitting a path segment for a block whose `type === 'root'`, walking\n    straight through to its `.parent` (already stamped to the owning `ArtifactNode` at ingestion\n    time) — so a heading's own computed path is `<artifact>/<heading>`, never\n    `<artifact>/document-root/<heading>`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00W",
    "text": "`resolve.ts`'s and `resolveCreate.ts`'s `descend`, on `..` from a `BlockNode`: after following\n    `.parent` once, if that landed on a `type === 'root'` block, follows `.parent` *again*\n    (already stamped to the `ArtifactNode`) before continuing — so `..` from a top-level heading\n    reaches the artifact directly, matching `toPath()`'s own treatment of the same hop.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00X:links:Link:00CBFDYYS8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR010",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00X:links:Link:00CBFDYYS8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYS8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "273"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00X",
    "text": "None of this touches storage or `kg:tree`'s rendering — the root block still exists, still holds\n`children`, still prints as its own `[root] Document Root` line. Only the deep-path grammar (and\n`--create-holder`'s attachment logic) treats it as transparent. (Superseded by [Step 7](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence/Step 7: `ArtifactNode extends BlockNode`, the root block retired outright, `text` a copied abstract for both — implemented, verified]]>) below: the\nroot block was later removed from storage entirely, not just made addressing-transparent.)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00Y",
    "text": "**A separate, pre-existing nuance this interacts with: a heading's *first* paragraph is a different\naddressing level than its later ones.** `Aperas-markdown-fractal-mapping-design.md` §2's consuming\nrule — a heading (or `listItem`) absorbs its immediately-following paragraph as its own `text`,\nrather than making it a child `BlockNode` — means a wikilink written in that *first* paragraph is,\nfor addressing purposes, a link *from the heading itself*: the containing block's own path is\n`<artifact>/<heading>`, so a sibling sub-heading is reachable directly, `subheading`, no `..`\nneeded. A *second* (or later) paragraph under the same heading is never absorbed — it's a real,\nseparate `BlockNode` one level *below* the heading (a sibling of any sub-headings, not the same node\nas the heading), defaulting to its own generated id as its title (nothing else names it, until\n`kg:title` overrides it). A wikilink written there needs one extra `..` to reach the same\nsub-heading: `../subheading`. The two paragraphs *read* identically in the rendered document —\nnothing about the surface markdown marks which addressing level a given paragraph's links resolve\nfrom — so a link's correct `..` count depends on whether it's the heading's first paragraph or not,\nnot just on how many headings visually separate it from its target. Unrelated to the root-document\nfix above (a `BlockNode`-to-`BlockNode` structural fact, not an artifact/root one), but easy to\nconflate with it since both change how many `..` a link needs relative to what the prose visually\nsuggests.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR00Z",
    "text": "**Verified live**, against the real corpus mirror (`test/test.md`, already containing a nested\nheading): `test/test.md/test` resolves the heading in one hop where `test/test.md/document-root/\ntest` now correctly misses; `kg:path` on that heading reports `test/test.md/--test` (no\n`document-root` segment); `--base <heading-id> ..` resolves to the artifact directly; `--create-\nholder` on a brand-new top-level heading under that already-ingested artifact attaches it as a real\nsibling of the existing content (confirmed via `kg:tree`), not a rejected \"already has a root\"\nerror; a temporary second paragraph added under `test/test.md`'s `# Test` heading confirmed the\nfirst-paragraph-vs-later-paragraph addressing split directly (`sub` resolved from the first\nparagraph's own base, missed from the second paragraph's base, `../sub` resolved from there) before\nbeing reverted. `npm run verify`'s own demo harness (`verify.ts`) needed the same\ntracking-then-folder-rebuild reordering applied to its own direct `trackArtifact`/`ingestArtifact`\ncalls (it doesn't go through `runIngest`), and its \"truly dangling\" wikilink fixture needed its\nhardcoded `..` count increased by one level — the same literal `../../../../` that used to correctly\novershoot into unresolvable territory under the old (one-level-deeper) tree shape now lands validly\non the artifact's own document, since a hop that used to be consumed by the synthetic root segment\nno longer exists to consume it. `test/test.md`'s own real wikilink had the identical, pre-existing\nstaleness (written against the old, one-level-deeper shape) and needed the same one-level `..`\ncorrection, found by exactly this recalibration check — a concrete reminder that any wikilink\nwritten before this fix landed may need re-auditing, not just the two test fixtures caught here.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR010:links:Link:00CBFDYYSR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR00M",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR010:links:Link:00CBFDYYSR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYYSR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "18"
          }
        ]
