    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01A",
    "text": "**Writing a `[[wikilink]]` code that survives the nuance above.** `astParser.ts`'s\n`collectLinkCodes` recognizes `[title]([[code]])` — a markdown link whose destination is wrapped in\n`[[...]]` — and `resolveBlockLinks` (`apeironNgn/artifacts.ts`) resolves `code` through the same\ndeep-path grammar `kg:resolve`/`kg:path` use (`resolveDeepPathDetail`, `--create-holder` on, so a\ngenuinely missing segment mints a holder rather than silently dropping the link). Two things worth\nspelling out, both easy to get wrong silently (no error, just a citation that never becomes a real\n`Link`):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR010",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01C"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01B",
    "text": "**A leading `/` makes `code` absolute** — resolved from the artifacts root regardless of which\n  block the link happens to sit in, the same as `aperas://tree/<path>`'s shorthand. Without it,\n  `code` resolves relative to the *containing block's own position* (`resolveBlockLinks`'s\n  `base: block.toPath()`) — correct for a citation to something nearby, but fragile for a citation\n  that could land anywhere in a long document: the right number of leading `..`s depends on\n  exactly how deep the citing paragraph happens to be nested, which shifts every time a section\n  gets reorganized. Every citation in this doc and `Aperas-treeview-design.md` uses the absolute\n  form for that reason — e.g. `/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/13.\n  Viewcone Zoom: projecting from an arbitrary apex`, following exactly the segment chain the\n  nuance above describes: doc filename, then its `H1` (literal `#` included), then each `##`/`###`\n  section by title in turn.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01C",
    "text": "**Wrap `[[...]]` in `<...>` whenever `code` contains a space** (i.e. essentially always, since a\n  path segment here is a real heading's own title text) — `[title]([[code with spaces]])` is not\n  valid CommonMark: a bare link destination can't contain unescaped spaces, so remark silently\n  parses it as plain text, never as a `link` node at all, and `collectLinkCodes` never sees it —\n  no error, no warning, the citation just quietly never becomes a real `Link`. `<...>`'s own\n  CommonMark destination form allows spaces (and is stripped back off before `code` reaches\n  `LINK_URL_RE`), so `[title](<[[code with spaces]]>)` is the form that actually works:\n  `[§13]([[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/#...]]>)` (missing the `<`) silently fails;\n  `[§13](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/#...]]>)` succeeds.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01D",
    "text": "**Verified**: `npm run build` (`tsc -b && vite build`) clean; `npm run verify`'s full demo harness\npassing end to end against the merged shape — tracking, ingestion, re-ingestion reconciliation\n(`18 matched, 0 moved, 0 changed, 2 added` on the edited re-ingest), `BlockNode.links` extraction\n(self-link now resolving to the `ArtifactNode` itself rather than a separate root `BlockNode`),\n`FolderNode` README projection (including a new explicit assertion that `FolderNode.text` picks up\na *headed* README's abstract — `# Demo Folder` first, `Intro sentence...` second — the exact shape\nthe old top-level-paragraph consuming rule produced an empty abstract for), and a dehydrate ->\nrehydrate round-trip (`{\"BlockNode\":27,\"ArtifactNode\":1,\"FolderNode\":4}`, 0 dangling references).\n`apeironNgnSmokeTest.ts` (ground-truth-vs-live-Store comparison against the real mirror) updated to\npick an artifact by `type !== undefined && children.length > 0` instead of a `root` reference, but\nnot itself re-verified end to end here — the real mirror is empty until the next real ingest sweep.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR010",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Step 8: `Link.props`, occurrence positions, and target-deduped wikilink edges — implemented, verified",
    "text": "**The gap.** Nothing correlates a specific `[[wikilink]]` occurrence in a block's `text` with the\n`Link` object it resolved to. Given a block whose text mentions two different targets (or the same\ntarget twice), a reader has `text` (raw, with the literal `[[code]]` substrings still in it) and\n`links` (a `Set` of `{predicate, target}`) but no way to line the two up short of independently\nre-running wikilink detection and hoping the order matches. Separately (found while designing a fix\nfor this): `resolveBlockLinks` (`artifacts.ts`, and the wikilink-regeneration fix earlier in this\ndoc's own narrative) mints one `Link` per raw occurrence, not per distinct target — so a block\nmentioning the same target twice already produces two edges to it today. That's a real problem\nbeyond tidiness: `.links` is a genuine second traversal axis alongside `children` (`TreeView.fold`,\n`node.ts`, walks both), so a duplicate edge to the same target is a graph-correctness smell, not\njust a display nit.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMG00V",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01Y",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR021"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F:props:StringProp:00CBFDYY28000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F:props:StringProp:00CBFDYY28001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "text": "**Resolved by generalizing `props` (Aperas-markdown-fractal-mapping-design.md §7) onto `Link`\nitself, rather than inventing a bespoke field for position specifically:**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01P"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01G",
    "text": "**One `Link` per distinct `(predicate, target)` pair per block, not per raw occurrence.** A block\n  mentioning the same target twice keeps one edge, carrying every position that target occurs at.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01H",
    "text": "**Position storage: `Link` gains `props: { cardinality: 'set', storageKind: 'embed' }`** — the\n  exact same `Set<StringProp>` mechanism `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` already use for\n  \"a new piece of type-conditional metadata never needs its own schema field\" (§7's own framing,\n  applied here to `Link` for the first time). A wikilink-derived `Link` carries one `{key:\n  'position', value: '<offset>'}` prop entry per occurrence of that target in the block's text —\n  a real multiset, reusing already-proven embed/mint/dehydrate/carry-forward machinery instead of\n  being the first thing to exercise a raw `cardinality: 'set'` *literal* field (which the\n  `writeField`/`readField` machinery already supports generically, just never exercised — `props`\n  is the safer, already-tested route to the same multiset capability). This settles a design\n  question `Link`'s own shape has carried since the original fractal-tree draft: `Link extends\n  BaseNode` (full inheritance, giving `props` but also an unwanted `links`/`tombstonedAt`/`holder`\n  — a link having its *own* outbound links or independent tombstone lifecycle corresponds to\n  nothing real) was tried and judged overkill; a single simple scalar field (enough for \"one note\n  per link,\" not enough for a set of positions without re-serializing into that one string) was\n  tried next and judged insufficient. Cherry-picking just `props` onto `Link` directly in\n  `LINK_SHAPE` — no `extends BaseNode`, since `SHAPE` tables are already fully flattened\n  per-class and don't rely on the class hierarchy for field composition — is the middle point:\n  exactly the one field actually needed, reusing the mechanism already proven elsewhere, nothing\n  dragged along unused.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01J",
    "text": "**Non-wikilink (`kg:link`, `'references'`-predicate) `Link`s share the same `props` slot** for\n  whatever metadata they might want later (a note, a timestamp) — no dedicated schema field needed\n  for that either, by the same §7 principle. `position` itself is only ever populated on\n  `WIKILINK_PREDICATE` links; a manual link has no source-text occurrence to position.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01K",
    "text": "**One number per occurrence — mdast's own link-node start offset** — not a start/end range and\n  not per-target-array-of-two-numbers. A single start position is sufficient to correlate an\n  occurrence with its `Link`; recovering the full span, if ever needed, re-applies the same\n  wikilink grammar starting from that known point rather than storing a redundant end offset.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01M",
    "text": "**Block-relative, never file-relative — deliberately, not as an afterthought.** Storing a\n  position relative to the whole file would reintroduce exactly the fragility git patches suffer\n  from (every edit before a link shifts its file offset, needing fuzzy context-matching to cope) —\n  precisely the class of problem the fractal block architecture exists to avoid by construction.\n  mdast's own link-node `position.start.offset` is file-relative by default; converting it to be\n  relative to the owning block's own (already-`.trim()`-ed) `text` needs to be one clean, correctly-\n  built primitive extending `rawSlice`'s own offset math (`astParser.ts`), not a subtraction\n  reinvented at each call site that happens to need it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01N",
    "text": "**Headings excluded from link-scanning entirely, not just from this position scheme.** A heading\n  functions as an anchor/target itself (other content links *to* it by title); a link nested inside\n  its own title text is an HTML nested-anchor situation (invalid per spec, and in practice exactly\n  as confusable as the spec's ban implies — confirmed the hard way, independent of this design).\n  `astParser.ts`'s heading case should stop calling `collectLinkCodes` on the heading's own title\n  line — only its consumed leading-paragraph `text` (§2's consuming rule) may still contain links.\n  Beyond the correctness case for banning it, this has a direct simplifying payoff for the position\n  scheme: with title excluded, every block's wikilink positions are relative to `text` alone,\n  uniformly — no `field: 'title' | 'text'` discriminant needed anywhere. (Real behavior change for\n  existing content: a wikilink written inside a heading's own title line stops resolving as a\n  `Link` edge on the next ingestion — the raw title text is unaffected, still a verbatim slice,\n  it simply stops being extracted.)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01P",
    "text": "**API gap to fill:** `props.ts`'s `getProp` is `.find()` — first match only, correct for\n  genuinely single-valued props (`frontmatter`, `orderedList`, `checked`). `position` is\n  legitimately multi-valued per `Link`, so it needs a plural sibling, `getProps(node, key):\n  string[]`, alongside the existing singular accessor.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01Q:props:StringProp:00CBFDYY2R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01Q:props:StringProp:00CBFDYY2R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR01Q",
    "text": "**Implemented as designed**, matching the surface sketched above one-for-one:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01X"
    ]
  },
