    "title": "00CBFDYXMR02K",
    "text": "*When are tombstones themselves ever removed?* At the time this was asked: never — a tombstoned\n    node kept its own quads (`title`/`type`/`text`/`tombstonedAt`) forever, mirroring TerminusDB's\n    soft-delete model (a permanent \"this used to exist, here's its last known state\" record), with\n    no expiry/compaction pass anywhere in the design. **Superseded by [Step 11](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence/Step 11: `parent`]]>)**: a tombstoned node\n    is removed once nothing live can reach it any more (its own structural subtree already cleared\n    at tombstoning time; the remaining question was always *incoming* references from elsewhere) —\n    see [Step 11](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence/Step 11: `parent`]]>)'s mark-and-sweep GC for the actual mechanism and when it runs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR02J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR02M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR02M:links:Link:00CBFDYZ6R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR01E",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR02M:links:Link:00CBFDYZ6R000:props:StringProp:00CBFDYZ70000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "415"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDYXMR02M",
    "text": "*Is `deleteSubdocument`'s orphan cleanup a correctness requirement, or just eager garbage\n    collection?* The latter, precisely — and more consequential than ordinary GC'd-language cleanup\n    would be, for a reason specific to this runtime. Nothing is semantically broken by an orphaned\n    `Link`/`StringProp` sitting around — `dehydrateToJsonLd` only walks structure reachable from its\n    own top-level sweep ([Step 8](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence/Step 8: `Link.props`, occurrence positions, and target-deduped wikilink edges — implemented, verified]]>)'s own framing), so an orphan is invisible to the mirror either way.\n    But `service.ts`'s `Store` is Rust compiled to WASM (`wasm-bindgen`), not a JS object — it lives\n    entirely in the WASM module's own linear memory, managed by Rust's ownership model, not the JS\n    GC. Rust's own collections do free memory internally when an entry is removed, reusable by later\n    allocations *within* that linear memory — but WebAssembly linear memory itself can only *grow*\n    (`memory.grow`); there is no instruction to shrink it back down. Whatever peak size the heap\n    reaches, the WASM instance holds for the rest of the process's life, however much of it is later\n    freed internally and sitting idle. So for this long-lived process specifically, letting orphans\n    accumulate before cleaning them up — rather than never creating them in the first place — would\n    leave a **permanent, irreversible** high-water mark: unlike a GC'd runtime, there is no way to\n    ever hand that memory back to the OS short of restarting the process. Eager cleanup avoids that\n    peak from forming at all, which is strictly more valuable here than the same cleanup would be in\n    a language with a real tracing GC underneath. (Not independently verified against Oxigraph's own\n    allocator/data-structure internals — this follows from WASM's memory model generally, which is\n    well established; if it ever matters enough to confirm precisely, measuring process RSS across a\n    long ingest-heavy `service.ts` session would settle it directly.)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR02J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR02N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
