    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Core model: placeholder is a flag, not a kind",
    "text": "Apeiron's unbounded-tree philosophy draws no distinction in *kind* between a placeholder and a\nreal node — a `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` marked `holder: true` is exactly the same\nshape of thing as one that isn't. `holder`'s only real meaning, confirmed by checking every place\nit's read (`node.ts`'s rendering, `FolderNode.hydrateFromParsed`'s preservation loop): **reconciliation\nsurvival** — \"don't tombstone me just because a disk-derived parse doesn't produce me; I'm not\nmissing content, I was never going to be produced by a disk walk in the first place.\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800C"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800C:links:Link:00CBFDZ0BG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800C:links:Link:00CBFDZ0BG000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0BG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "312"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800C",
    "text": "**Promotion** is simply clearing that flag once real content authoritatively arrives — through\nwhichever of the channels below apply to that node's kind. There's no separate \"placeholder\" type\nto convert *from*; a promoted node is the exact same node, same id, same position, just with the\nflag cleared and (per [§6](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)]]>) below) its derived fields finally populated.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800B",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. Promotion channels, per node category",
    "text": "A node's kind determines which channels apply — this is the actual reason Folder/Artifact and\nBlock need different treatment, not an arbitrary split.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 4.1 Folder/Artifact — no position channel",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ0ER000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800F:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ03G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800F:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ03G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800F",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800E",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800J"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800G:links:Link:00CBFDZ0C0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800G:links:Link:00CBFDZ0C0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0C0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "593"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800G",
    "text": "**(i) Ingestion match — path only, not text.** `trackArtifact`/`ingestFolderTree`'s first pass\n  matches purely on `path` (`findLiveArtifactByPath`, `existingByPath`); this is the whole\n  promotion story for the common case — a holder's `path` was set to the exact location real\n  content is expected to land. The *separate* rename-detection pass (`matchLeftoverByAbstract`,\n  exact-equality Gestalt matching on `.text`) exists to catch an already-real artifact moving to a\n  new path — it cannot serve as a promotion channel for a holder, because a holder's `.text` key is\n  always `''` (see [§6](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)]]>)), and even in the degenerate case of two simultaneously-empty keys,\n  `dropAmbiguousSingletons` (reconcile.ts:77-93) refuses the match as unanchored. These are\n  orthogonal mechanisms, not layered: path-match is promotion; text-match rename-detection is a\n  different feature for content that's already real.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800H:links:Link:00CBFDZ0CR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800K",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800H:links:Link:00CBFDZ0CR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0CR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "215"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800H:links:Link:00CBFDZ0CR002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800H:links:Link:00CBFDZ0CR002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0CR003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "457"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800H",
    "text": "**(o) Projection.** Writing an in-graph subtree back out to disk (`kg:project`/`runProject`) is\n  equally a promotion event — gated on the whole subtree being *purely concrete* (no holder\n  anywhere underneath; see [§4.2](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/4. Promotion channels, per node category/4.2 Block — the full three channels]]>)'s own **(o)**, which is where this requirement actually\n  originates). Needs new bookkeeping — see [§6](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)]]>).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800J",
    "text": "**No (p).** Folder/Artifact children carry an ordered `siblingIndex` only because the storage\n  mechanism is shared homogeneously with `BlockNode` — real filesystem order is never\n  human-authored (`folders.ts`'s `buildFolderTree` walks bare `readdirSync` with no `.sort()`\n  anywhere in the scan), so position carries no identity meaning for this kind. Folder/Artifact\n  creation stays append-only, correctly, not just \"good enough\" — `kg:insert` (§7) deliberately\n  excludes these kinds from positioning.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 4.2 Block — the full three channels",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800M"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800M:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ03R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800M:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ03R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800M",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800K",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800Q"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800N:links:Link:00CBFDZ0D8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800N:links:Link:00CBFDZ0D8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0D8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "439"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800N",
    "text": "**(i) Ingestion match — title only, via reconciliation.** Stage A's exact-title Gestalt matching\n  (`reconcile.ts`, `leafKey`) already promotes a matched holder heading for free: `carryForwardFields`\n  (reconcile.ts:137-150) whitelists only `blockId`/`title`/`links`/`props` — `.holder` was never in\n  that list, so the *reconciled plain-object tree* naturally ends up with no `.holder` at all. That\n  alone isn't sufficient, though — see [§6](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)]]>)'s note on `BlockNode.hydrateFromParsed`, which is what\n  actually writes the real store node and today never touches `.holder` at all.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800P:links:Link:00CBFDZ0DR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801F",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800P:links:Link:00CBFDZ0DR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0DR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "113"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800P",
    "text": "**(p) Relative position-setting.** Deliberately anchoring a block's position in Apeiron directly\n  (`kg:insert`, [§7](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/7. `kg:insert` — position, promote, or create]]>)) is itself sufficient promotion signal, independent of **(i)** — a Block\n  is the one kind where order is actually part of its meaning, so a human asserting \"this goes here\"\n  is real evidence of intent, not scaffolding. Applies unconditionally: repositioning an already-real\n  node is just a move, with the `.holder` clear being a no-op — there's no branch on prior holder\n  state, one mechanism covers both cases.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800Q:links:Link:00CBFDZ0ER001",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ0ER000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800Q:links:Link:00CBFDZ0ER001:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0ER002",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "103"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800Q",
    "text": "**(o) Projection.** Eligible only if it, and everything under it, is purely concrete — the same\n  gate [§4.1](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/4. Promotion channels, per node category/4.1 Folder/Artifact — no position channel]]>)'s own **(o)** relies on, just stated at the level it actually originates from (a\n  Folder/Artifact's own projectability is really \"is every Block underneath me concrete,\" recursively).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. The reconciliation \"nowhere problem,\" worked example",
    "text": "Today, *any* unmatched old child — holder or not — is destructive: `diffChildren` (reconcile.ts:164-234)\nroutes every `removedOld` index into `ctx.removedCandidates`, `reconcileNode` (reconcile.ts:334-336)\ncollects them there, and `detectCrossParentMoves` (reconcile.ts:386-389) ends with `tombstoneSubtree`\nfor whatever's left unclaimed by a cross-parent move. A block-level holder would be deleted the very\nfirst time its owning artifact is reconciled at all, unrelated edits included — confirmed live, this\nis why block-level holder support was left out originally: a floating placeholder has no obvious\nhome once the surrounding content shifts.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800Y",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800Z",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028010",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028011",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028012"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800S",
    "text": "The fix reuses scaffolding `diffChildren` already computes for an unrelated purpose (scoping Stage\nB's container alignment): its anchor-derived `segments` (reconcile.ts:194-202) partition old/new\nchildren into aligned ranges. A `removedOld` index necessarily falls inside exactly one such segment.\nInstead of pushing a `holder`-flagged `removedOld` child into `ctx.removedCandidates`, splice it\ninto `newNode.children` at that segment's `newRange` upper bound (i.e. immediately before the next\nanchor) — this keeps it between the same two real neighbors it originally sat between, even if\ncontent around it changed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800T",
    "text": "**Worked example** (all headings — text-less containers, matched purely by title, same as any\nStage-B container alignment already is):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800V",
    "text": "Old tree (holder present, never yet matched):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800W",
    "text": "```\n0: heading \"Intro\"\n1: heading \"Setup\"\n2: heading \"Future Section\"   holder:true, no .text\n3: heading \"Conclusion\"\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800X",
    "text": "New parse (unrelated edit elsewhere; \"Future Section\" still doesn't exist on disk):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800Y",
    "text": "```\n0: heading \"Intro\"\n1: heading \"Setup\"        (text changed — irrelevant, leafKey ignores it)\n2: heading \"Conclusion\"\n3: heading \"Appendix\"     (new)\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02800Z",
    "text": "Stage A matches `{0→0, 1→1, 3→2}` (the \"Intro,Setup\" run, then \"Conclusion\" alone); `\"Future\nSection\"` matches nothing → `removedOld = [2]`; `\"Appendix\"` is new → `addedNew = [3]`. The anchor\nwalk produces segment `{oldRange:[2,3), newRange:[2,2)}` for old-index 2 — an *empty* new range,\nbut not nowhere: it names index 2 in the final array, exactly the slot immediately before\n`Conclusion`. Splicing there gives:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028010",
    "text": "```\nIntro, Setup, [Future Section], Conclusion, Appendix\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028011",
    "text": "— original neighbors preserved, Appendix (added elsewhere) unaffected. This generalizes to a\nnon-empty segment too: the holder always lands immediately before the next real anchor, wherever\nthat anchor currently sits, whether or not something else was also added in the same gap.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028012",
    "text": "**Implementation note (not yet designed in code, flagged so it isn't lost).** `ChildDiff`\n(reconcile.ts:152-156) currently returns only `{matched, removedOld, addedNew}` — the `segments`\narray above is local to `diffChildren` and never exposed, so `reconcileNode` has no way to look up\nwhich segment a given `removedOld` index falls into. The interface needs extending to carry that\n(e.g. a splice-target index per holder-flagged `removedOld` entry). There's also an ordering hazard:\n`reconcileNode`'s matched-pairs loop (reconcile.ts:321-332) indexes `newNode.children[newIndex]` by\n*original* array position, so any splice into `newNode.children` must happen only *after* that loop\nfinishes — never interleaved with it — and if more than one holder lands at the same level in the\nsame call, they must be spliced from the highest target index down to the lowest, so an earlier\nsplice doesn't shift the target position of a later one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ04R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ04R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ028014",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028015",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028016",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028017",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028018",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028019",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801E",
      "BlockNode:00CBHMGY80017"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028015:links:Link:00CBFDZ0F8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028015:links:Link:00CBFDZ0F8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0F8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "171"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ028015",
    "text": "**`toReconcileShape()` (node.ts:487-499) doesn't include `.holder` at all** — `reconcile.ts` has\n  no way to see which old child is a placeholder yet. Needs adding before [§5](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/5. The reconciliation \"nowhere problem,\" worked example]]>)'s fix can work.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028016:links:Link:00CBFDZ0G0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028016:links:Link:00CBFDZ0G0000:props:StringProp:00CBHMGZNG000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "264"
          },
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028016:links:Link:00CBFDZ0G0000:props:StringProp:00CBHMGZNG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "426"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ028016",
    "text": "**`BlockNode.hydrateFromParsed` (node.ts:524-535) has no preservation logic** — only\n  `FolderNode.hydrateFromParsed` (node.ts:826-840) protects an unmatched holder from being\n  overwritten by the fresh parse's own `children` write. This is really the same gap as [§5](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/5. The reconciliation \"nowhere problem,\" worked example]]>)'s\n  tombstone problem, one layer up: with [§5](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/5. The reconciliation \"nowhere problem,\" worked example]]>)'s fix, reconciliation itself never lets a holder become\n  a `removedOld`-tombstone candidate, so `hydrateFromParsed`'s own `children` write can stay as-is.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028017",
    "text": "**`BlockNode.hydrateFromParsed` also never clears `.holder` on the matched-and-promoted node\n  itself.** §4.2 (i)'s claim that a Stage-A-matched holder \"naturally ends up with no `.holder`\"\n  describes only the reconciled *plain-object* tree `reconcileTree` hands back — `carryForwardFields`\n  never copies `.holder` onto that object, true. But the write that actually lands on the real\n  store node (same id, carried forward via `blockId`) is `BlockNode.hydrateFromParsed` (node.ts:524-535),\n  which sets `type`/`title`/`text`/`props`/`links`/`children` but never assigns `.holder` at all — so\n  whatever `holder:true` quad already exists on that id is left untouched forever. Needs an explicit\n  `this.holder = undefined`, same class of fix as the next bullet.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028018",
    "text": "**`FolderNode.hydrateFromParsed` has the identical gap for its own (i) channel.** §4.1 (i) is\n  scoped to `ArtifactNode`, but a holder-flagged `FolderNode` is just as real (minted today by\n  `resolveCreate.ts`'s deep-path scaffolding for an imagined intermediate folder). Once a real\n  directory appears on disk, `ingestFolderTree`/`buildFolderTree` reuse that `FolderNode` by path —\n  landing in `FolderNode.hydrateFromParsed` (node.ts:805-843), which sets `title`/`path`/`text`/\n  `props`/`children` but, like `BlockNode`'s own version, never touches `.holder`. Needs the same fix.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028019",
    "text": "**The Folder/Artifact rename/removal *sweep* is a wholly separate mechanism from the above, and\n  has the same \"nowhere problem\" §5 fixed at Block level — found live while testing §5's fix.**\n  `ingestFolderTree`'s own rename-detection sweep (`apeironNgn/folders.ts:77-83`, `dbOnlyPaths`/\n  `removedCandidates`) and `trackAllArtifacts`'s identical sweep (`apeironNgn/artifacts.ts:74,81`,\n  `dbOnlyIds`/`removedCandidates`) both tombstone any live Folder/Artifact whose `path` doesn't\n  appear in the fresh disk walk, unless `matchLeftoverByAbstract` finds a rename match for it —\n  neither checks `.holder` at all. A top-level holder Folder/Artifact (minted by\n  `resolveCreate.ts`'s scaffolding for an as-yet-nonexistent path) *always* looks exactly like a\n  removed node to this sweep, and — being freshly minted with no real abstract text yet — has\n  nothing for `matchLeftoverByAbstract` to rename-match either, so it gets tombstoned the very\n  first time `kg:ingest` runs a full pass, unrelated edits included. This is a different code path\n  from `FolderNode.hydrateFromParsed`'s own preservation loop just above (that one protects a\n  holder's *reference from its parent's `children`*; this sweep can tombstone the holder *itself*,\n  independent of whether any parent still points at it) — confirmed live: creating a holder\n  Folder/Artifact chain and then running a full `kg:ingest` tombstoned the holder folder outright.\n  Fix: exclude holder-flagged ids from `dbOnlyPaths`/`dbOnlyIds` in both places, before they ever\n  reach `removedCandidates` — the same \"not on disk isn't evidence of removal\" principle §5 already\n  established for Block-level children, applied at the sweep's own entry point instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801A",
    "text": "**Nothing ever clears `.holder` on the (o) promotion path either.** Confirmed: `runProject`\n  (kgProject.ts:19-45) doesn't touch `.holder` at all — needs an explicit `this.holder = undefined`\n  once real content is actually written, same as the two bullets above.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801B",
    "text": "**`ArtifactNode.text` is only ever computed by `ingestFromDisk`** (`extractAbstract(newRoot)`,\n  node.ts:660) — nothing derives it from a node's own live children. So **(o)** projection needs its\n  own equivalent derivation (walk `treeChildren` for the first concrete content, same shape as\n  `extractAbstract` but reading the graph instead of a parsed file) — otherwise a projected-and-thus-real\n  artifact would still read `.text === undefined` forever, and would still fail rename-detection if\n  it's ever later moved. **(o)** also needs to refresh `fileHash`/`ingestedHash`/`lastIngestedAt`\n  (so a subsequent real re-ingest of the same file reads \"unchanged,\" not a spurious reconciliation\n  against itself) — the same three fields `ingestFromDisk` already sets for **(i)**. `FolderNode`\n  has the identical gap, minus the hash fields it doesn't have: its own `.text` is likewise only\n  ever set by a real disk-read (`folders.ts`'s `buildFolderTree`), so a promoted-via-projection\n  `FolderNode` needs the same graph-based derivation too, or its own rename-detection fails the same\n  way `ArtifactNode`'s would.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801C",
    "text": "**`ArtifactNode.toMarkdown()`'s own guard was checking the wrong thing for this new channel** —\n  found while wiring the fix above. It returned `null` whenever `this.ingestedHash === undefined`\n  (node.ts:659, a proxy for \"nothing ingested yet\" that was accurate back when a real disk-based\n  ingest was the only way content ever arrived). `kg:insert` (§7) can now populate a holder\n  `ArtifactNode`'s children directly, with `ingestedHash` staying `undefined` forever since\n  `ingestFromDisk` never runs on it — so the old guard would refuse to render (and thus project)\n  perfectly real, `kg:insert`-authored content. Fixed by checking `children.length === 0` instead —\n  `children` is never `undefined` for `orderedContainment` fields (always a real, possibly-empty\n  array by construction), so this is the actually-correct \"nothing to render yet\" signal, and it's\n  correct for content that arrived via either channel.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801D:links:Link:00CBFDZ0GR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800K",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801D:links:Link:00CBFDZ0GR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0GR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "192"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801D",
    "text": "**`runProject` doesn't check subtree purity.** Nothing today stops projecting a subtree that\n  still has holder descendants mid-tree, silently baking placeholder content into a \"real\" file.\n  [§4.2](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/4. Promotion channels, per node category/4.2 Block — the full three channels]]>)'s **(o)** gate needs an actual `hasNoPlaceholderDescendant`-style check before writing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801E",
    "text": "**`project.ts`'s renderer is not tombstone-aware — found live via `kg:remove` followed by\n  `kg:project` on the same artifact.** Tombstoning (`applyTombstone`/`tombstoneLiveSubtree`)\n  deliberately leaves a dead node in its parent's `children` list, marked via `tombstonedAt` rather\n  than spliced out, so mark-and-sweep GC and referrer tracking still see it. Every tree-rendering\n  site in `node.ts` already knows to check for this (`tombstoneTag`), but `project.ts`'s\n  `renderChildren` (which both `ArtifactNode.toMarkdown()` and `FolderNode.toReadme()` funnel every\n  child through via `serializeBlock`) walks `node.children` unconditionally, with no `tombstonedAt`\n  check anywhere — so a tombstoned child's last-known content gets silently re-emitted into the\n  projected file. Not specific to `kg:remove`: any tombstone, however it got there (an ordinary\n  `kg:update` reconcile against now-removed content included), leaks the same way. Fix: filter\n  `!c.tombstonedAt` out of `renderChildren`'s children before walking/serializing them.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801F:links:Link:00CBFDZ0HR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR02P",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801F:links:Link:00CBFDZ0HR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0HR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "469"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 7. `kg:insert` — position, promote, or create",
    "text": "**The primitive.** `insertOrderedChild(store, parentId, childId, anchorId, side: 'before' | 'after')`\n— read the parent's current ordered children, splice `childId` next to `anchorId`, rewrite every\nsibling's `siblingIndex` from the resulting array position. This is not a new convention —\n`siblingIndex` values are already contiguous `0..N-1` per parent by construction today, every\nremoval path already being a full rewrite that renormalizes from array position (per\n[Step 11](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence/Step 11: `parent`]]>)'s own doc note in `Aperas-apeironngn-design.md`); insertion just needs the same\ntreatment on the other side.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801M"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801G:links:Link:00CBFDZ0J8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801N",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801G:links:Link:00CBFDZ0J8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0J8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "178"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ05G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ05G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801G",
    "text": "**The command, `kg:insert <path> [--after <anchor>|--before <anchor>]`, is concrete-only** — it\nnever mints abstract placeholders (that stays `kg:resolve --create-holder`'s job, [§8](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/8. `kg:resolve --create-holder` — scope and internal unification]]>)), and it\nnever scaffolds missing parent structure — the parent named or implied must already exist, holder\nor real, it doesn't matter which. It has two modes, distinguished by whether markdown is piped to\nstdin (`process.stdin.isTTY` — no pipe means no stdin content):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801F",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801J"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801H",
    "text": "**No stdin — move/promote an existing node.** `<path>` names the existing node itself (real or\n  holder). Effect: reposition it relative to `--after`/`--before <anchor>` (the anchor's *current*\n  parent becomes the node's new parent — cross-parent moves work for free, no special-casing), and\n  unconditionally clear `.holder`. If the node was already real, the `.holder` clear is simply a\n  no-op and this is a plain move. Omitting both `--after` and `--before` is valid: a bare promote\n  with no repositioning (useful when a holder is already sitting exactly where it should — e.g. one\n  auto-created in place by a dangling wikilink whose own deep path already implied the right\n  parent).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801J",
    "text": "**Stdin piped — create a new concrete node.** `<path>` names the **parent** to create under (not\n  the new node's own address — there's nothing left for a path segment to name once the node's\n  title comes from the parsed content instead of a `--titles` flag). The piped markdown is parsed\n  via the same `parseMarkdownTree` real artifacts use; each new node's type/title/text/children are\n  taken entirely from that parse (a multi-block snippet becomes a real subtree, not a single flat\n  node) — no `--type`/`--titles` needed. If the parsed content has more than one top-level block\n  (e.g. two sibling headings), all of them are created and inserted as a sequence at the anchor\n  position, preserving their own relative order — not merged into one node, not rejected. The whole\n  sequence is attached under `<path>` at `--after`/`--before <anchor>` (or appended at the tail if no\n  anchor given), and is real from the moment it's created — it never passes through a `holder:true`\n  state, unlike a placeholder minted by `--create-holder`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801K:links:Link:00CBFDZ0JR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028022",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801K:links:Link:00CBFDZ0JR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0JR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "428"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801K",
    "text": "**Wire note.** Piped content reaches the service as a plain string field on the request object\n(e.g. `{ op: 'insert', path, markdown, after, before, flush }`) — the existing NDJSON framing\nalready handles this safely, since `JSON.stringify` escapes embedded newlines (the same reasoning\n`service.ts`'s own protocol doc comment already gives for the wire format generally); no new framing\nis needed. Same applies to `kg:update` ([§9](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/9. `kg:update` and `--text-only`]]>)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801F",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801M:links:Link:00CBFDZ0K8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ0ER000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801M:links:Link:00CBFDZ0K8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0K8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "123"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801M",
    "text": "**Guards:** the target (existing-node mode) must resolve to a `BlockNode` — `ArtifactNode`/\n`FolderNode` are rejected, per [§4.1](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/4. Promotion channels, per node category/4.1 Folder/Artifact — no position channel]]>)'s \"no (p)\" ruling. The anchor (if given) must resolve and must\nnot be the same node as the target. `--after` and `--before` are mutually exclusive.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801F",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 8. `kg:resolve --create-holder` — scope and internal unification",
    "text": "**Unchanged externally.** This stays the *abstract-only* mechanism: mints a forward-reference\nplaceholder chain (`holder:true` throughout), append-only, used both manually and automatically\n(`resolveBlockLinks` passes `createHolder: true` for every dangling wikilink). It answers \"I don't\nknow exactly where this ends up yet, I just need something to point at\" — the opposite of\n`kg:insert`'s \"I know exactly what and where.\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028021"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801P:links:Link:00CBFDZ0KG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801P:links:Link:00CBFDZ0KG000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0KR000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "241"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801P",
    "text": "**Why Block-tier creation here is always `type: 'heading'`, not user-specifiable.**\nReconciliation's matching key (`reconcile.ts`'s `leafKey`) is `node.type === 'heading' ? node.title\n: (node.text ?? '')`. A holder's `.text` is always `''` ([§6](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)]]>)), and two empty-text candidates are\ndeclined as an unanchored ambiguous match (`dropAmbiguousSingletons`). So a non-heading placeholder\ncould never be matched/promoted via channel (i) at all — it would sit forever unfindable by any\nfuture real content. This isn't a missing feature to add later; it's why the type has to stay fixed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801N",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Q:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ060000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Q:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ060001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801Q",
    "text": "**How `--titles` is actually consumed (as it exists today, restated precisely since it's easy to\nmisread).** It's one flat array, but it is *not* one entry per path segment:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801N",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801V"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801R",
    "text": "Folder/Artifact segment titles are always the **literal path-segment text itself** (`descend`'s\n  miss-branch, node.ts — see below) — never drawn from `--titles`. A folder/file's title is just\n  its own filesystem name; there's nothing separate to supply.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801S",
    "text": "Only Block/heading segments — past the `.md` boundary — consume `--titles` entries, tail-aligned\n  against however many *name* tokens are still being resolved at that point. So the number of\n  `--titles` needed equals the number of new heading segments past the artifact boundary, not the\n  total path segment count.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801T",
    "text": "**Each `--titles` entry must be the full markdown heading line, `#` prefix included** (e.g.\n  `### 1.2.3`, not `1.2.3`) — confirmed live, the hard way: `serializeBlock` (project.ts) emits a\n  heading's `title` as-is with no prefix synthesized, exactly matching what a real parsed heading's\n  own title already contains (`rawSlice` captures the whole raw line). A bare title with no `#`\n  round-trips silently as a `paragraph`, not a `heading`, the moment the holder is promoted/projected\n  and re-ingested — the reconciler isn't wrong to treat that as a real structural change; the title\n  was simply never in the shape a heading's title is supposed to be in.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801V",
    "text": "The Folder/Artifact-vs-Block boundary itself is detected purely syntactically: the **first name\n  segment whose text ends in `.md`** is the artifact; everything before it is Folder tier, everything\n  after is Block tier. If no segment ends in `.md`, resolution refuses to guess and throws.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801W",
    "text": "**The gap found, and the fix (full unification, approved).** `resolveTokens`'s hop-by-hop walk\n(via `TreeNode.findChild`) only fell into the old `createImaginedPrefix` when *nothing at all*\nresolved from the root (`consumed === 0`). If some real prefix existed and then a miss occurred\n(`consumed > 0`), it called `descend()` directly instead — and `descend()`'s miss-branch\nunconditionally minted a `BlockNode` heading regardless of tier. So e.g. resolving\n`foo/bar/baz.md/heading --titles Heading` where `foo` is a real folder but `bar` isn't yet: the walk\nconsumes `foo`, misses on `bar`, and wrongly minted `bar` as a heading instead of a `FolderNode` —\n`createImaginedPrefix`'s own `.md`-boundary logic never ran at all for this case.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801N",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801X:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ06G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801X:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ06G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801X",
    "text": "Fixed by generalizing `descend()`'s miss-branch to check `kindOf(currentId)`:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801N",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Y",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Z",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028020"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801Y",
    "text": "Current node is `FolderNode` and the missed token doesn't end in `.md` → mint a `FolderNode`\n  (literal-text title, same as `createImaginedPrefix` used to).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801X",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02801Z",
    "text": "Current node is `FolderNode` and the missed token *does* end in `.md` → mint an `ArtifactNode`\n  (literal-text title).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801X",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028020",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028020",
    "text": "Current node is `ArtifactNode`/`BlockNode` → mint a `BlockNode` heading (title from `--titles`,\n  unchanged from today).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801X",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028021",
    "text": "With this in place, `createImaginedPrefix` is removed entirely — the `consumed === 0` case in\n`resolveTokens` just calls `descend(store, rootId, tokens, opts, trace)` directly and the same\ngeneralized miss-handling covers it, no separate bottom-up-then-attach construction needed. The one\nthing that needs explicit re-preservation: the upfront \"some segment must end in `.md`\" validation,\nre-expressed incrementally (before minting a new `FolderNode`, confirm at least one *later*\nremaining token still ends in `.md`; otherwise throw the same \"needs a filename to know where the\nartifact boundary is\" error) rather than a single upfront scan.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02801N",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 9. `kg:update` and `--text-only`",
    "text": "Generalizes `ArtifactNode.ingestFromDisk`'s own mechanism (parse, then either fresh-hydrate or\n`reconcileTree`-reconcile depending on whether there's already content) from \"artifact root\" down to\n**any existing node**. `<path>` always names the existing target; stdin is always required — there's\nno dual-mode ambiguity here the way there is for `kg:insert`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028026"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023:links:Link:00CBFDZ0MG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800K",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023:links:Link:00CBFDZ0MG000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0MG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "846"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023:links:Link:00CBFDZ0MG002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023:links:Link:00CBFDZ0MG002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0MG003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1084"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ070000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ070001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ028023",
    "text": "Both modes start identically: parse stdin via `parseMarkdownTree`, whose own leading-paragraph\n\"consuming\" rule (`astParser.ts` §2) only fires for a real heading/listItem *container* — the piped\nmarkdown is parsed as a standalone root, which never gets it (checked live: `isHeadingOrListItem`\nis `false` for a file's own root). `path` here plays exactly the role a heading would, though, so\nthis replicates the rule by hand rather than getting it for free: if the parsed root's first child\nis a `paragraph`, its text becomes `path.text` and it's dropped from the child list before anything\nelse runs; `path`'s own `type`/`title` are never touched, only `text`/`children`. Both modes also\nunconditionally clear `path.holder` — real content just arrived, regardless of which mode runs next,\nsame reasoning as `kg:insert`'s own unconditional clear ([§4.2](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/4. Promotion channels, per node category/4.2 Block — the full three channels]]>) (p)). Confirmed live: the default\nmode gets this for free via `hydrateFromParsed`'s own clear ([§6](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)]]>)); `--text-only` never calls\n`hydrateFromParsed`, so it needs the same clear spelled out explicitly instead. They differ in what\nhappens to everything after that:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028022",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028024",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028025"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028024:links:Link:00CBFDZ0N0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02800R",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028024:links:Link:00CBFDZ0N0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0N0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "132"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ028024",
    "text": "**Default — full reconcile.** Overflow blocks are diffed against `path`'s existing children via\n  the full Gestalt-match machinery ([§5](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/5. The reconciliation \"nowhere problem,\" worked example]]>)'s apparatus) — matched/moved/changed/removed/added,\n  identical to what a real artifact re-ingest already does.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028025",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ028025",
    "text": "**`--text-only`.** Overflow blocks still become children — never silently dropped — but via a raw\n  **prepend** ahead of whatever's already there, with no diffing and no attempt to match/reuse\n  existing child identities. This is the cheap path: skip the expensive, identity-preserving\n  reconciliation when all that's actually wanted is a text-field update, while still not losing any\n  extra content that happened to be piped in alongside it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028023",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028026",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028026:links:Link:00CBFDZ0NG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028013",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028026:links:Link:00CBFDZ0NG000:props:StringProp:00CBHMH0E8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1160"
          },
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028026:links:Link:00CBFDZ0NG000:props:StringProp:00CBHMH0E8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1536"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ028026",
    "text": "**Targeting an `ArtifactNode` specifically.** `kg:update` never reads or writes the backing file, so\n`fileHash` is correctly left untouched — but leaving `ingestedHash` untouched too would be wrong:\nthe next plain `kg:ingest` of that file would still see `ingestedHash === fileHash` (if the file\nitself hasn't changed) and skip re-processing entirely, silently leaving the `kg:update`-injected\ncontent permanently unreconciled against the file. Fixed by having `kg:update` recompute\n`ingestedHash` itself — via the same in-memory rendering pipeline `kg:project`/`runProject` already\nuses (`toMarkdown()` + `withFrontmatter`), hashed with the same `computeFileHash` real files use, but\nnever written to disk. `fileHash` stays exactly as it was (only a real disk read ever changes it),\nso `ingestedHash === fileHash` now correctly fails the moment the graph and file diverge — forcing\nthe next real ingest to reconcile properly instead of wrongly skipping, whether or not the file\nitself ever actually changes in the meantime. Can call `toMarkdown()`'s own public accessor\ndirectly, no bypass needed — its guard now checks `children.length`, not `ingestedHash` ([§6](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)]]>)'s\nown toMarkdown fix), so it already returns real content the moment `kg:update` has written any,\nregardless of whether a real ingest ever ran. This isn't gated on subtree purity the way\n`kg:project`'s own **(o)** channel is ([§6](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/6. Bookkeeping gaps found while designing this (all currently real, all need closing)]]>)) — it's a fingerprint, not a disk write, so a holder elsewhere in the\nsubtree doesn't block it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028022",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028027",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 10. `kg:remove`",
    "text": "Wraps the existing `tombstoneLiveSubtree` (node.ts:706-714) for manual, on-demand invocation against\nan arbitrary node — today that function only ever runs as a side effect of a whole tracked artifact\ndisappearing from disk (`artifacts.ts`'s artifact-tombstone path). `kg:remove <path>` exposes the\nsame recursive tombstone directly, against any Block/Artifact/Folder target (the artifacts root\nitself, `path === '.'`, is explicitly refused — tombstoning the whole corpus is never intended).\nExplicitly **not** a hard delete — `hardDeleteNode` (the actual quad-erasing GC primitive used for\norphaned embedded subdocuments) stays internal-only, never exposed to users; a tombstoned node's\nquads remain, inert, exactly like any other reconciliation removal.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028028",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028028:links:Link:00CBFDZ0P0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028004",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028028:links:Link:00CBFDZ0P0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0P0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "101"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 11. `kg:title`",
    "text": "Already implemented (interactive, reads from stdin/readline) — included here only so the CRUD map\nin [§2](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/2. Command map]]>) is complete. No design changes from this round.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028029",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 12. Considered and rejected",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802A:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ07G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802A:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ07G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02802A",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028029",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802D"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02802B",
    "text": "**Ordinal/absolute-index addressing** (e.g. treating a bare `\"2\"` or `\"§2\"` code as \"the 2nd\n  child,\" for the case where a wikilink forward-references content that doesn't exist yet). Turned\n  out to be based on a misreading of the actual link syntax: a real wikilink is\n  `[§2](<[[/full/real/path/with/the/real/heading/title]]>)` — the `§2` is display text only; the\n  actual resolution path already carries the real title. So `resolveBlockLinks`\n  (apeironNgn/artifacts.ts:194-205) never needs anything beyond ordinary exact-then-prefix title\n  matching (`findChild`, node.ts:428-439), which already works today. Heuristically extracting a\n  position from a numbered heading title (`\"2. Concrete design\"` → position 2) was also considered\n  and rejected as too risky — not worth the guess, consistent with reconciliation's own general\n  \"decline rather than guess\" posture.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802C:links:Link:00CBFDZ0PG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ0ER000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802C:links:Link:00CBFDZ0PG000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ0PR000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "63"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ02802C",
    "text": "**Text-match rename-detection as a holder-promotion channel** ([§4.1](<[[/Aperas-crud-design.md/# Apeiron Node CRUD — Design/4. Promotion channels, per node category/4.1 Folder/Artifact — no position channel]]>)) — structurally can't\n  work (empty-string key, ambiguous-singleton guard), and even if it could, blending it with\n  path-match would muddy two mechanisms that are conceptually answering different questions (\"did\n  this move\" vs. \"did the thing I was waiting for arrive\").",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ02802D",
    "text": "**Separate `kg:move` + `kg:create` commands, instead of one unified `kg:insert`.** Considered\n  because \"for an existing node this is just a move\" is a fair observation on its own — but splitting\n  it would mean duplicating the same deep-path resolution and positioning logic across two commands\n  for no real benefit. One command whose behavior branches on \"does the target already exist\" is\n  simpler, and mirrors how `ingestFromDisk` already unifies \"fresh hydrate\" vs. \"reconcile\" under one\n  operation rather than two. (This is unrelated to `kg:resolve --create-holder`, which stays its own\n  command regardless — the split that was actually rejected here is move-vs-create, not\n  abstract-vs-concrete.)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802E:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ07R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ02802E:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ07R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 13. Status",
    "text": "**Implemented and verified** (`npm run verify` green throughout; every mechanism below additionally\ncovered by disk-free unit tests — a plain in-memory `Store`, no service/disk involved — exercising\nthe specific scenario each fix targets, all removed again once confirmed since they were scratch,\nnot permanent suite additions):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ028001",
