  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ138005",
    "text": "See `Aperas-architecture.md` for how everything below is actually built (schema, CRUD, WOQL/GraphQL, version control, artifact ingestion, KG backup/restore, cross-machine remotes). **Stale as of the ApeironNgn migration** (see `Aperas-apeironngn-design.md`) — it still describes the now-archived TerminusDB implementation as the current system; migrating it to describe ApeironNgn is a deferred follow-up, not yet done.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ13R002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ140000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ138006",
    "text": "Phase 0: Substrate and Core Skills — **Done, closed out.** See roadmap (`Aperas-design.md`'s Development Roadmap, Phase 0). Implemented and live-verified: direct database integration, `npm run verify:phase0 -- --db` + `npm run build` both clean, basic assertion authoring, and agentic query tools. The two loose ends open as of an earlier review — cross-machine push/pull/rebase reconciliation, and Stage 2 item 5's title/text split — were both closed below, well before Phase 0's actual close. The closing act was the ApeironNgn CRUD surface (below) and the Phase 0 → Phase 1 direction flip it enabled — Phase 1 (work directly on the graph; `artifacts/*.md` is now a projected *output*, not the source of truth) is the live posture as of this entry. \"Not yet started\" (further down) carries nothing Phase-0-scoped anymore, only Phase 1/2 items.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138007",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800D"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ138007",
    "text": "Schema initialization — done.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ138008",
    "text": "Artifact tracking & on-demand ingestion (`kg:track`/`kg:ingest`) — done, re-verified live against the new fractal-ontology schema.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ138009",
    "text": "**Fixed**: `kg:ingest` used to double-ingest `README.md`. `listArtifactFiles` (`artifacts.ts`) walked every `.md` file with no README exclusion, so `ingestAllArtifacts` ingested a directory's `README.md` as an ordinary `ArtifactNode`+`BlockNode` tree — in addition to, and completely independently of, `folders.ts`'s `buildFolderTree`, which separately parses the same file and correctly folds its content into the owning `FolderNode`. Reproduced live before fixing: a real `README.md` produced both a correct `FolderNode`-absorbed copy and a separate stray `ArtifactNode`. Root cause: `artifacts.ts` and `folders.ts` each walked `AperasKG/artifacts/` with their own file-listing logic, only one of which knew to skip README files. Fixed by factoring one shared `isReadmeFilename` predicate into `artifacts.ts` (which `folders.ts` now imports instead of its own local constant) and applying it in `listArtifactFiles` — plus the same guard added to `kg:track`'s explicit single-file path (`kgCli.ts`), which bypasses that list entirely and needed its own check.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800A",
    "text": "Automated tracking trigger (`AperasKG/.githooks/post-commit`, `post-index-change`) — done: `post-commit` auto-runs a scoped `kg:track` on commit, `post-index-change` auto-runs a full `kg:track` sweep on branch checkout / `git reset` (including `--hard`, which fires no hook of its own) — `post-checkout` was tried too but dropped as redundant once `post-index-change` was confirmed to fire alongside it with equivalent coverage.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800B",
    "text": "Temporal commit management — done (`versionControl.ts` is class-agnostic — branch/commit/diff/reset operations, unaffected by the schema redesign below).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800C",
    "text": "Cross-machine remotes — **done**: reciprocal SSH-tunneled TerminusDB remotes (reciprocal remote endpoints), `fetch` confirmed both directions. `push`/`pull` themselves only fast-forward and fail with `no_common_history` once both sides have diverged past their common point (no `--force` flag exists either) — reconciliation is `rebase` (replays one side's divergent commits onto a base built from the other, see `tdb-cli-status-walkthrough.md` §6) or, for a deliberate discard-and-replace sync, `reset` directly onto a fetched commit id (§7, reciprocal in both directions, no intermediate branch needed). Both live-verified against the real `aperas` DB: rebased a 9-commit remote-derived branch onto local `main`'s 1120 commits (zero conflicts; the reverse direction hangs — direction matters, see §6.2), then promoted `main` to the reconciled 1129-commit tip via `reset`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800D",
    "text": "Reference tooling — `skills/terminusdb/references/cli.md` audited end-to-end against a live server and the official CLI docs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800E",
    "text": "KG backup/restore — `restore.sh`/`tdb-log.sh`/`tdb-doc.sh` relocated to `Aperas/scripts/` and re-scoped as Core-Engine infra (documented in `Aperas-architecture.md` §1/§8), not domain-KG-specific — `db/` itself is gone from `AperasKG/`. `tdb-cross-diff.sh` was *not* migrated with them (its own hash-gated rewrite was already pending the ID-identity fix); genuinely gone, not relocated. **Further relocated to `Aperas/.archive/scripts/`** as part of the ApeironNgn migration's archiving pass (`Aperas-apeironngn-design.md` §4) — TerminusDB itself is no longer the live substrate, so this infra is retired, kept for reference before eventual removal rather than deleted outright.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800F",
    "text": "**ApeironNgn engine swap — done.** TerminusDB retired as the live substrate; ApeironNgn (in-process Oxigraph, `web/src/lib/apeironNgn/`) is now it. Every `kg:*` npm command and `verify` are the ApeironNgn scripts under their plain names (`kg:track`, `kg:tree`, ...) — the TerminusDB-backed originals were renamed with a `Tdb` suffix and archived to `web/.archive/src/lib/` (self-contained: shared deps copied in, not reached-back-into) alongside `.archive/scripts/`'s Docker/TDB infra. Every `kg:*` command now talks to a shared background service (auto-started on first use, holding the mirror in memory rather than rehydrating/dehydrating it per invocation) instead of running standalone — `--flush` forces an immediate write, used by the `pre-commit` hook. Full rollout history, the write-path validation against real artifacts, bugs found and fixed along the way, the archiving mechanics, and the service process design are in `Aperas-apeironngn-design.md`. Follow-up not yet done: `Aperas-architecture.md` still describes the archived TDB implementation as current (see the note at the top of this doc).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ14G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ14G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800G",
    "text": "**RESOLVED** Design issue: \"AperasKG stores the KG\" was answered with a DB backup, not a KG export — corrected",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800H",
    "text": "`AperasKG` was designed to hold both the domain artifacts and the KG itself, but the KG lives in a DB — so the earlier approach to fulfilling that second half was to associate whole-TerminusDB-volume/bundle backups (`restore.sh`, briefly stored under `AperasKG/db/`) with the domain-KG repo. That coupling was the wrong move: a git-tracked repo has no good way to diff, merge, or meaningfully version an opaque binary DB snapshot, and (per the `#2509` investigation above) those snapshots turned out not to even be portable between independent TerminusDB stores — so it wasn't a viable backup path for `AperasKG` even setting the git-coupling problem aside.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800J",
    "text": "Corrected via the JSON-LD import/export round-trip (`web/src/lib/export.ts`, `kg:export`/`kg:import`, `Aperas-architecture.md` §5): `AperasKG/Apeiron/` holds the canonical graph content as plain, diffable JSON-LD text, committed to the same repo as `AperasKG/artifacts/` — an ordinary `git commit` over that directory is now the KG's backup, `kg:import` is the restore path.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800K",
    "text": "`restore.sh`'s whole-volume/bundle mechanism (§6) is unaffected and stays as-is — it's TerminusDB server infrastructure (Docker-volume disaster recovery), a different, unrelated concern from how `AperasKG` records its own content. Likewise, the SSH-tunneled TerminusDB remotes (§7) are unrelated to `AperasKG` — that's cross-machine sync for the TerminusDB server itself, not something this redesign touches.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ13800M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ13800M",
    "text": "**Fixed**: TerminusDB database renamed `aperas_apeiron` → `aperas` — the `aperas_` prefix was project-namespacing for a database name that already only ever means one thing on this dedicated instance, and `Apeiron` names a philosophical/architectural role (the fluid core substrate), not a resource identifier; naming the database after the project itself was simpler and avoided that mismatch. Migrated live via the JSON-LD round-trip rather than any TerminusDB-native rename (there isn't one — only `createDatabase`/`deleteDatabase`): `kg:export` from the old database, `kg:import` into a freshly created `aperas`, content parity verified class-by-class via the same order-independent `hashDocSet` hash `kg:import`'s own skip-if-unchanged check uses (all 5 matched: schema, `ArtifactNode`, `BlockNode`, `FolderNode`, `Assertion`), then the old `aperas_apeiron` database deleted. All defaults (`client.ts`, `tdb-log.sh`, `tdb-doc.sh`, `restore.sh`) and doc mentions updated to match.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ138004",
