    ],
    "title": "# TreeView & Profile — Design",
    "text": "**Status: implemented, live-verified against a synthetic graph exercising every scenario [§4](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/4. Worked example]]>) works\nthrough by hand — not yet run against the real `AperasKG/Apeiron/` corpus.** Written to pin down\nthe design before Phase 0 work on it started, per `Aperas-design.md`'s roadmap: the tree view\nneeded refactoring in Phase 0 so Phase 1's Human UI Peras would have something stable to build\nagainst, rather than being built on top of the single-flag model this replaces and then needing\nrework once multiple viewers exist. Grounded in\n`Aperas-apeironngn-design.md` and the live `web/src/lib/apeironNgn/` code, not in\n`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`'s older Three-Projection-Modes/Intrinsic-vs-Extrinsic-links\nframing — that doc predates the ApeironNgn migration and describes a model since superseded in\nreal ways (e.g. `Assertion`/`BaseEdge` eliminated outright, `Link` demoted from a top-level node\nto a `BlockNode`-owned subdocument). Only \"Abstraction by Folding\" — fold a subtree to an\nabstract, an abstract to a title — carries forward as still-live philosophy; the specific\nmechanics below are new, not a restatement of that doc's [§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>).",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW958000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0023",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0024",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W002A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W8006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W800E"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. The problem: `unfolded` is one flag, shared by everyone",
    "text": "Today `BaseNode.unfolded` (`shape.ts`'s `BASE_NODE_SHAPE`) is a single boolean per node, in the\ngraph itself, read and written by whoever last called `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold`\n(`Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md`). A human expanding a subtree and an agent doing its own\nBFS traversal are the same writer as far as the graph is concerned — one collapses what the other\njust expanded. There's no \"expanded for me\" versus \"expanded in the underlying data.\" Separately,\n`links` are graph structure `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` already carry (`BaseNode.links`,\ninherited by `TreeNode` and everything under it) but nothing today makes them part of a folded\nwalk the way structural `children` are.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0003:links:Link:00CBFDZ27G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0003:links:Link:00CBFDZ27G000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ27R000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1273"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0003",
    "text": "**Why links belong in the walk at all.** Every node's own view is centered on itself — it's always\nthe center of its own world. Structural `children` is the *physical* structure: fixed, authored,\npositional, the same distance apart for every viewer regardless of what they're currently thinking\nabout. `links` form a second, independent structure over the same nodes — the *mental* structure:\nwhich nodes are related enough, right now, to think about together, regardless of where they\nphysically sit in the containment tree. An assertion drawing on three nodes scattered across three\ndifferent documents wants those three treated as siblings for the purpose of writing it — placed\nside by side for direct comparison — not left at whatever physical distance separates them in the\ntree, the way a split-screen editor puts two unrelated files next to each other because you're\nworking on them together, not because they live near each other on disk. That's what folding links\ninto the same walk as `children` buys an i-projection that a purely-structural tree could never give:\nbringing mentally-related content into direct proximity without moving or duplicating anything in\nthe physical tree. Physical structure still wins wherever the two collide in the combined render —\n[§6](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers]]>)'s canonical-position rule (a node's structural home is always canonical when it qualifies) is\nexactly this principle made concrete: mental structure supplements the physical tree at the\npositions links create, it never overrides it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ1WG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ1WG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 2. Three tiers of perata, not one",
    "text": "`Aperas-design.md`'s own vocabulary (Apeiron the substrate, Aperas the transduction, Peras/perata\nthe crystallized interfaces it produces) already implies layering; the graph today only really has\ntwo: the raw Apeiron store, and whatever a given projection (`kg:tree`, `kg:project`, the future\nWeb UI) renders directly off it. This proposal makes the middle layer real:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0005",
    "text": "**Artifact projection** (unchanged) — the existing always-fully-unfolded `.md` sync\n   (`Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md`). `BlockNode.children` stays the source of truth for\n   document structure; this tier ignores fold state entirely, exactly as today.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0006:links:Link:00CBFDZ280000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0008",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0006:links:Link:00CBFDZ280000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ280001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "468"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0006",
    "text": "**i-projection** — the new intermediate tier this doc proposes. \"i-\" is deliberately underspecified,\n   the same way the project's other core terms (Apeiron/Aperas/Peras) carry more than one reading\n   at once: imagine, inner/internal, idea, intel, intermediate, index all apply to what this tier\n   does — it's the graph's own internal, indexable idea of \"what a viewer currently sees,\" sitting\n   between the raw substrate and any outward-facing render. `TreeView` ([§3](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/3. Data model: `Profile` and `TreeView`]]>)) is the first, and for\n   now the only, i-projection kind.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0007",
    "text": "**UI projection** — Human UI and Agentic Interface, both consuming an i-projection rather than\n   reading `BaseNode.unfolded` off the graph directly. Out of scope here beyond noting that this is\n   the dependency Phase 1's Web App needs settled first.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Data model: `Profile` and `TreeView`",
    "text": "Two new top-level classes, alongside the existing `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`/`Link`/\n`StringProp` (`node.ts`'s `CLASS_BY_KIND`, `vocab.ts`'s `ID_PREFIX_RE`/`KIND_RE`, `shape.ts`'s\n`SHAPE_BY_KIND` — all three need entries for both). Named `Profile`/`TreeView`, not\n`ProfileNode`/`TreeViewNode` — neither is a `TreeNode` subclass (no title/text/tree position of its\nown), so the un-suffixed naming matches `Link`/`StringProp`, not `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/\n`FolderNode`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0009",
    "text": "```\nProfile\n  handle:      One, xsd:string           -- stable, addressable slug (\"default\", \"will\", \"claude-agent-1\");\n                                          -- named `handle`, not `id` — every node already has an opaque\n                                          -- intrinsic id (`ApeironInstance.id`), and this is a second,\n                                          -- human-chosen identifier, not a replacement for it (§11)\n  name:        Optional xsd:string       -- display/social name\n  kind:        Optional xsd:string       -- open label, suggested {\"human\",\"agent\"}; no auth (§7)\n  preferences: Set, embed -> StringProp  -- arbitrary key/value bag, same shape as BlockNode.props (§11)\n\nTreeView\n  profile: One, reference -> Profile\n  name:    One, xsd:string               -- stable, globally-unique addressable handle (§11); tightened\n                                          -- from Optional once every TreeView is always created with\n                                          -- one, via `kg:profile create-view` or the \"default\" bootstrap\n  unfolds: Set, reference -> (TreeNode | Link)\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0008",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000A:links:Link:00CBFDZ28G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000A:links:Link:00CBFDZ28G000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ28G001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "722"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000A",
    "text": "`TreeNode` here is the existing class (`node.ts`) — `BlockNode | ArtifactNode | FolderNode` — and\n`Link` is the existing embedded `{ target, predicate }` subdocument (`BaseNode.links`). **There is\nno separate \"view-time\" node type.** A `TreeView` is a lens over the one real graph — a set of\nwhich edges out of an already-visible node are currently revealed — not a parallel structure that\ngets its own copy of nodes. Rendering walks the real `TreeNode`/`Link` graph directly, consulting a\n`TreeView`'s `unfolds` membership at each hop to decide whether to keep descending. (An earlier\ndraft of this doc proposed a distinct view-time `TreeNode` to handle a node reached by more than\none path — unnecessary, retracted; see [§6](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers]]>).)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000B:links:Link:00CBFDZ290000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W002A",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000B:links:Link:00CBFDZ290000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ290001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "600"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000B",
    "text": "`Profile > TreeView` is one-to-many, stored as the reference living on `TreeView` (`profile`), not\na `treeViews` list on `Profile` — the reverse direction is a backlink query (`node.ts`'s existing\n`backlinks(store, id, 'profile')` pattern), same as every other one-to-many relationship in this\ngraph already resolves (a `BlockNode`'s children are found by reverse-querying `__parent`, not\nstored forward). No new mechanism needed — including for a future non-`TreeView` view kind (a\n\"linear view,\" say): the same backlink resolves it for free as long as it also stores a forward\n`profile` reference ([§11](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/11. `Profile` becomes real identity — `kg:profile`]]>)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C:links:Link:00CBFDZ29R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C:links:Link:00CBFDZ29R000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ29R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "17"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C:links:Link:00CBFDZ29R002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C:links:Link:00CBFDZ29R002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ29R003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "157"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 4. Worked example",
    "text": "The sample graph [§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>)/[§6](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers]]>)'s rules are checked against. Labels are shorthand for real node ids (`1.2`\nstands in for whatever real id sits at that structural position) — a concrete stand-in, not a\nliteral addressing scheme. The ground-truth graph below is deliberately not fully explored by the\nunfold sequence — `3` has children and a link of its own that the view never touches, exactly as a\nreal graph would: unfolding only ever reveals what's been explicitly asked for, never the full\nstructure around it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0010",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0013",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0014",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0015"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000D",
    "text": "**Sample graph**: three top-level `TreeNode`s under `Root`, each with structural children, plus a\nhandful of `Link`s crossing between them:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000E",
    "text": "```\nRoot\n├─ 1\n│  ├─ 1.1\n│  ├─ 1.2\n│  │  ├─ 1.2.1\n│  │  ├─ 1.2.2\n│  │  ├─ l1 → 2.1\n│  │  └─ l2 → 3.1\n│  ├─ 1.3\n│  ├─ l1 → 2.2\n│  └─ l2 → 3.3\n├─ 2\n│  ├─ 2.1\n│  ├─ 2.2\n│  │  ├─ 2.2.1\n│  │  ├─ 2.2.2\n│  │  └─ l1 → 1.2\n│  ├─ l1 → 1.2\n│  └─ l2 → 3.3\n└─ 3\n   ├─ 3.1\n   ├─ 3.2\n   ├─ 3.3\n   │  ├─ 3.3.1\n   │  │  ├─ 3.3.1.1\n   │  │  └─ 3.3.1.2\n   │  └─ 3.3.2\n   └─ l1 → 1.1\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F:links:Link:00CBFDZ2A8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F:links:Link:00CBFDZ2A8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2A8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "189"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ1Y0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ1Y0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "text": "**Unfold sequence** (`Root` is always unfolded — listing `1`/`2`/`3` needs no action; every other\nline is one `kg:unfold <ref> --view <this>` call, each adding *only* the stated `ref`, per [§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>)):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000P"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000G",
    "text": "`kg:unfold 1` → `1` becomes `{.1, .2, .3, l1→2.2, l2→3.3}` (its children and its links, both\n   listed).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000H",
    "text": "`kg:unfold 1.2` → `1.2` becomes `{.1, .2, l1→2.1, l2→3.1}`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000J",
    "text": "`kg:unfold` the `Link` `1.l1` (→ `2.2`) → target shown fully: `2.2{.1, .2, l1→1.2}`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000K",
    "text": "`kg:unfold` the `Link` `2.2.l1` (→ `1.2`) → target shown fully: `1.2{.1, .2, l1→2.1, l2→3.1}`\n   (already true from step 2 — this unfolds a *different* `Link` that also reaches the same node).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000M",
    "text": "`kg:unfold 2` → `2` becomes `{.1, .2, l1→1.2, l2→3.3}`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000N",
    "text": "`kg:unfold 2.2` → `2.2` becomes `{.1, .2, l1→1.2}`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000P",
    "text": "`kg:unfold 3.3.1` directly — not reached through `3 > 3.3 > 3.3.1` — → `3.3.1` becomes\n   `{.1, .2}`. Nothing else under `3` (`3.1`, `3.2`, `3.3.2`, `3`'s own link `l1→1.1`) was ever\n   asked for, so none of it appears below.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000Q",
    "text": "**Resulting tree** (`kg:tree --view <this>`):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000R",
    "text": "```\n├─ 1{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 1.1{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 1.2{title,abstract} [*]\n│  │  ├─ 1.2.1{title,abstract}\n│  │  ├─ 1.2.2{title,abstract}\n│  │  ├─ 1.2.l1→2.1{title,abstract}\n│  │  └─ 1.2.l2→3.1{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 1.3{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 1.l1→2.2{title}  [*see 2/2.2]\n│  └─ 1.l2→3.3{title,abstract}\n├─ 2{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 2.1{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 2.2{title,abstract} [*]\n│  │  ├─ 2.2.1{title,abstract}\n│  │  ├─ 2.2.2{title,abstract}\n│  │  └─ 2.2.l1→1.2{title}  [*see 1/1.2]\n│  ├─ 2.l1→1.2{title,abstract}\n│  └─ 2.l2→3.3{title,abstract}\n└─ 3{title,abstract}                    — Root-child preview (unconditional, §5)\n   └─ 3.3{title}                        — breadcrumb: 3.3 itself has no `unfolds` entry\n      └─ 3.3.1{title,abstract}\n         ├─ 3.3.1.1{title,abstract}\n         └─ 3.3.1.2{title,abstract}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
