        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000S",
    "text": "Reading this against [§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>)/[§6](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers]]>)'s rules:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000Y",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000Z"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000T:links:Link:00CBFDZ2C0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000T:links:Link:00CBFDZ2C0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2C0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "155"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000T",
    "text": "`1.2` gets `[*]` at its structural home — qualifying: visible under unfolded `1`, and separately\n  unfolded itself (step 2) — the *canonical* full render ([§6](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers]]>)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000V:links:Link:00CBFDZ2CR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000V:links:Link:00CBFDZ2CR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2CR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "101"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000V:links:Link:00CBFDZ2CR002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000V:links:Link:00CBFDZ2CR002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2D0000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "345"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000V",
    "text": "`2.2.l1→1.2` is a `Link`, itself unfolded (step 4), which would normally mean \"show target\n  fully\" ([§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>)'s rule b) — but `1.2` already has a qualifying home, so this position collapses to a\n  pointer instead ([§6](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers]]>)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000W:links:Link:00CBFDZ2D8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000W:links:Link:00CBFDZ2D8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2DG000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "81"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000W",
    "text": "`1.l2→3.3` is a plain link entry, listed only because its owner `1` is unfolded ([§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>)'s rule a) —\n  never itself separately unfolded — so it gets the ordinary preview tier, `{title,abstract}`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000X",
    "text": "`3` gets `{title,abstract}` — *not* title-only — because it's a direct child of `Root`, which\n  always earns the preview tier regardless of whether `3` is itself in `unfolds` (it isn't). It\n  additionally lists exactly one child, `3.3`, purely because something further down is unfolded —\n  not `3.1`/`3.2`, and not `3`'s own link `l1→1.1` (links are only ever listed by a node that's\n  genuinely *in* `unfolds`, which `3` is not).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000Y",
    "text": "`3.3`, one level deeper, gets `{title}` only — its own immediate parent (`3`) doesn't itself\n  qualify as genuinely unfolded (it's only a preview), so `3.3` doesn't inherit `3`'s preview\n  status the way a plain child of a *genuinely* unfolded node would. It lists exactly one child,\n  `3.3.1` — not its sibling `3.3.2` — for the same \"only the relevant path\" reason. This is the\n  concrete case answering the previous draft's open question about whether an interior breadcrumb\n  node prunes down to just the relevant path or lists everything the way `Root` does: it prunes —\n  `3.3.2` never appears anywhere in this tree.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W000Z",
    "text": "`3.3.1` is genuinely unfolded (step 7) — full tier, regardless of the breadcrumb chain above it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0010:links:Link:00CBFDZ2DR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0010:links:Link:00CBFDZ2DR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2DR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "47"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0010:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ1Z0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0010:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ1Z0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0010",
    "text": "**Fold sequence**, applied to the state above ([§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>)):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0011",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0012"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0011",
    "text": "`kg:fold 1` — removes `1`'s own entry, cascading to remove everything under it that was *also*\n  separately unfolded: `1.2` and the `Link` `1.l1→2.2`. (`1.2`'s own children/links were never\n  separately unfolded, so nothing further cascades from there.)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0012",
    "text": "`kg:fold 3` — cascades all the way down through `3`'s structural subtree (`3` itself was never\n  in `unfolds`, `3.3` wasn't either — the cascade walks *through* both, since neither has to be\n  in `unfolds` itself to be on the path to something that is) to remove `3.3.1`'s entry.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0013",
    "text": "**Resulting tree**:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0014",
    "text": "```\n├─ 1{title,abstract}\n├─ 2{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 2.1{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 2.2{title,abstract}               — no [*]: 1.2's home no longer qualifies\n│  │  ├─ 2.2.1{title,abstract}\n│  │  ├─ 2.2.2{title,abstract}\n│  │  └─ 2.2.l1→1.2{title,abstract}     — now the only qualifying position; renders fully\n│  │     ├─ 1.2.1{title,abstract}\n│  │     ├─ 1.2.2{title,abstract}\n│  │     ├─ 1.2.l1→2.1{title,abstract}\n│  │     └─ 1.2.l2→3.1{title,abstract}\n│  ├─ 2.l1→1.2{title,abstract}\n│  └─ 2.l2→3.3{title,abstract}\n└─ 3{title,abstract}                    — still a Root-child preview; no children listed now,\n                                            since nothing beneath it is unfolded anymore\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0015:links:Link:00CBFDZ2E8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0015:links:Link:00CBFDZ2E8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2EG000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "238"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0015",
    "text": "`2.2.l1→1.2` flips from a pointer to a full render the moment `1.2`'s home stops qualifying —\nthe canonical-position decision is recomputed fresh at render time from whatever's currently in\n`unfolds`, never cached from an earlier render ([§6](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers]]>)). And `3` shows `{title,abstract}` in *both*\nthe pre-fold and post-fold trees — an earlier draft of this doc had it flip from title-only\npre-fold to title+abstract post-fold, which was simply inconsistent: a `Root`-child's own-line\ndetail doesn't depend on whether it's currently breadcrumbing toward something unfolded.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref",
    "text": "`TreeOptions` (`tree.ts`) and `kg:tree`'s CLI drop `unfoldedMode`/`--unfolded` as a bare boolean;\n`--view <ref>` takes its place. Supplying a view drives unfolded-mode rendering keyed off that\nview's `unfolds` set; omitting it keeps today's other default (title-only, no expand/collapse\nsimulation) unchanged.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0017",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001G"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0017",
    "text": "**Correction to an earlier draft of this doc**: `kg:unfold <ref> --view <ref>` adds *only* the one\nspecified `ref` (a `TreeNode` xor a `Link`) to the view's `unfolds` set — matching the current\n`setUnfolded(store, id, value)` implementation exactly, not the \"add every child and every link\ntoo\" behavior a previous draft wrongly proposed. `<ref>` for a `TreeNode` resolves the normal way\n(id or path); for a `Link`, only a bare id (snowflake code) is accepted — a `Link` has no `path`\nfield and no natural slug of its own to build one from.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018:links:Link:00CBFDZ2M8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018:links:Link:00CBFDZ2M8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2M8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "267"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ1ZR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ1ZR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0018",
    "text": "What actually gets *shown* at render time follows from whatever's in the set — not from anything\n`kg:unfold` writes beyond that one entry. A `TreeNode`'s own-line detail has three tiers; whether\nit lists any children at all is a separate question layered on top (see [§4](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/4. Worked example]]>) for each in context):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0019",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0019",
    "text": "**Genuinely unfolded (in `unfolds`)** — own line `{title, abstract}`; lists *both* its structural\n  children and its own `links` (rule a), each recursively at whichever tier applies to it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001A:links:Link:00CBFDZ2MR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001A:links:Link:00CBFDZ2MR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2MR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "534"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001A",
    "text": "**Not itself unfolded, but its immediate parent is genuinely unfolded — or it's a direct child of\n  `Root`** (`Root` always counts here without needing an `unfolds` entry of its own — it's the one\n  always-qualifying \"parent\") — own line `{title, abstract}` (preview). Lists nothing further by\n  default. **Exception**: if something below it is unfolded (reachable only by descending through\n  it), it additionally lists just the *one* child continuing toward that — pruning every other,\n  irrelevant child. This is what makes `3` in [§4](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/4. Worked example]]>)'s example show `{title,abstract}` plus exactly one\n  child (`3.3`), never `3.1`/`3.2` and never its own link `l1→1.1`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001B:links:Link:00CBFDZ2N0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001B:links:Link:00CBFDZ2N0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2N8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "274"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001B",
    "text": "**Neither of the above** — its own parent doesn't qualify either (it's itself only reached as a\n  breadcrumb link in someone else's chain) — own line `{title}` only, no abstract. Same\n  passthrough-only-child behavior as the tier above, minus the abstract. This is `3.3` in [§4](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/4. Worked example]]>)'s\n  example: `3`'s parent-qualification doesn't propagate down to it, since `3` itself is only at\n  preview tier, not genuinely unfolded — and `3.3` in turn shows only its one relevant child\n  (`3.3.1`), never `3.3.2`. That's the concrete answer to whether an interior breadcrumb node\n  prunes to just the relevant path or lists everything the way `Root`'s children do: it prunes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0018",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001C:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ200000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001C:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ200001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001C",
    "text": "A `Link`'s own rendering is a separate, one-hop question, layered onto whichever `TreeNode` owns\nit, governed by two named rules (only ever invoked for a `Link` whose owner is itself genuinely\nunfolded — a `Link` belonging to a non-unfolded owner is never listed at all, by either rule):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001E"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001D",
    "text": "**Rule a** — the `Link` is merely *listed*, because its owner is genuinely unfolded, but the\n  `Link` itself has no `unfolds` entry of its own: shows the target's `{title, abstract}` as a\n  flat, one-line preview, never recursing further, regardless of where the target sits.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001E",
    "text": "**Rule b** — the `Link` itself is genuinely unfolded (has its own `unfolds` entry): shows its\n  target *fully*, one level, as if the target were itself in the tier-1 case above — recursing\n  into the target's own structural children and its own `links`, each at whichever tier applies.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001F:links:Link:00CBFDZ2NG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001F:links:Link:00CBFDZ2NG000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2NG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "345"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001F",
    "text": "This is computed fresh at render time from the current `unfolds` set and the graph's own structure\n— nothing about it is stored, which is also what resolves the previous draft's open \"should\nbreadcrumb auto-expansion mutate `unfolds`, permissive or strict\" question: it doesn't mutate\nanything either way, so there's no such choice to make. See [§6](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers]]>) for how a target reached two\ndifferent ways picks which tier wins where.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001G",
    "text": "`kg:fold <ref> --view <ref>` removes `<ref>`'s own `unfolds` entry, and cascades: recursively\nremoves the `unfolds` entry of anything reached *from* `<ref>` (structural children, then `<ref>`'s\nown links, same order as rendering) that also has its own explicit entry. Anything outside\n`<ref>`'s own subtree that happens to reach into it via a separate, unrelated `Link` is untouched —\nfolding one path to a node doesn't fold every path to it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Two paths to the same node: one canonical, the rest become pointers",
    "text": "Your original framing: for structural `children` the graph is a strict tree — one path from root to\nany node — so reaching a deep target just means the breadcrumb down to it is already well-defined.\nThe complication is a `Link` creating a second path to something also reached structurally (or via\nanother `Link`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001S"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001J:links:Link:00CBFDZ2P0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001J:links:Link:00CBFDZ2P0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2P0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "23"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001J",
    "text": "Worked through against [§4](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/4. Worked example]]>)'s example line by line, the rule turns out simpler than \"whichever gets\nvisited first in the render walk\":",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001K:links:Link:00CBFDZ2PR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001K:links:Link:00CBFDZ2PR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2PR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "449"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001K:links:Link:00CBFDZ2PR002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001K:links:Link:00CBFDZ2PR002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2PR003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1279"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001K",
    "text": "**A node's structural (`parent`-chain) position is its one true home.** The home wins — is\n*always* the canonical full expansion, no matter what — whenever it actually appears anywhere in\nthis rendered view, for *any* reason: because it's genuinely unfolded itself, because its immediate\nparent is (making it a listed preview child), or merely because it sits on the breadcrumb\npassthrough path down to something else genuinely unfolded beneath it ([§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>)'s passthrough-child\nexception). The home's own line doesn't need to earn a `{title,abstract}` tier to still be the\ncanonical position — even a bare title-only breadcrumb outranks a competing `Link`, as long as that\nline is actually printed somewhere in the tree. Any `Link` elsewhere pointing at the same node —\neven one that's itself in `unfolds`, which would otherwise mean \"show the target fully\" (rule b) —\ncollapses instead to a short pointer back to the home, tagged `[*see <path>]`, and doesn't recurse.\nThe home position itself gets tagged `[*]` — \"reachable more than one way in this view,\" regardless\nof whichever display tier its own line happens to earn. Matches `1.2`/`2.2` in [§4](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/4. Worked example]]>)'s example\n(both qualify under the narrower original reading too) *and* extends to a node reached only as a\nbreadcrumb (§4's `3.3`-shaped case) that also happens to be a `Link`'s target: the breadcrumb still\nwins, since it's already printed there for its own, unrelated reason — a `Link` recursing fully on\ntop of that would just duplicate content the breadcrumb's own passthrough chain already shows\nbeneath it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001M",
    "text": "**Loosened from an earlier, narrower version of this rule**, which required the home to separately\nhave its *own* `unfolds` entry *and* a genuinely-unfolded parent — real gap, found live: a `Link`\ntargeting a pure breadcrumb node (neither it nor its parent genuinely unfolded) several levels above\nsomething else that *is* genuinely unfolded. The narrower rule let the `Link` \"win\" there and fully\nre-expand a subtree the breadcrumb's own passthrough chain was *already* rendering underneath it —\nsame content twice, once via each position, not the clean \"one canonical, one pointer\" split this\nsection promises. The home was always going to be printed regardless of the `Link` existing at all;\ngiving the `Link` a competing full expansion never added anything but a duplicate.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001N:links:Link:00CBFDZ2Q8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W000C",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001N:links:Link:00CBFDZ2Q8000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2Q8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "411"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001N",
    "text": "**If the home has no reason to appear in this render at all** — nothing beneath it is genuinely\nunfolded, its own parent isn't genuinely unfolded, and it isn't itself genuinely unfolded —\nwhichever `Link`(s) pointing at it *are* themselves in `unfolds` get to show the target fully,\ndirectly at their own position, since there's truly no competing position anywhere else in the\nview. Exactly what happens after [§4](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/4. Worked example]]>)'s `kg:fold 1`: `1.2`'s own entry is gone and its home (under `1`)\nno longer lists anything, nor is `1.2` reachable as a breadcrumb toward anything else, so\n`2.2.l1->1.2` — untouched by folding `1` — becomes the *only* position with any claim on it and\nrenders fully there instead of pointing anywhere.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001P",
    "text": "This also makes link-following cycle-safe for free: a `Link` pointing back toward an ancestor (or\ntoward anything else already canonical elsewhere in this same render) hits the \"already canonical\nelsewhere — point back\" branch before it can recurse.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001Q",
    "text": "**If a node has no home rendered anywhere in this view and is reached by two or more different\nunfolded `Link`s at once** — both are equally \"second pass\" (neither is the structural home), so\nthere's no meaningful ordering between them to break the tie with. Whichever one the walk happens to\nencounter first becomes canonical; that's purely a function of implementation-internal iteration\norder, not a structural or positional rule worth defining — a real tie, not a case that needs a\nprincipled answer.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001R",
    "text": "**Real implementation consequence**: `renderTreeLines`'s current shape is a single top-down\nrecursive pass, emitting each line as it's visited. Deciding \"does this position get the canonical\nfull render or a pointer\" requires knowing, for the whole view, which nodes have a qualifying home\nand which don't — derivable from the `unfolds` set and the graph structure alone, *before* any line\nis emitted. A first pass that computes that (a map from node id → its canonical position, if any)\nfollowed by a second pass that emits lines using it is the natural shape; not a one-line addition\nto the existing function.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001S",
    "text": "**Line format** — implemented as proposed (`node.ts`'s `renderViewLines`/`renderLinkLine`): `[*]`\nfollows the existing `(holder)` tag's pattern, appended at the line's end rather than sitting next\nto the `[kind]` bracket (`displayLabel`) where it'd risk reading as part of it; a pointer line keeps\nthe normal `id  [kind]  title` prefix (still identifiable/greppable) with the target's path\nappended instead of descending, reusing `TreeNode.toPath()` when the canonical position is a home,\nor `<linkId> (link)` when it's another `Link` with no qualifying home — e.g.\n`BlockNode/xyz  [heading]  Some Title  [*see /reportX/sectionY]`. No space between `*` and `see`,\nand the path itself uses the real `/`-separated form (`toPath()`'s own output), never the informal\n`>` shorthand this doc's own worked-example diagrams once used.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001T:links:Link:00CBFDZ2QR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W002A",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001T:links:Link:00CBFDZ2QR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2R0000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "29"
          },
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001T:links:Link:00CBFDZ2QR000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2R0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "434"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 7. `Profile`: lightweight identity, still no auth",
    "text": "Confirmed scope, extended in [§11](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/11. `Profile` becomes real identity — `kg:profile`]]>): `Profile` started as a way to keep separate `TreeView`s from\ncolliding — \"human\" vs. \"agent,\" or finer-grained if that turns out to matter (per-agent-session,\nsay). It has since grown a small amount of real, addressable identity (`handle`/`name`/`kind`) and an\nopen preferences bag ([§11](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/11. `Profile` becomes real identity — `kg:profile`]]>)), but the posture hasn't changed: no auth, no permissions, matching every\nother stance this solo-local-dev tool already takes (the ApeironNgn service itself assumes one\ntrusted local caller). `kind` values like `\"human\"`/`\"agent\"` are a suggested starting vocabulary,\nnot an enforced enum — the same unenforced-label philosophy the original single `name` field\ncarried, now split across `name` (display) and `kind` (category).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V:links:Link:00CBFDZ2RG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W002A",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V:links:Link:00CBFDZ2RG000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2RG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "232"
          },
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V:links:Link:00CBFDZ2RG000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2RR000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "673"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ218000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ218001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 8. Git separation: a `.state/` subfolder, gitignored",
    "text": "`TreeView` is per-viewer UI state (\"what's currently expanded\") — genuinely ephemeral, churning on\nevery expand/collapse click, with no reason to ever sit in git history. `Profile`, by contrast, is\nstable: identity and preferences ([§11](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/11. `Profile` becomes real identity — `kg:profile`]]>)) that should survive a `git clone` and diff like any other\ntracked file, even though it's still \"per-viewer\" in the sense that each caller owns their own\nrows. The original version of this section conflated those two questions — *whose* is it, versus\n*how long does it live* — because until `Profile` grew real content ([§11](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/11. `Profile` becomes real identity — `kg:profile`]]>)), everything \"per-viewer\"\nalso happened to be ephemeral. Splitting them apart:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001Y",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001Z",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0020",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0021",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0022"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001W",
    "text": "**`TreeView`** stays in `dehydrate.ts`'s `STATE_CLASSES`, written to the gitignored\n  `AperasKG/Apeiron/.state/TreeView.jsonld` by `dehydrateStateToJsonLd`, on its own\n  `STATE_FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS`/`stateDirty` cadence — everything else in this section describes only\n  `TreeView` now.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001X",
    "text": "**`Profile`** moved to `dehydrate.ts`'s `DEHYDRATE_CLASSES`, written to\n  `AperasKG/Apeiron/Profile.jsonld` alongside `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`, on the\n  ordinary `FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS`/`dirty` cadence — tracked by default like every other content-mirror\n  file. Whether a given repo actually commits it is a decision for *that repo's own* `.gitignore`,\n  not something ApeironNgn's code enforces: an org mirror likely wants its members' `Profile.jsonld`\n  tracked; a personal fork might ignore it on branches meant to be merged upstream while tracking it\n  on personal branches. **Not** auto-staged by `AperasKG/.githooks/pre-commit`, though — that hook\n  only runs (and only ever stages `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.jsonld`) because its one\n  job is reflecting `kg:track`'s own rewrite of *those* files into the same commit as the `.md`\n  change that triggered it; `kg:track`/`kg:ingest` never touch `Profile` at all, so there's nothing\n  for that hook to pick up. Committing a `Profile.jsonld` change (from `kg:profile` or a direct\n  edit) is a manual `git add`, same as any other hand-edited settings file — consistent with, not\n  an exception to, the \"settings file\" framing above.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001Y",
    "text": "Implemented (for `TreeView`): `dehydrateToJsonLd`'s existing `${kind}.jsonld` pattern\n(`dehydrate.ts`), reused for a second, separate pass — `dehydrateStateToJsonLd(store, dir =\njoin(getApeironExportDir(), '.state'))` — reusing the same `serializeDoc`/`allIdsOfKind`/`stableId`\nhelpers rather than a parallel implementation. `store.ts`'s `rehydrateStore` reads `.state/\nTreeView.jsonld` as a second, tolerant pass (missing `.state/` entirely — a fresh checkout, or\nbefore any view has ever been unfolded — is not an error, unlike a missing content-mirror file).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W001Z",
    "text": "`AperasKG/Apeiron/` lives in the **AperasKG repo**, a sibling of this one, reached from here only\nvia the `AperasKG` symlink (confirmed: `git -C AperasKG rev-parse --show-toplevel` resolves to\n`/home/dinhlx/source/AperasKG`) — so the gitignore entry (`Apeiron/.state/`, now covering only\n`TreeView.jsonld`) belongs in *that* repo's own `.gitignore`, alongside its existing\n`*.bundle`/`*.tar.gz`/`snapshots/` entries, not this repo's `web/.gitignore` (which only covers\n`.run/`, this repo's own analogous local-state precedent, for a different reason — the ApeironNgn\nservice's lock/socket files).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0020",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0020:links:Link:00CBFDZ2S0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDYXMR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0020:links:Link:00CBFDZ2S0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2S0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "115"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0020",
    "text": "The shared service isn't hypothetical — `web/src/lib/apeironNgn/service.ts` is a complete, running\nimplementation ([Aperas-apeironngn-design.md §4 step 5](<[[/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md/# ApeironNgn: Embedded Substrate Design/4. Rollout sequence/Step 5: shared service process — implemented, verified]]>), whose own \"not started\" line has been\nfixed to match). This design landed as edits to that real file, not a new one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0021",
    "text": "**Flush cadence: two separate, independently-adjustable intervals — implemented.**\n`STATE_FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS` and a `stateDirty` flag sit alongside the pre-existing\n`FLUSH_INTERVAL_MS`/`dirty`, with their own `setInterval` calling `dehydrateStateToJsonLd` instead\nof `dehydrateToJsonLd` — the exact same pattern `service.ts` already used for the content mirror,\nduplicated rather than parameterized, so each interval stays independently tunable (`.state/`\nchurns on every expand/collapse click, tuned tighter than the 10s content default; being\ngitignored and cheap to rewrite, there's no reason to tie the two together).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0022:links:Link:00CBFDZ2T0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0024",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0022:links:Link:00CBFDZ2T0000:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2T0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "410"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0022:links:Link:00CBFDZ2T0002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0016",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0022:links:Link:00CBFDZ2T0002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ2T0003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "621"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ1W0022",
    "text": "**Consequence for the `'unfold'`/`'fold'` handlers — implemented.** Both now set `stateDirty`, not\n`dirty` (they mutate `TreeView.unfolds`, never `BlockNode`/`ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`), and their\nrequest shapes (`ServiceRequest`'s `unfold`/`fold`/`tree` variants, `serviceProtocol.ts`) carry a\n`viewRef` field alongside the existing `ref`/`flush` fields — omitted, it resolves to the\n`\"default\"`-named view ([§10](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/10. Open questions — all resolved]]>)) on `unfold`/`fold`; `kg:tree` instead keeps its plain no-view default\nwhen `viewRef` is omitted, matching [§5](<[[/Aperas-treeview-design.md/# TreeView & Profile — Design/5. Rendering: three display tiers; `kg:unfold` touches exactly one ref]]>).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W001V",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 9. Migrating existing `unfolded` data — checked, nothing to migrate",
    "text": "Checked live against the real mirror: `AperasKG/Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld` has 1220 `\"unfolded\":\nfalse` entries and zero `true`; `ArtifactNode.jsonld` has 1, also `false`. Nothing has ever\nactually set it. `unfolded` can be dropped from `BASE_NODE_SHAPE`/`BaseNode` outright — no\nmigration, no default-view seeding needed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ1W0024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 10. Open questions — all resolved",
