    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ41R02R",
    "text": "Remains an emerging bleeding-edge capability explored in moldable development and project lenses.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02S:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ4W0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02S:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ4W0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ41R02S",
    "text": "Open challenges on the bleeding edge",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R01Z",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02W"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ41R02T",
    "text": "Lossless round-trip synchronization struggles to parse human edits on high-level summaries back into exact graph diffs without dropping unrendered metadata.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ41R02V",
    "text": "Granularity calibration frequently pulls too much AST-level noise or too little contextual rationale during subgraph extraction.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ41R02W",
    "text": "Human–agent advisory loops shift away from autonomous destructive overwrites toward advisory invalidation, generating visual diffs and triage queues for human verification.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ41R02S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Agentic Query Tools — Design",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW95R000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510017",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51001C"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 0. What this is, and what it supersedes",
    "text": "`Aperas-agentic-bfs-projection-design.md` conflated two different traversal shapes under one\nwrong name — a depth-bounded recursive walk it called \"BFS Traversal\" is actually depth-first\n(IDDFS-shaped, once bounded), not breadth-first. This doc replaces it: three distinct tools,\ncorrectly named, plus a third capability (keyword/regex search) every earlier revision flagged\nas missing but never built. Together they answer \"what does an agent or human have to find a\nnode or explore the graph,\" per `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A's Agentic Interface\nconcept and the gap first identified in `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md` §2.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Three distinct tools, not one",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ518000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ518001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ510004",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510003",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510005",
    "text": "**`kg:tree`** — a deep structural map: recursively lists nodes across many levels, optionally\n  depth-bounded. Algorithmically a depth-first walk with an optional depth cutoff —\n  IDDFS-shaped, not BFS. Good for getting oriented across a large chunk of the KG at a glance.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510006",
    "text": "**`kg:unfold`** (and its inverse, `kg:fold`) — true breadth-first: given one node, show its\n  own `title` plus the full `text` of each of its immediate children (first level only) — no\n  recursion, no depth param, since \"first level\" is the whole point. Named `fold`/`unfold` (not\n  `collapse`/`expand`) to match the ontology's own existing vocabulary — see §3, this is also\n  the first thing to ever give the long-inert `unfolded` schema field (§4: promoted from\n  `BlockNode` to `BaseNode`) a real effect.\n  This is the real analog of `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A mode 3's \"BFS tool calls to\n  expand subtrees\": a single frontier-expansion step an agent calls repeatedly, choosing which\n  child to unfold next based on what it just read.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510007",
    "text": "**`kg:search`** — keyword/regex content search, matched server-side via WOQL. Not structural\n  at all — finds nodes by matching a pattern against content, regardless of position in the\n  tree. Closes the gap every earlier revision of this design named and left open.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ51G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ51G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 2. A real implementation gap this surfaced, fixed directly in `astParser.ts`",
    "text": "Designing `kg:unfold` (§3) exposed a missed gap in already-shipped ingestion behavior, not just\na display-layer choice: `heading` and `blockquote`/`listItem` were all being treated as\nequally content-less containers (`text = \"\"`), but they aren't equally opaque —",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000E"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510009",
    "text": "A `heading`'s real content lives in its children, but per the folding philosophy (\"a block's\n  children fold into its text (abstract)\"), it should still carry an abstract of its own, the\n  same way `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` already do via `extractAbstract`. It didn't — it was\n  hardcoded to `\"\"` regardless.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000A",
    "text": "A `listItem` is a real semantic unit (like a heading's section, just one level down), so it\n  deserves the same treatment — also missing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000B",
    "text": "A `list` genuinely has no content of its own — its children (`listItem`s) are the actual\n  content units. It's the *only* true content-less container.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000C",
    "text": "A `blockquote` is a quote, not a summary — its full content should be projected as-is, not\n  reduced to an abstract or emptied out.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000D",
    "text": "Fixed in `astParser.ts`'s `convertAstNode`: `heading`/`listItem` now compute `text` via\n`extractAbstract` once their `children` exist (the first paragraph found among descendants,\ncascading correctly through nesting — confirmed live: a `Sub` heading two levels down correctly\npicked up its own nearest paragraph, not an ancestor's); `list` still empties `text`; `blockquote`\nnow falls through to the ordinary leaf default (`text = rawText`, its full raw slice, quote\nmarkers included) instead of being emptied. Verified this doesn't disturb reconciliation:\n`reconcile.ts` keeps its own independent `LEAF_TYPES`/`CONTAINER_TYPES` (type-based, not\ntext-based) for Stage A/B matching, so none of this changes which nodes match how — confirmed by\nre-running `verify:phase0 -- --db` clean, both in-memory and against the live DB.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000E",
    "text": "**Known limitation surfaced by this fix**: `kg:track`/`kg:ingest` skip re-ingestion based on file\ncontent hash — a parser-logic change like this one doesn't change any file's bytes, so already-\ningested real artifacts keep their old (empty) heading/listItem `text` until something forces\nre-ingestion. Confirmed live: re-running `kg:track`/`kg:ingest` after this fix touched zero\nartifacts. Not fixed here — a real, pre-existing gap (parser version isn't part of the\nchange-detection key), out of scope for this design.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. `kg:tree` — render rule: `title`, exclusively",
    "text": "An earlier revision proposed falling back to a text excerpt when `title` isn't meaningful.\nRejected: a `BlockNode`'s `title` literally *is* its own `blockId` whenever nothing has\nsummarized it yet (`astParser.ts:76`) — and that **is** the meaningful signal, not a gap to\npaper over. Seeing a raw id as a node's title tells the reader \"this node is opaque — nothing\nhas read and summarized me\" (Fractal Ontology Enhancements' AI-summarization step, still\nunbuilt per `Aperas-dev-status.md`). Extracting a text excerpt instead would hide that signal\nbehind a false sense of readability. So: print `title` uniformly for every node kind, no\nfallback branching, no special-casing for containers either — `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode`\ntitles are always meaningful (dirname/filename) anyway, so the rule is genuinely uniform.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ528000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ528001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000G",
    "text": "Command: `kg:tree [path] [--depth N]`",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000F",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000N"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000H",
    "text": "`path` optional, defaults to `.` (the artifacts-root `FolderNode`) — the full tree from\n  folders.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000J",
    "text": "Same addressing as the assertion CLI: a full node id or a bare artifact/folder path, resolved\n  via the existing `resolveNodeRef` (direct-or-query, `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`\n  §2).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000K",
    "text": "No depth limit by default — genuinely the full tree. `--depth N` truncates to N levels,\n  printing a `…` marker at each truncation point rather than silently stopping, so it's visibly\n  a limit, not the actual leaf.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000M",
    "text": "One recursive walk handles every level uniformly — `FolderNode` (nested `FolderNode`s,\n  `ArtifactNode` references, README-derived `BlockNode` children) and `ArtifactNode` →\n  `BlockNode` tree alike; the fractal ontology means no node kind needs special-casing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000N",
    "text": "Output: one line per node, indented by depth: `<id>  [<kind>]  <title>` — `<kind>` is\n  `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` for those, or the `BlockNode`'s own mdast `type` for blocks.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ530000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ530001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 4. `kg:unfold` / `kg:fold` — one breadth-first step, persisted",
    "text": "Command: `kg:unfold <path>`",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000Y"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000Q",
    "text": "`path`: same direct-or-query addressing as everywhere else (full id or artifact/folder path).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000R",
    "text": "No depth, no recursion by design — resolves to one node, then prints its own\n  `<id>  [<kind>]  <title>` **plus**, for each of its immediate children,\n  `<id>  [<kind>]  <full text>`. Full `text` *in addition to* title, not instead of it — an\n  earlier revision of this doc worded it as \"full text, not title,\" which wrongly implied\n  dropping the title; the point was only to emphasize that children get their full content\n  here, unlike `kg:tree`'s title-only listing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000S",
    "text": "Now that §2's fix gives `heading`/`listItem` a real abstract, only a `list` child (the one\n  true content-less container per §2) ever has nothing to show — it prints\n  `<id>  [<kind>]  (no text of its own — see kg:unfold <id>)`. Every other child kind now has\n  something real to display.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000T",
    "text": "**Persists**, not just displays: also sets `unfolded = true` on the target node — the first\n  code path to ever write `true` to that field (it's existed since early in the schema,\n  defaulted `false` on creation, carried forward by reconciliation, but never meaningfully\n  written or read — confirmed live by grepping the codebase). Implementation note: a node's\n  `children` (or, for `ArtifactNode`, its singular `root`) is a required field, so the write has\n  to resubmit the full existing document with only `unfolded` changed (fetch-then-resubmit), the\n  same pattern `tombstoneSubtree` (`reconcile.ts`) already uses for a different field.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000V",
    "text": "**`unfolded` is a `BaseNode` field, not a `BlockNode`-only one** — corrected from an earlier\n  version of this design that scoped it to `BlockNode`. That scoping was a loophole, not a\n  deliberate choice: `unfolded` was designed back when `BlockNode` was the only concrete kind,\n  and simply never got carried onto `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` when the ontology split into\n  three. There's nothing BlockNode-specific about \"has this node's children been revealed\" — an\n  `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` target is just as real a thing to fold or unfold. So `kg:unfold`/\n  `kg:fold` now have genuine, persisted effect against every node kind, not display-only\n  no-ops for two of the three (§4.1 depends on this being uniform).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000W",
    "text": "`kg:fold <path>` is the exact inverse: sets `unfolded = false` on the target node, no content\n  printed beyond a confirmation — there's nothing new to show by re-collapsing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000X",
    "text": "**Composability, corrected**: an earlier revision described `kg:tree` + `kg:unfold` as\n\"composable\" but only demonstrated a *sequence* of separate calls (`tree` → `unfold x` →\n`unfold y` → ...) whose results a reader has to mentally combine — not a single artifact. What\n\"composable\" should actually mean: the *persisted* `unfolded` state left behind by a sequence of\n`kg:fold`/`kg:unfold` calls is a standing, queryable shape — \"a selectively expanded tree\" — not\njust this session's scrollback. §4.1 settles the fork this section used to leave open (should\n`kg:tree` change its default behavior, or should this be a separate command): an opt-in flag on\n`kg:tree` itself, default behavior unchanged.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 4.1. `kg:tree --unfolded` — rendering the combined shape",
    "text": "Decided: an opt-in flag on `kg:tree`, not a new command or a changed default — `kg:tree` without\nthe flag stays exactly the uniform title-only walk of §3; `--unfolded` swaps in a second render\nrule that reads the very state `kg:fold`/`kg:unfold` (§4) persist, so a sequence of those calls\nbecomes visible as one tree instead of separate scrollback.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000P",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000Z",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51000Z",
    "text": "Command: `kg:tree [path] [--depth N] [--no-holders] [--unfolded]` — `--unfolded` composes with\nboth existing flags unchanged (`--depth` still a hard ceiling, `--no-holders` still transparent\nper §7.5 of `Aperas-deep-path-resolution-design.md`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000Y",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ53G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ53R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ510010",
    "text": "**Rule** (applied per node during the same recursive walk, no second pass): a node is rendered\nwith its full `text` instead of `title` exactly when *its own parent* has `unfolded === true` —\ni.e., exactly the set of nodes a real `kg:unfold <parent>` call against that parent would have\nshown. Now that `unfolded` lives on `BaseNode` (§4), this applies uniformly to `BlockNode`,\n`ArtifactNode`, and `FolderNode` alike — no kind gets special-cased. Concretely:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51000Y",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510011",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510012",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510013",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510014",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510015",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510016"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510011",
    "text": "The node `kg:tree` was pointed at (the root of this particular walk) is always rendered\n  title-only, regardless of its own `unfolded` state — same convention `kg:unfold` itself already\n  uses for its own target (§4: the target's line is title, only its children get full text). A\n  tree rooted partway into an already-unfolded chain still opens on a title line.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510012",
    "text": "A node whose own `unfolded` is `true`: its children are rendered full-text (per the rule above)\n  **and** the walk recurses beneath each of them, carrying the same rule forward — a grandchild\n  gets full text too, exactly when *its* parent (this child) is itself unfolded. This is what\n  makes multiple persisted `kg:unfold` calls compose into one shape: unfolding a great-grandchild\n  earlier makes it show up expanded here too, at whatever depth it sits, and unfolding a\n  `FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` reveals the abstracts of the files/subfolders inside it exactly the\n  same way unfolding a `BlockNode` reveals its children's text.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510013",
    "text": "A node whose own `unfolded` is not `true` (folded, including the never-unfolded default): the\n  walk does **not** descend into its children at all — they haven't been revealed, so showing\n  them would assert something no `kg:unfold` call ever established. If it has children, one line\n  is printed in their place: `…  (folded — kg:unfold <id> to expand)` — the same affordance as\n  the existing list-type message in §4, naming the exact command that would reveal them. This is\n  the \"stops at folded ones\" half of the shape, and it now applies at a folder or artifact\n  boundary too, not just inside a block tree.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510014",
    "text": "The one content-less case from §4 (a `list`-type `BlockNode`, the only kind with genuinely\n  nothing of its own) prints the same `(no text of its own — see kg:unfold <id>)` line it does in\n  `kg:unfold`'s own output, rather than an empty string.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510015",
    "text": "`--depth N` is checked first, as an unconditional ceiling — it can still truncate inside an\n  unfolded chain (printing the existing `…` marker), independent of any node's `unfolded` state.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510016",
    "text": "A holder (`Aperas-deep-path-resolution-design.md` §7.5) is rendered by the same rule as any\n  other node — its `(holder)` tag is orthogonal to whether it's shown full-text or title-only, and\n  `--no-holders` keeps hiding its own line while still descending through it exactly as before.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510010",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. `kg:search` — keyword/regex content search",
    "text": "Command: `kg:search <pattern>` — PCRE-style regex, matching `WOQL.re`'s own semantics.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510018"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510018:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ548000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510018:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ548001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ510018",
    "text": "Confirmed live (not just documented): TerminusDB's WOQL builder exposes\n`WOQL.re(pattern, inputVarName, resultVarList)` (`terminusdb` npm package, `lib/woql.js:1262`)\n— genuine server-side regex matching, not a client-side scan. Tested directly against the real\nKG: `WOQL.re('Assertion', titleVar, matchVar)` combined with `WOQL.triple(doc, 'title',\ntitleVar)` correctly matched 3 real `BlockNode` titles containing \"Assertion.\" A pattern with a\nregex metacharacter (`kg:\\w+`) also matched correctly using ordinary single-level JS string\nescaping — the \"escape twice\" note in the client's own docstring turned out to be about\ninternal JSON-LD wire transport, already handled by the client library; callers don't need to\ndouble-escape anything themselves.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510017",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510019",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51001A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51001B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ510019",
    "text": "Searches `title` and `text` across all three node classes (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`,\n  `FolderNode`) — six queries (3 classes × 2 fields), each\n  `WOQL.and(WOQL.triple(v.Doc, field, v.Value), WOQL.re(pattern, v.Value, v.Match))`. A\n  class/field a given document doesn't have (e.g. `Optional text` absent) simply produces no\n  triple to match against — no special-casing needed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510018",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51001A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51001A",
    "text": "`title`/`text` are `xsd:string` literals, so bindings come back wrapped\n  (`{\"@type\", \"@value\"}`), exactly like `predicate` did in `Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`\n  §4 — reuses the existing `unwrapLiteral` (`woql.ts`) rather than re-solving the same problem.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510018",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51001B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ51001B",
    "text": "Output: `<id>  [<kind>]  <field>  <value>` per match, across all six queries, deduplicated by\n  id (a node could match on both `title` and `text`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510018",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ51001C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Phase placement — decided",
    "text": "Confirmed: moved into Phase 0, same reasoning as Artifact Projection and Basic Assertion\nAuthoring (a real capability gap blocking basic KG usability, not contingent on the UI existing\nfirst). `Aperas-design.md`'s roadmap and `Aperas-dev-status.md` updated to match.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ510001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Aperas Substrate Architecture Specification (Phase 0)",
    "text": "Technical reference for the Phase 0 TerminusDB substrate implementation, canonical JSON-LD schema design, AST transduction pipelines, backup mechanisms, and cross-machine synchronization protocols.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW960000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0010"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G002",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. System Paradigm & Repository Layout",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G008"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Architectural Paradigm",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G003",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G005:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5A8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G005:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5A8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G005",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G004",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G006",
    "text": "**Apeiron (Fluid Core Substrate)**: Schema-free semantic continuum — Markdown content as an unbounded fractal tree, not a fixed document/block hierarchy.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G007",
    "text": "**Peras (Crystallized Interfaces)**: Typed boundary structures. As of the fractal-ontology redesign, the whole `BlockNode` tree is eagerly parsed and committed on ingest, each block assigned a Snowflake-generated identity independent of its content or offsets, rather than reified lazily on-demand — the on-demand-reification mechanism (`SpanNode`, character-offset spans) described in earlier revisions of this doc was eliminated, not merely deferred.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Directory Structure: Core vs Community Boundaries",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G003",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G009",
    "text": "```\nAperas/                               # Core Engine (Maintained by Core Dev Team)\n├── scripts/                          # Universal infrastructure & DB operational tooling\n│   ├── restore.sh                    # Backup, restore & verification suite\n│   ├── tdb-log.sh                    # Local-time commit log helper (default count: 20)\n│   └── tdb-doc.sh                    # Local-time document inspection helper\n├── skills/                           # Core agent skill definitions\n│   └── terminusdb/                   # TerminusDB operational reference skill\n├── web/                              # TypeScript project for substrate transducers & UI\n│   └── src/lib/                      # Core data access, AST transducer & schema modules\n│\n└── AperasKG/                         # Domain KG (Maintained by Community)\n    ├── artifacts/                    # Plain-text Markdown domain projections & documentation\n    ├── Apeiron/                      # Portable JSON-LD substrate mirror (schema + instances), bidirectional — see §5\n    ├── schema/                       # Domain-specific JSON-LD schemas extending core\n    ├── ontology/                     # Domain-specific predicate taxonomy / vocabularies\n    └── transducers/                  # Domain-specific ingestion/parsing scripts\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G008",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00A",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G008",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. Canonical Data Substrate & Schema",
    "text": "The canonical schema is implemented in `web/src/lib/schema.json` (pure JSON-LD, no longer duplicated as TypeScript interfaces) using a fractal, content-addressed class lineage — see `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` for the full rationale:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00F"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00C",
    "text": "| JSON-LD Class | Key Strategy | Core Fields | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `BaseNode` | — (abstract) | `links: Set<BaseLink>` | Root of all addressable content; owns intrinsic links. |\n| `BaseLink` | — (abstract) | `target: BaseNode`, `predicate` | Intrinsic connection authored inside a node. |\n| `BaseEdge` | — (abstract) | `source: BaseNode` (+ `BaseLink` fields) | Extrinsic assertion, independently authored/floating. |\n| `Assertion` | — (concrete, no own fields) | inherits `BaseEdge` | The one concrete extrinsic-edge leaf type — `BaseEdge` is abstract, so something instantiable is needed to actually write edges like `impacts`/`verifies`/`affects`. See `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C. |\n| `BlockNode` | Lexical (`blockId`, Snowflake-generated) | `blockId`, `title`, `text`, `children: List<BlockNode>`, `unfolded` | Fractal content atom — a document is just the root `BlockNode` of its `ArtifactNode`; no separate `DocumentNode`. |\n| `ArtifactNode` | Lexical (`path`) | `path`, `fileHash`, `lastTrackedAt`, `ingestedHash`, `lastIngestedAt`, `root: BlockNode` | Metadata index for tracking repository files and hash deltas; anchors the fractal tree. `fileHash`/`ingestedHash` are flat file-content hashes, not structural — see `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.B. |\n| `FolderNode` | Lexical (`path`) | `title`, `path`, `text`, `children: List<BaseNode>` | Structural container mirroring a directory under `AperasKG/artifacts/`; absorbs its own `README.md` (never exposed as a separate `ArtifactNode`) and references nested `FolderNode`s/`ArtifactNode`s by id. See `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §4.A. |",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00B",
    "type": "table",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00D",
    "text": "> **Design note**: `BlockNode` identity is a Snowflake-style generated id (see `web/src/lib/snowflake.ts`), not a content hash — decoupling node identity from content fingerprinting was the resolution to the identity/dedup conflation documented in `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` Appendix F.\n>\n> **Superseded**: `DocumentNode`, `SpanNode`, and `TripleAssertion` (Lexical/Random-keyed, positional `blockId`s) were the Phase 0 schema before this redesign — eliminated entirely, not merely renamed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00B",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00E",
    "text": "> **Schema Protocol Requirement**: Schema initialization uses `full_replace` for idempotent updates. The submitted schema array **must include an explicit `@context` document** (`@base: \"terminusdb:///data/\"`, `@schema: \"terminusdb:///schema#\"`), or the server will reject the replacement.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00B",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00F",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00B",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Data Access & Transduction Modules",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00H",
    "text": "| Module | Location | Responsibilities & Protocol Constraints |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Client Manager** | `web/src/lib/client.ts` | Configures `TerminusDB.WOQLClient`, creates `aperas` database, applies idempotent schema initialization via `full_replace` from `schema.json`. |\n| **Node Identity** | `web/src/lib/snowflake.ts` | Generates the 64-bit Snowflake-style `blockId` (timestamp + machine identity + sequence, 13-char Crockford Base32) — see `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §1.A. |\n| **AST Transducer** | `web/src/lib/astParser.ts` | Uses `unified.js` (`remark-parse`) to recursively convert Markdown into a nested `BlockNode` tree, assigning each block a fresh Snowflake id via `snowflake.ts` (identity is never derived from content or position). |\n| **Artifact Tracking & Ingestion** | `web/src/lib/artifacts.ts` | Computes `fileHash`, tracks/upserts lightweight `ArtifactNode`s, and ingests the full `BlockNode` tree on change — see §4 below. |\n| **Folder Ingestion** | `web/src/lib/folders.ts` | Walks `AperasKG/artifacts/` and commits the `FolderNode` structural tree — see §4 below. |\n| **Extrinsic Assertion CRUD** | `web/src/lib/crud.ts` | `insertAssertion`/`deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` for the concrete `Assertion` type (source/predicate/target, node-typed per §3 of the design doc's rejection of the `xsd:string` anti-pattern), plus generic best-effort document delete helpers. |\n| **WOQL Engine** | `web/src/lib/woql.ts` | Builds and executes WOQL queries over `Assertion` — `queryNodeAssertions` (both directions) and `traceImpactPropagation` (one-hop sweep along a predicate like `impacts`). |\n| **GraphQL Client** | `web/src/lib/graphql.ts` | `getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` fetches an `ArtifactNode` and its full nested `BlockNode` tree (bounded depth) via the auto-generated GraphQL endpoint — see the note below on why this is the *only* full-tree read path. |\n| **Version Control** | `web/src/lib/versionControl.ts` | Manages branches, temporal commit histories, version diffing, merge applications (`apply`), and state resets. Class-agnostic — unaffected by the schema redesign. |\n| **JSON-LD Import/Export** | `web/src/lib/export.ts` | `exportJsonLd`/`importJsonLd` mirror the current schema plus every instance document (per class) between TerminusDB and plain JSON-LD files in `AperasKG/Apeiron/` — see §5. |",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00G",
    "type": "table",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00J",
    "text": "> **Read-path note**: `client.getDocument()` on an `ArtifactNode` returns `root` as a bare reference id string, not the nested tree, and likewise for `BlockNode.children`. This is *not* because `@subdocument` is required for unfolding (a natural-sounding but wrong assumption we initially made) — it's simply that `BlockNode` isn't schema-annotated as unfoldable. TerminusDB has two independent schema-level annotations that control this: property-level `\"@unfold\": true` and class-level `\"@unfoldable\": []`. We benchmarked marking `BlockNode` class-level `@unfoldable` (which does work, including through the recursive `List<BlockNode>` children) against the real 149-block `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` tree, and it was ~3.5x slower than `getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL`'s GraphQL query (~48ms vs ~13.5ms steady-state) — so we kept GraphQL as the tree-read path rather than adopting it. Separately, we found that the *property-level* `\"@unfold\": true` variant silently does nothing on `List`-typed fields specifically (works on `Set`/`Optional`/`Cardinality`) — an upstream bug, filed as [terminusdb/terminusdb#2512](https://github.com/terminusdb/terminusdb/issues/2512), traced to `List`'s internal RDF-linked-list (`Cons`/`rdf:first`/`rdf:rest`) representation not being reachable from the property-level unfold check. That bug doesn't affect our decision (we'd have used class-level `@unfoldable`, which is unaffected), but is a useful data point that this part of TerminusDB is new and still maturing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00G",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00K",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00G",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00M:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5B8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00M:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5B8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 4. Artifact Tracking & Ingestion Pipeline",
    "text": "Managed via `web/src/lib/artifacts.ts` and CLI entrypoint `web/src/lib/kgCli.ts`:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0000",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ59G00N",
    "text": "**Lightweight Registration (`npm run kg:track`)**:\n  Computes content hashes for files under `AperasKG/artifacts/` and upserts lightweight `ArtifactNode` records — a no-op skip (no KG write at all) when the hash is unchanged, so unscoped sweeps (e.g. the `post-index-change` hook) stay cheap.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0000",
    "text": "**Incremental Ingestion (`npm run kg:ingest`)**:\n  Compares current file content hash (`fileHash`) against stored `ingestedHash`. If changed, parses Markdown into a nested `BlockNode` tree (fresh Snowflake ids throughout) and commits it via a single `ArtifactNode.root` update — TerminusDB recursively ingests the whole tree in one operation. Re-ingesting an already-ingested artifact currently orphans its entire previous tree rather than reusing unchanged blocks' ids — acceptable for now since ingestion targets uningested artifacts; cross-ingestion identity matching is unimplemented (see `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` Appendix F).\n  Also rebuilds the `FolderNode` structural tree (`web/src/lib/folders.ts`) in the same run: one `FolderNode` per directory under `AperasKG/artifacts/`, each absorbing its own `README.md` (parsed blocks become the folder's own children; the file itself is never exposed as a separate `ArtifactNode`) and referencing nested `FolderNode`s/`ArtifactNode`s by id. The whole folder tree is committed as a single nested write from the artifacts root each run — cheap, since it's directory-count-sized, not content-sized.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0001",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G00M",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. JSON-LD Substrate Import/Export",
    "text": "`web/src/lib/export.ts`, wired through `kgCli.ts`, bidirectionally mirrors the schema and every instance document between TerminusDB and plain JSON-LD files in `AperasKG/Apeiron/`:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0007",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0008"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0003",
    "text": "| File | Contents |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `schema.jsonld` | The full schema graph (`getDocument({ graph_type: 'schema' })`), verbatim. |\n| `ArtifactNode.jsonld`, `FolderNode.jsonld`, `BlockNode.jsonld`, `Assertion.jsonld` | One file per instance class — a leading `@context` document, then every live (`getDocument({ type, as_list: true })`) instance of that class, sorted by id for diff-stable re-exports. |",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0002",
    "type": "table",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5BR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5BR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0004",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0002",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0006"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0005",
    "text": "**`npm run kg:export`** — writes all of the above from the current TerminusDB state, overwriting the files.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0006",
    "text": "**`npm run kg:import`** — reads the files back: applies `schema.jsonld` via `full_replace`, then upserts each class's instances (every document already carries its own `@id`, so a matching document is updated in place, not duplicated). Each class is submitted as a single batched `updateDocument` call, in the dependency order `BlockNode` → `ArtifactNode` → `FolderNode` → `Assertion`, so a document is never written before something it references — `BlockNode`'s own parent/child references resolve within its own batch (one commit, so intra-batch forward references are fine); everything after it can then safely reference already-committed ids. Every write is its own TerminusDB commit, so before writing the schema or a given class, its content hash is compared (`hashDocSet` in `client.ts` — the same order-independent hash already used to skip a no-op schema apply) against what's currently live, and the write is skipped entirely when nothing changed, rather than landing a no-op commit.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Purpose: how `AperasKG` holds a backup of its own KG",
    "text": "This round-trip — not a TerminusDB-level backup — is the correct answer to \"how does `AperasKG` hold a record of its KG,\" per its original design goal of storing both the artifacts and the KG. `AperasKG/Apeiron/` is the canonical graph content, materialized as plain, diffable JSON-LD text and committed to the same git repository as `AperasKG/artifacts/` — an ordinary `git commit` over that directory *is* the KG's backup, with all the history, diffing, and portability that implies, and `kg:import` is the restore path (bootstrap a fresh database, or recover state) back out of it. This also happens to make the content engine-agnostic — `AperasKG/Apeiron/` is a snapshot any future substrate engine could read without a bespoke migration script (see `Aperas-design.md`'s Development Roadmap, \"Phase 4: Substrate Evolution\") — but that's a secondary benefit of the same design, not the primary reason it exists.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0002",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Scope and non-goals",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0002",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000D"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0009:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5C0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0009:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5C0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0009",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0008",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000C"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000A",
    "text": "**Flat, not nested.** Instances are dumped and read back exactly as `getDocument`/`updateDocument` handle them — `BlockNode.children` and `ArtifactNode.root` are reference ids, not inlined subtrees (see §2's \"Read-path note\"), matching how TerminusDB itself stores documents and keeping per-class diffs scoped to that class alone.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0009",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000B",
    "text": "**Includes tombstoned documents.** The export/import round-trip is a full audit snapshot, not a live-only view — reconciliation's `tombstonedAt` markers (`Aperas-reconciliation-matching-design.md`) are preserved rather than filtered out.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0009",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000C",
    "text": "**Whole-class granularity, not a merge.** Import upserts every document in a class file; it doesn't diff against the live database or reconcile divergence — reconciliation (`reconcile.ts`) only ever runs against `AperasKG/artifacts/` content during `kg:ingest`, not against this export/import path.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0009",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000D",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0008",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. TerminusDB Server Operational Backup",
    "text": "This section is server infrastructure for the TerminusDB instance itself — Docker-volume-level disaster recovery, unrelated to `AperasKG`'s own record of its content. It answers \"how do I recover this TerminusDB server if its volume is lost,\" not \"how does `AperasKG` hold a backup of its KG.\" That second question is answered by §5: `AperasKG/Apeiron/` plus `kg:export`/`kg:import`, git-tracked alongside `AperasKG/artifacts/` like any other content in the domain-KG repo — not by storing TerminusDB-level snapshots in or near `AperasKG` at all, which an earlier design iteration tried (`db/` briefly lived under `AperasKG/`) before being reconsidered as the wrong coupling: a git-tracked domain repo has no good way to diff, merge, or meaningfully version an opaque binary DB snapshot, and (per the investigation below) those snapshots aren't even portable between independent TerminusDB stores.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000M"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Whole-Volume Disaster Recovery (`restore.sh backup-full | verify-full | restore-full`)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000G"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5CG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5CG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000G",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000F",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000H",
    "text": "**Primary Adopted Method**: Creates a tarball of the backing Docker volume `terminusdb_storage` via a temporary Alpine container with the TerminusDB container temporarily stopped to ensure on-disk head consistency.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000J",
    "text": "**Scope**: **Cross-Machine Host Transfer**.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000K",
    "text": "**Restore Safety**: `restore-full` extracts into an isolated temporary volume/container (`<container>-restored`) for inspection. Automatic promotion (`--overwrite`) updates container volume bindings with automatic fallback to `-old` on startup failure.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Same-Store Snapshot (`restore.sh backup | verify | restore`)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000R"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000N:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5CR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000N:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5CR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000N",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000M",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000Q"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000P",
    "text": "**Mechanism**: Wraps TerminusDB `bundle` / `unbundle` CLI commands. `backup` self-verifies by unbundling into a temporary verification database.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000Q",
    "text": "**Scope**: **Same-instance snapshot & local rollback only**. CLI `.bundle` exports suffer from cross-store layer reference incompatibility (issue #2509) and cannot be transferred across independent TerminusDB server instances.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000R",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000M",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5D0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5D0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 7. SSH-Tunneled Remotes",
    "text": "TerminusDB remotes communicate over HTTP REST endpoints. Cross-machine communication between separate hosts utilizes SSH local port forwarding:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000Y",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000Z"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000T",
    "text": "**Transport Rationale**: Native remote commands (`push`/`pull`/`fetch`/`clone`) speak HTTP(S) only. TerminusDB itself listens on `0.0.0.0:6363` inside the container (confirmed via `/proc/net/tcp`) — the loopback-only restriction (`127.0.0.1:6363`) is this project's own `docker run -p 127.0.0.1:6363:6363` deployment choice, not a TerminusDB default. Either way, this project's containers are loopback-only, so cross-machine transport requires an SSH tunnel.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000V",
    "text": "**Tunnel Endpoint**: `ssh -N -f -L 0.0.0.0:6364:localhost:6363 <user>@<remote-host>`",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000W",
    "text": "**Firewall Access**: `sudo ufw allow from 172.17.0.0/16 to any port 6364 proto tcp`",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000X",
    "text": "**Bridge Addressing**: Remote URLs target the host's Docker bridge gateway (`http://172.17.0.1:6364/admin/aperas`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000Y",
    "text": "**Remote Tracking**: `fetch` updates local remote-tracking descriptors (`<org>/<db>/<remote>/branch/<branch>`). `fetch` must be executed before `push` or `pull` can resolve remote heads.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A000Z",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A000S",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 8. Verification & Operational Utilities",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ59G001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0011"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0011:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5DG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0011:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5DR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0011",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0010",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0012",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0013",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0014"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0012",
    "text": "**Verification Test Harness**: `npm run verify:phase0 -- --db` (`web/src/lib/verifyPhase0.ts`) — end-to-end, idempotent, self-cleaning live test: AST parsing, schema init, artifact track/ingest, GraphQL tree read, `FolderNode` ingestion, `Assertion` CRUD + WOQL impact propagation, and temporal commit management (branch + commit log). Demo-state cleanup has to happen in reference-reversed order — `FolderNode` before `ArtifactNode` before its `BlockNode`s — since TerminusDB enforces referential integrity and rejects deleting a still-referenced document.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0011",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0013",
    "text": "**`Aperas/scripts/tdb-log.sh`**: Provides compact, local-time commit history (`--count 20` by default) with fast client-side message filtering (`--filter`). Avoids sluggish `tdb history` diffs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0011",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5A0014",
    "text": "**`Aperas/scripts/tdb-doc.sh`**: Wraps `tdb doc get` to convert ISO UTC timestamps to local time and unescape literal newlines (`\\n`) into readable multi-line prose.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5A0011",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Artifact Projection — Design",
    "text": "`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §5.A names three projection modes that turn a stored\n`BlockNode` tree back into an output: Artifact Projection (serialize back to the physical\n`.md` file, always fully unfolded regardless of each block's `unfolded` state), the Human UI,\nand the Agentic BFS interface. This doc settles the design for the first of the three —\nArtifact Projection, the direct inverse of `astParser.ts`'s Markdown → tree transform. The\nother two modes are out of scope here. The design below is settled; nothing has been\nimplemented yet.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW968000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Canonical regeneration, not byte-exact",
    "text": "`heading`/`paragraph`/`code` blocks already round-trip exactly — their `title`/`text` are raw\nsource slices, not derived summaries. Container types (`list`, `listItem`, `blockquote`) are\nregenerated as clean, valid Markdown rather than reproducing the original file's exact\nwhitespace and spacing. This matches the bar reconciliation already uses elsewhere in this\nproject: \"parses back to the same tree,\" not \"identical bytes.\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. List items are always blank-line-separated in the output",
    "text": "mdast's `spread` (tight vs. loose list) isn't captured. CommonMark requires blank lines\nbetween list items whenever any item is \"loose\" (multi-paragraph); always emitting them is\nvalid either way and has no effect on the re-parsed tree, since remark already wraps every\nlist item's content in a paragraph regardless of the original list's tightness (a pre-existing,\nseparately-confirmed quirk — not something this design introduces). Capturing `spread` would\nonly ever affect cosmetic rendering spacing, never structure, so it's left out.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Blockquote line-prefixing is a projection-side concern",
    "text": "CommonMark only requires the `> ` marker on a blockquote's first line (or after a blank line\nends and restarts the quote) — a continuation line needs no marker at all (\"lazy\ncontinuation\"). Whether `astParser.ts`'s raw-text slice for a multi-line paragraph inside a\n`blockquote` contains a stray `> ` on its continuation lines therefore depends on how the\nsource was actually written, not on anything the parser does: confirmed live, `\"> line\none\\n> line two\"` (marker repeated, common in practice) slices to `\"line one\\n> line two\"`\n(marker present in the leaf's own `text`), while `\"> line one\\nline two\"` (lazy continuation,\nalso valid) slices to `\"line one\\nline two\"` (nothing stray — matches mdast's own semantic\n`text.value` either way). Rather than depending on which form a given source used — or fixing\nthis at ingestion, which would perturb reconciliation's matching keys for existing blockquote\ncontent — the serializer normalizes at output time: strip any leading `> ` from each line of a\nblockquote's rendered content (a no-op when there isn't one), then re-apply `> ` uniformly.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. New fields: `ordered`, `start`, `checked` — captured, not derived",
    "text": "A `list` node's numbered-vs-bulleted style and starting number, and a `listItem`'s task-list\ncheckbox state, aren't recoverable from anything currently stored — they have to be captured\nat parse time. mdast already carries all three directly on the relevant nodes (`list.ordered`,\n`list.start`, `listItem.checked`, confirmed live) — nothing lazy or derived about them, they\nsit on the same mdast node object `astParser.ts` already visits. They're absent from\n`ParsedBlockNode` today because `convertAstNode` was never asked to read them, not because\nthey were unavailable: it extracts `blockId`/`title`/`text`/`type`/`children` identically for\nevery node type and has never read a type-specific mdast property, since nothing downstream\nneeded one before projection made round-tripping a goal. So this is a scope gap in the\nexisting extraction logic, closed by capture — not inference of something the parser\npreviously discarded.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG006"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG006:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5H0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG006:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5H0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG006",
    "text": "Schema (`BlockNode`, all `Optional` — unlike the `artifactId`/`type` migration, this needs no\nDB wipe, since existing documents remain valid against new optional fields):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG005",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG007",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG009"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG007",
    "text": "`ordered: Optional xsd:boolean` — set on `list` nodes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG008",
    "text": "`start: Optional xsd:integer` — set on `list` nodes (mdast always provides one, even for\n  unordered lists, where it's simply unused).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG009",
    "text": "`checked: Optional xsd:boolean` — set on `listItem` nodes only when mdast's own `checked` is\n  non-null; its absence (not `false`) is what distinguishes \"not a task item\" from \"unchecked.\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5H8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5H8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 5. The algorithm",
    "text": "Pure, recursive dispatch on `node.type`:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00H"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00B",
    "text": "**root** / any other container fallback: children rendered and joined with a blank line.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00C",
    "text": "**heading**: `title` (already the full raw heading line, `#`s included) followed by its\n  rendered children, blank-line-joined.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00D",
    "text": "**paragraph**: `text`, dedented (every line but the first stripped of whatever common leading\n  whitespace they share — needed because a multi-line leaf's raw slice only has line 1's\n  container-required indentation stripped; every later line keeps its literal original-file\n  column, confirmed live on both a `blockquote` continuation line and a nested `code` fence's\n  interior — left alone, a container's own re-indentation (below) would compound on top of that\n  stale absolute value instead of establishing a clean baseline).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00E",
    "text": "**code**: same dedenting, plus one more check — a *fenced* block's raw slice starts with its\n  own `` ``` ``/`~~~` marker, same as any other leaf, and is emitted as-is once dedented. An\n  *indented* (4-space) code block is also mdast type `code`, but its raw slice carries no fence\n  at all, and unlike every other leaf, line 1 isn't stripped of its marker either (confirmed\n  live — the indentation *is* the syntax marker, so it survives on every line including the\n  first). Left alone this would silently degrade into an ordinary paragraph on projection, so a\n  raw slice with no fence marker is dedented across all its lines (line 1 included) and\n  re-wrapped in a fresh generic fence — always converting indented-style input to fenced-style\n  output (§8).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00F",
    "text": "**blockquote**: render children, strip a leading `> ` from every line, re-prefix every line\n  with `> ` (§3).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00G",
    "text": "**list**: render each child as a list item — marker is `${n}. ` if `ordered`, else `- `,\n  with `n` starting at `start ?? 1` — joined with a blank line (§2).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00H",
    "text": "**list item**: prefix = marker + (`[x] ` / `[ ] ` if `checked` is defined), body = children\n  rendered and blank-line-joined, every line after the first indented to the prefix's width.\n  This is what makes a nested `list` under a `listItem` indent correctly — it falls out of the\n  recursion for free, no special-casing needed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Entry point",
    "text": "`projectArtifactToMarkdown(client, path)`: fetch the tree via the existing\n`getArtifactTreeViaGraphQL` (`graphql.ts` — its field selection needs `ordered`/`start`/\n`checked` added, the same fix already needed once this session for the `type` field), then run\nthe serializer over `tree.root`. Root itself is never emitted — it falls through the generic\ncontainer fallback, which is exactly \"join my children.\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 7. Verification strategy",
    "text": "Round-trip equivalence — parse a sample exercising every branch (ordered list, unordered list\nwith a task item, nested list, blockquote, code, headings) → serialize → re-parse → compare —\nis checked by reusing `reconcile.ts`'s `reconcileTree` (the original parse as `oldRoot`, the\nre-parsed result as `newRoot`): equivalence is `stats.added === 0 && stats.removed === 0`,\ni.e. every block matches. This reuses already-verified matching logic as the equivalence check\ninstead of a bespoke tree comparator, and doubles as exercise of `reconcile.ts` against list/\ntask-list shapes it hasn't been run against yet.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00M"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00M:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5HR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00M:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5HR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00M",
    "text": "Run against every real artifact in `AperasKG/artifacts/` (not just the synthetic sample): 9 of\n11 round-trip with zero added/removed. The other 2 each produce exactly one added+removed pair,\nboth explained by §1's canonical-not-byte-exact choice rather than a bug:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00K",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00P"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00N",
    "text": "A nested `code` fence whose original *absolute* indentation was deeper than the minimal\n  amount its list item requires (source used 4 spaces where 2 would do) re-projects at the\n  canonical 2 — the code's own *relative* internal formatting is preserved, only the baseline\n  shifts, but that's still a different `text`, so exact-key Stage A correctly doesn't match it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5GG00P",
    "text": "An indented-style code block converts to fenced-style on projection (§5) — a real, disclosed\n  change to the stored `text`, so it doesn't match either, for the same reason.\n  Both are accepted per the same \"precision over recall\" principle reconciliation already uses\n  elsewhere (§2 of the reconciliation design) — a wrong same-content guess is worse than a\n  visible remove+add.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5GG00M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Basic Assertion Authoring — Design",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW96G000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0007",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000J"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5N8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5N8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 0. Why this moved out of Phase 2",
    "text": "The original roadmap (`Aperas-design.md`'s Development Roadmap) only ever named one assertion-\nrelated deliverable — Phase 2's \"graph search skill\" — and treated the Human UI (Phase 1) as\nthe thing that lets humans/agents see what's in the graph at all. Neither assumption survived\nPhase 0 finishing:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N0003",
    "text": "`Assertion` (the concrete `BaseEdge`, `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §2.C-D) has been a\n  fully working CRUD/WOQL primitive since Phase 0 — `insertAssertion`/`deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode`\n  (`crud.ts`), `queryNodeAssertions`/`traceImpactPropagation` (`woql.ts`) — but the *only* thing\n  that has ever called `insertAssertion` is a single hardcoded synthetic edge in\n  `verifyPhase0.ts`'s test harness. Nothing lets a human or agent write a real one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N0004",
    "text": "Artifact Projection (`Aperas-artifact-projection-design.md`) turned out to cover the\n  monitoring need the Human UI was assumed to be required for — `kg:project` already lets a\n  human read back exactly what's stored, without a SolidJS front end.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N0005",
    "text": "So the basic ability to *write and inspect* a real `Assertion` — with no automated traversal,\nimpact-sweeping, or agent behavior attached — is built now, in Phase 0, so that Phase 1's\nskill-building work starts with it already available rather than blocked on it. Phase 2's\n\"graph search skill\" (`Aperas-design.md` §Development Roadmap, Phase 2) still owns everything\n*automated*: agents that traverse `affects`/`impacts` edges on their own initiative and flag\nstaleness. This doc only covers the manual primitive those agents would eventually build on.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Scope: manual, not automated",
    "text": "This is a CLI-operated capability, not a skill that runs on its own. A human or an agent\nexplicitly invokes a command to assert one claim, or to look up what's already asserted about\na node. No code here decides *when* to assert something or walks the graph proactively — that\nremains Phase 2 territory, undesigned.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. Addressing is direct-or-query, not \"resolution\" or \"memory\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0008:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5NR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0008:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5NR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N0008",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0007",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N0009",
    "text": "**Direct addressing** — the normal case. You already hold a real node id, from wherever (a\n  prior command's own printed confirmation, a query result, code that still has the parsed\n  tree available). `AssertionInput.source`/`target` (`BlockNode/<id>`, `ArtifactNode/<id>`,\n  `FolderNode/<id>`) is passed through completely unchanged — no lookup, no search, nothing to\n  resolve.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000A",
    "text": "**Query-based addressing** — applies when there's no id in hand at all. The simplest query\n  this project has is an exact match on a path, and both path-addressable node kinds get it\n  identically: a bare artifact path (e.g. `Aperas-design.md`) resolves via the existing\n  `getArtifactRecord` (`artifacts.ts`) to `ArtifactNode/<artifactId>`, and a bare folder path\n  (e.g. `.` for the artifacts root, or a subdirectory name) resolves via the structurally\n  identical `getFolderRecord` (`folders.ts`) to `FolderNode/<folderId>`. An artifact path and a\n  folder path never collide, so trying artifact-then-folder resolution in that order is\n  unambiguous. (Earlier revisions of this doc called this \"from memory\" — that framing\n  conflated an ordinary exact-match query with a distinct human-recall mechanism; see\n  `Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` §1 for the fuller correction and the complete\n  inventory of what query tools this project actually has.)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000B",
    "text": "`BlockNode` has no path-like shortcut: nobody carries an opaque id in their head or types block\ncontent as a handle. So a block-level assertion needs either an id already in hand from\nwhatever produced the claim (e.g. a skill that just parsed content and still holds the tree), or\na real query tool to find one — see `Aperas-agentic-query-tools-design.md` (`kg:tree`,\n`kg:expand`, `kg:search`), which closes this gap three ways: a structural map, a one-level\ncontent peek, and keyword/regex search. (Superseded reasoning, kept for context: this doc\noriginally argued no such command was needed in Phase 0, since nothing yet created a situation\nrequiring one — that held until the need was recognized as a set of general-purpose query\ntools in their own right, not just an assertion helper.)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0007",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Commands (`kgCli.ts`)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000D"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000D:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5P0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000D:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5P0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000D",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000C",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000G"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000E",
    "text": "`kg:assert <source> <predicate> <target>` — `source`/`target` each accept a full node id\n  (direct addressing) or a bare artifact/folder path (query-based addressing, per §2); calls\n  `insertAssertion`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000F",
    "text": "`kg:assertions <node>` — same addressing as above, calls `queryNodeAssertions`, prints each\n  as `<direction>  <predicate>  <otherNodeId>`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000G",
    "text": "`kg:unassert <source> <predicate> <target>` — same addressing, deletes exactly the matching\n  assertion(s), not every assertion touching a node. `deleteAssertionsInvolvingNode` (existing,\n  `crud.ts`) is intentionally blunt — it exists for demo/reset cleanup, where wiping everything\n  touching one node is correct. A real \"undo this claim\" command needs a narrower\n  `deleteAssertion(client, source, predicate, target)`: filter `Assertion` docs on all three\n  fields (not just one), delete only those. New function, `crud.ts`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. Real bug found by live testing (fixed)",
    "text": "`kg:assertions` initially printed the predicate as `[object Object]`. WOQL binds a node-typed\nfield (`source`/`target` — `BaseNode`) as a plain id string, but a literal-typed field\n(`predicate` — `xsd:string`) as a wrapped `{\"@type\": \"xsd:string\", \"@value\": \"...\"}` object —\nconfirmed live by querying directly. `traceImpactPropagation`'s existing test coverage never\ncaught this because it only checks binding *presence*/*membership* (`AffectedNode`, itself\nnode-typed), never a literal field's actual value. Fixed in `woql.ts`'s `queryNodeAssertions`\nwith a small `unwrapLiteral` helper applied to `predicate` in both directions.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. Out of scope",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000K:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5PG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000K:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5PG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000K",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000J",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000P"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000M",
    "text": "Any automated creation or traversal (Phase 2's graph search skill).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000K",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000N",
    "text": "Per-asserter branch placement — `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`'s \"Author-Based Placement\"\n  (§ Design Rationale) describes assertions eventually living in the asserting agent's own\n  branch/artifact so they don't require mutating someone else's. That's a multi-agent-branching\n  concern (Phase 3 territory); today, same as every other write in this codebase, an `Assertion`\n  simply commits to whatever branch the client is already pointed at (`main`). Not addressed\n  here.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000K",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5N000P",
    "text": "Restricting `predicate` to a fixed vocabulary — stays free text, same as `insertAssertion`\n  today; the known examples (`impacts`/`verifies`/`derived_from`/`affects`) remain\n  conventions, not an enum.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5N000K",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Aperas Core Ontology Design",
    "text": "This document defines the core semantic graph ontology for Aperas. It establishes mathematically rigorous identity, strong polymorphic typing, and universal addressability for all graph components.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW96R000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0011",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0020",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Node Identity vs. Content Fingerprint",
    "text": "Addressable components (e.g., `BlockNode`) require stable identities that survive re-ingestion. Because identity must remain constant across edits while content-change detection must reflect every edit, these concerns are handled by two separate fields (see Appendix F for why a single-hash design failed).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000C"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. Node Identity (Stable, Content-Independent)",
    "text": "A `BlockNode`'s `@id` is a **Snowflake-style generated identifier**. It is assigned once at creation and is fully decoupled from the node's content and position, ensuring that `BaseLink` and `BaseEdge` assertions survive edits to their referenced nodes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0002",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0007",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0004",
    "text": "**64-bit Layout**: Encoded as a 13-character Crockford Base32 string. It is compact and lexicographically sortable by creation time.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0005",
    "text": "| Field | Bits | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sign** | 1 | Unused. Keeps value a positive signed int64 for cross-language compatibility. |\n| **Timestamp** | 45 | Milliseconds since custom epoch (e.g., 2025-01-01) — provides ~1,115 years of range. |\n| **Machine ID** | 10 | 1 anomaly-flag bit + 9 machine-number bits — supports 512 machines with personal failover identities. |\n| **Sequence** | 8 | Per-machine-identity counter, reset each millisecond — allows 256 ids/ms/machine. |",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0003",
    "type": "table",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0006",
    "text": "**Deterministic Collision Avoidance**: \nCollisions are prevented by construction rather than probability. Timestamps separate different times, the sequence counter disambiguates same-millisecond generation on one machine, and the machine ID separates different machines.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0007:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5YG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0007:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5YG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0007",
    "text": "**Handling Local Clock Anomalies (NTP corrections)**:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0008",
    "text": "**Prevention**: NTP uses gradual \"slew\" adjustments instead of \"step\" (instant jumps).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0009",
    "text": "**Absorption**: The generator uses a high-water-mark (`max(now, last_emitted)`). Small backward jumps are absorbed by the sequence counter.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000A",
    "text": "**Failover**: If a backward jump is too large (risking counter overflow), the generator flips its anomaly bit to use a distinct failover machine ID, preventing collisions with its normal output.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000B",
    "text": "*(Note: While extreme, unbounded backward jumps under sustained load remain theoretically possible, they require deliberate clock tampering.)*",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. Content Fingerprint & Reconciliation",
    "text": "Once a `BlockNode` tree is ingested into the database, **the DB becomes the source of truth**. Edits are made directly in the DB, and artifact files are regenerated (projected) from it. Re-ingestion is a **fallback reconciliation path**, triggered only when an external hand-edit causes the file to drift from its projected state.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0002",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000H"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000D:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5Z0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000D:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5Z0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000D",
    "text": "**Minimal Fingerprinting on `ArtifactNode`**:\nOnly two flat, file-level hashes are maintained (no structural hashes):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000F"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000E",
    "text": "**`fileHash`**: Hash of the raw file's current content, recomputed on every `track` event.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000F",
    "text": "**`ingestedHash`**: Hash of the raw file's content as of the last confirmed sync (ingestion or projection).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000G",
    "text": "**Reconciliation Trigger**: If `fileHash !== ingestedHash`, an unwitnessed edit occurred outside the DB, triggering reconciliation.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000H",
    "text": "**DB-to-File Changes**: No hash is needed for DB updates. Because DB writes are always witnessed, the system can mark the corresponding artifact as needing re-projection within the same transaction.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. The Universal Node-Link-Edge Lineage",
    "text": "The core graph is built on a clean, fractal inheritance lineage. Every relationship is a fully addressable, first-class Document.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000Z"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. BaseNode (The Root)",
    "text": "The abstract root of all addressable content (inherited by `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`, `FolderNode`). It explicitly owns an array of its intrinsic links.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000J",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000M"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000M",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"BaseNode\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@abstract\": [],\n  \"links\": { \"@type\": \"Set\", \"@class\": \"BaseLink\" }\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000K",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000N:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5ZG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000N:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5ZG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### B. BaseLink (Intrinsic Connections)",
    "text": "An abstract class representing connections authored physically *inside* a source node (e.g., Markdown hyperlinks).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000J",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000R"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000P",
    "text": "**Inheritance**: Inherits from `BaseNode`. (This means links themselves possess a `links` array, allowing arbitrary depth for provenance or comments).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000Q",
    "text": "**Implicit Source**: Because a `BaseLink` resides within the `BaseNode.links` array, the node holding the array is implicitly the source.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000R",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"BaseLink\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@abstract\": [],\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseNode\"],\n  \"target\": \"BaseNode\",\n  \"predicate\": \"xsd:string\"\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000N",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000S:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5ZR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000S:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ5ZR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### C. BaseEdge (Extrinsic Assertions)",
    "text": "An abstract class representing independent semantic claims asserted from the outside (e.g., an AI Agent deducing a relationship).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000J",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000W"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000T",
    "text": "**Inheritance**: Inherits from `BaseLink`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000V",
    "text": "**Explicit Source**: Because it floats independently of any node's `links` array, it adds a `source` pointer to explicitly declare its origin.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000S",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000W",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"BaseEdge\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@abstract\": [],\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseLink\"],\n  \"source\": \"BaseNode\"\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000S",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### D. Assertion (The Concrete Extrinsic Edge)",
    "text": "`BaseEdge` is abstract, so it can never be instantiated directly — something concrete has to exist to actually write an extrinsic claim (`impacts`, `verifies`, `derived_from`, `affects`, ...). `Assertion` is that one generic leaf: it adds no fields of its own, just makes the `source`/`predicate`/`target` (+ inherited `links`) lineage instantiable.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000J",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000Y"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y000Y",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"Assertion\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseEdge\"]\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000X",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### E. Link (The Concrete Intrinsic Edge)",
    "text": "`BaseLink` is abstract too — the same gap as `BaseEdge`/`Assertion`, just noticed later, once `BlockNode.links` extraction (Aperas-markdown-fractal-mapping-design.md §4) actually needed to write one. `Link` is `BaseLink`'s one concrete leaf: no fields of its own, just makes the `target`/`predicate` (+ inherited `links`) lineage instantiable. Used for an inline Markdown link whose target resolves to an internal node (`[title]([[code]])`) — the `predicate` is always the fixed constant `\"references\"` for this use, distinguishing structural inline links from `Assertion`'s deliberately-chosen semantic predicates at query time.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000J",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0010"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0010",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"Link\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseLink\"]\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y000Z",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 3. Universal Addressability & Ownership",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0012",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0015"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0012:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ608000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0012:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ608001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0012",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0011",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0013",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0014"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0013",
    "text": "**No Subdocuments**: `BaseLink` and `BaseEdge` are standard TerminusDB Classes, *not* `@subdocument`s. Every relationship possesses a global `@id`, enabling graph reification (assertions about assertions).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0012",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0014",
    "text": "**Author-Based Placement**: Extrinsic assertions (`BaseEdge`) are never embedded into the nodes they connect. They are stored in the branch or artifact owned by the **Asserter** (the author). This ensures that agents can assert claims about human-authored nodes without mutating the human's artifact.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0012",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. Native Backlinks & Bidirectional Traversal — only for `Set`-typed fields, not `List`",
    "text": "Because TerminusDB is built on RDF triples under the hood, `Set`-typed fields — `BaseNode.links:\nSet<BaseLink>`, `BaseEdge.source`/`target: BaseNode` — really do produce one direct triple per\nvalue, so a generic `t(X, Predicate, Target)` query genuinely finds anything pointing at a target\nthrough one of those, no custom backlink table needed. **Confirmed live**:\n`t(X, 'links', 'Link/y8LuGtix5YFbBn6Y')` correctly returned the owning `BlockNode` as `X`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0011",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0016"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0016:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ60G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0016:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ60G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0016",
    "text": "That does **not** extend to `List`-typed fields — and the tree's actual containment structure\n(`BlockNode.children`, `ArtifactNode.root`, `FolderNode.children`) is `List`, not `Set`,\ndeliberately: child order is semantically meaningful (document/section order; `project.ts`'s\nserializer depends on it) and `Set` is unordered. TerminusDB represents `List` membership through\nintermediate cons-cell/RDF-list structure at the triple level, not a direct `Parent predicate\nChild` triple. **Confirmed live**: `t(X, 'children', '<a known child's id>')` returned zero\nbindings, even though that id is genuinely present in its parent's `children` list. So \"upward\nbreadcrumbs\" and \"universal backlink tracing\" *do* work for `links` (item 1's own example happens\nto pick a `Set` field, so it was never actually wrong) but do **not** give you a node's structural\nparent, or an id→path conversion, for free — that needs either a schema change (a persisted\nback-reference field, the standard trade of write-time bookkeeping for O(1) reverse lookup) or an\nexplicit downward search from a known root, not a generic triple query. **Implemented**: a typed\n`parent: Optional<BaseNode>` field on `BlockNode` (`Aperas-deep-path-resolution-design.md` §8),\nstamped by `astParser.ts`/`artifacts.ts`/`folders.ts` and walked by `nodeRef.ts`'s `resolveIdToPath`\n(`kg:path`). Currently `BlockNode`-only, not yet promoted to `BaseNode` — `FolderNode`-to-`FolderNode`\nnesting and a `FolderNode`'s `ArtifactNode` references have no back-pointer set today, the same shape\nof gap `unfolded` had before its own `BaseNode` promotion (§4 agentic-query-tools design).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0015",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0017",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0019"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0017",
    "text": "**Upward Breadcrumbs via a `Set` field (Context Retrieval)**:\n      Because `BaseLink` is a fully addressable Document, it does not need a physical `parent`\n      pointer for this specific case. If a user addresses a `Link`/`BaseLink` directly\n      (`Link/123`), they can follow the breadcrumbs \"upward\" to find the parent context, since\n      `links` is `Set`-typed:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0016",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0018"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0018",
    "text": "```javascript\n   // What ParentNode has a 'links' property pointing to me?\n   WOQL.triple(\"v:ParentNode\", \"links\", \"Link/123\")\n   ```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0017",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0019",
    "text": "**Backlink Tracing, scoped to `Set`-typed edges (Logseq/Roam style)**:\n      You can instantly query the graph for anything pointing to a target *through a `Set`-typed\n      field* — `links`, or `Assertion.source`/`target` — regardless of the predicate:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0016",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001A",
    "text": "```javascript\n   // What nodes point to this block via a Set-typed field, and via what relationship?\n   WOQL.triple(\"v:SourceNode\", \"v:Predicate\", \"BlockNode/MyTargetBlock\")\n   ```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0019",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001B",
    "text": "This returns every intrinsic `BaseLink`/`Link` or extrinsic `BaseEdge`/`Assertion` referencing\n   the target — but never a structural parent, since `children`/`root` aren't reachable this way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0019",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 4. The Unbounded Block Tree (Node Typology)",
    "text": "The graph topology models all content as an unbounded fractal tree spanning across folders, files, and blocks — \"unbounded\" in the same sense **Apeiron** (§Philosophy) names the unconditioned, boundless substrate: no fixed depth limit, not merely \"very large.\" The rigid boundaries between structural layers are eliminated to form a single continuous tree.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001D",
    "text": "> **Recurring pitfall when designing tools against this ontology**: reaching for \"file\" as a unit\n> of scope. A path like `foo.md` names one `ArtifactNode` — a single node in the same uniform\n> tree as every `FolderNode`/`BlockNode` around it, not a bounded container with its own\n> traversal rules. There is no \"whole file\" tier to recurse into or out of; a command that walks\n> \"everything under `<path>`\" is doing exactly the same walk whether `<path>` resolves to a\n> `BlockNode`, an `ArtifactNode`, or a `FolderNode` — see `kg:tree`'s `childRefs`/`printTree`\n> (`kgCli.ts:47-51,59`), which already recurses through all three with no kind-based branching.\n> Any new command's scoping flags should be designed against depth/traversal-cost concerns (e.g.\n> \"should this default to walking descendants at all,\" per\n> `Aperas-interactive-summarization-design.md` §3/§7), never against a file/folder/block\n> distinction — that distinction doesn't exist here, and designing as if it does is the mistake\n> to catch early.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001C",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001E:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ610000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001E:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ610001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### A. FolderNode (The Structural Container)",
    "text": "Inherits from `BaseNode`. Represents a directory in the filesystem, acting as a seamless bridge in the unbounded tree.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001C",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001J"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001F",
    "text": "**`README.md` Ingestion**: A separate `README.md` file is a legacy \"dumb folder\" workaround. The `README.md` is fully absorbed into the `FolderNode`. Its content populates the folder's `text` (abstract) and initial `children` blocks. The `README.md` is not exposed as a separate `ArtifactNode`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001E",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001G",
    "text": "**`children`**: Contains other nested `FolderNode`s and `ArtifactNode`s, alongside the parsed block children from the `README.md`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001E",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001H",
    "text": "**Identity**: a Snowflake-style generated id, same scheme as `BlockNode` (§1.A); `path` is a plain, mutable field, not part of the key (Appendix G).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001J",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"FolderNode\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseNode\"],\n  \"title\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"path\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"text\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:string\" },\n  \"children\": { \"@type\": \"List\", \"@class\": \"BaseNode\" }\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001E",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. ArtifactNode (The Physical Anchor)",
    "text": "Represents the physical file on disk. It handles file metadata and holds a single pointer to the root of the block tree. Inherits from `BaseNode` — without this, no `BaseLink`/`BaseEdge` could target or originate from a whole file, contradicting §3's universal-addressability goal.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001C",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001N"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001M",
    "text": "**Identity**: Snowflake-generated, same as `FolderNode` above; `path` is a plain, mutable field, not the key (Appendix G). Gains `title`/`text` to match the folding philosophy (§5) uniformly across the whole fractal lineage: `title` is the filename, `text` is an abstract of the file's own content (naive fallback — first paragraph — until AI-driven summarization, §5 enhancement backlog, actually exists).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001K",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001N",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"ArtifactNode\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseNode\"],\n  \"title\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"path\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"text\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:string\" },\n  \"fileHash\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"lastTrackedAt\": \"xsd:dateTime\",\n  \"ingestedHash\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:string\" },\n  \"lastIngestedAt\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:dateTime\" },\n  \"root\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"BlockNode\" }\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001K",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ61G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ61G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### C. BlockNode (The Fractal Atom)",
    "text": "Inherits from `BaseNode`. Every distinct piece of content is a `BlockNode`. The schema is driven by the philosophy of **Abstraction by Folding**: a block's children fold into its `text` (abstract), which folds into a `title`, which folds into a hidden `@id`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001C",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001T",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Z"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Q:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ61G002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Q:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ61G003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001Q",
    "text": "**`title` (The Semantic Label)**: Used for wikilinks and graph queries.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001S"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001R",
    "text": "For direct UI input: The author is prompted to provide a title.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001S",
    "text": "For Markdown ingestion: The parser uses an AI agent to summarize the block into a title. If AI is unavailable, it falls back to the block ID.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Q",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001T",
    "text": "**`text` (The Abstract/Body)**: The actual content shown in the current document.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001V",
    "text": "**`children`**: An ordered `List` of child `BlockNode`s.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001W:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ620000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001W:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ620001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001W",
    "text": "**`unfolded`**: A boolean flag representing the persistent view-state for UIs and Agents.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001X",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Y"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001X",
    "text": "**Human UI & Agent Interface**: When rendering to a screen or an AI prompt, the graph defaults to *folded* (`false`) to manage cognitive/token overload. Only the current block's `text` and the children's `title`s are shown. If this flag is `true`, the subtree auto-expands on load.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001W",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001Y",
    "text": "**Artifact Projection**: When syncing to a physical `.md` file, the tree is ALWAYS 100% unfolded (writing the full document to disk). This flag is ignored.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001W",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y001Z",
    "text": "```json\n{\n  \"@id\": \"BlockNode\",\n  \"@type\": \"Class\",\n  \"@inherits\": [\"BaseNode\"],\n  \"title\": \"xsd:string\",\n  \"text\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:string\" },\n  \"children\": { \"@type\": \"List\", \"@class\": \"BlockNode\" },\n  \"unfolded\": { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:boolean\" }\n}\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y001P",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0020",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. The Folding Philosophy & Graph Traversal",
    "text": "The entire architecture is governed by the philosophy of **Abstraction by Folding**: an unbounded subtree folds into an abstract (`text`), which folds into a semantic label (`title`), which folds into a hidden `@id`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0021",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0021",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0021:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ628000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0021:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ628001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### A. The Three Projection Modes",
    "text": "How this folded state is handled depends strictly on the interaction interface:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0020",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0022",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0023",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0024"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0022",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0022",
    "text": "**Artifact Projection (File Syncing)**: When the system serializes a graph back to a physical `.md` file, the tree is ALWAYS 100% unfolded. The persistent `unfolded` state is ignored, as the physical file must contain the entire document body. For `FolderNode`s, the block children are serialized back out to the folder's `README.md`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0021",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0023",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0023",
    "text": "**Human UI (Phase 1 Web App)**: The UI is folded by default and traverses seamlessly across folder/file boundaries. Users see the `text` (abstract) of the current node (`FolderNode`, `ArtifactNode`, or `BlockNode`) and the abstracts of its direct children. Expanding a `FolderNode` reveals child files/folders and its own `README.md` blocks inline without context switching.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0021",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0024",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0024",
    "text": "**Agentic Interface (BFS Traversal)**: AI agents interact with the graph exactly like the Human UI to manage token limitations. They receive folded views and can navigate continuously from a workspace root `FolderNode` down to a deeply nested `BlockNode` using BFS tool calls to expand subtrees.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0021",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ62G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ62G001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### B. Link Targeting & \"Hover Previews\"",
    "text": "We distinguish between **Intrinsic Links** (`BaseNode.links`) and **Extrinsic Edges** (`BaseEdge`):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0020",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0026",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0027",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0028",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0029",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002D"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0026",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0026",
    "text": "**Intrinsic Links (`links`)**: Links and metadata (tags, properties) authored directly inside the node. Stored in `BaseNode.links`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0027",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0027",
    "text": "**Extrinsic Edges (`BaseEdge`)**: Independent assertions created by external authors/agents. They float independently (to avoid mutating the source node) and are discovered via WOQL queries.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0028",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0028",
    "text": "**UI Display**: The UI displays non-inline metadata/properties from `links` and queried extrinsic `BaseEdge`s in dedicated reference panels. Inline links remain rendered naturally inside the `text` body.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0029",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0029:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ62R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0029:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ62R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y0029",
    "text": "**The Preview Mechanism**:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002A",
    "text": "**Human UI**: Humans receive the preview interactively by **hovering** over a link or wikilink in the UI, popping up the target block's abstract (`text`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0029",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002B",
    "text": "**Agent Interface**: Agents receive the target block's abstract (`text`) embedded directly in the `links` list returned by the projection/tool call, providing instant preview context without requiring UI hover triggers.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0029",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002C",
    "text": "**Manual Jump**: If the user or agent needs more context than the target's abstract, they manually \"jump\" to the target block to explore its children.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002D",
    "text": "---",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0025",
    "type": "thematicBreak",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## Appendix: Design History & Rationale",
    "text": "This section archives the original problems that necessitated this design.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002Q"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### A. The Positional Identity Flaw",
    "text": "In Phase 0, `BlockNode` IDs were positional (`doc1_block_1`). If an author inserted a paragraph at the top of a document, all subsequent blocks shifted their IDs. This silently broke or orphaned any semantic edges pointing to those blocks upon re-ingestion. Moving to **Content-Addressed Identity** resolves this — though content-addressing itself was later found to have its own problems and was superseded; see Appendix F.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. The `xsd:string` Anti-Pattern",
    "text": "Initially, relationships defined `subjectId` and `objectId` as `xsd:string`. This treated relationships as flat text rather than true graph edges, destroying referential integrity. Introducing `BaseNode` as a polymorphic type target resolves this, forcing all edges to point to actual documents in the database.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### C. The Rejection of Subdocuments",
    "text": "TerminusDB's `@subdocument` feature was initially considered for `BaseNode.links` to tightly couple intrinsic links to their parent. However, subdocuments lose their global `@id`. In Aperas, every entity—even a single parsed link or punctuation mark—must be universally addressable so that external agents can comment on or refute it. Thus, subdocuments were rejected, and `BaseLink` was promoted to a full Document class.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### D. Resolving Provenance",
    "text": "Initially, `provenance` was modeled as a hardcoded string or dedicated context object on an edge. By making `BaseLink` inherit from `BaseNode`, every link gets its own `links` array. Provenance is now simply achieved by pushing a new `BaseLink` (e.g., `predicate: \"asserted_by\"`) into the parent link's array, unifying the ontology completely.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### E. The Unbounded Block Tree",
    "text": "Initially, the schema enforced a rigid structural hierarchy (`DocumentNode` -> `BlockNode`). However, this rigid \"solid\" paradigm was abandoned in favor of a fractal, Logseq-inspired block tree. `DocumentNode` was eliminated entirely, as a document is conceptually just a root `BlockNode`. Flattening the graph to Markdown is now handled cleanly via an `unfolded` flag in the projection layer.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### F. Separating Identity from Content Fingerprint",
    "text": "The content-addressed scheme from Appendix A (`@id = hash(parent context + content)`) conflated two requirements that pull in opposite directions: identity needs to stay constant across edits and moves so external references don't break; a content fingerprint needs to change whenever content changes, for dedup and change-detection. A single content-derived hash cannot satisfy both. Concretely, it broke two ways: (1) two independently-authored blocks with coincidentally identical text collapsed onto the same `@id`, silently merging distinct entities and their assertions; (2) because the hash was computed bottom-up through the tree, any edit to a leaf changed the hash of every ancestor up to the artifact root, orphaning assertions on whole sections for a single-word edit anywhere beneath them.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002P"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002N",
    "text": "Resolved by decoupling the two entirely: `@id` is now a Snowflake-style generated identifier, assigned once and fully independent of content (§1.A) — collision-avoidance is deterministic (timestamp + machine identity + sequence counter), not derived from what the node contains. The content fingerprint (§1.B) turned out not to need a recursive/structural hash at all.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002P",
    "text": "A first attempt at §1.B proposed reusing the bottom-up construction from this section — a recursive, per-`BlockNode` `treeHash` — for three things: detecting the DB-ahead-of-file case (by comparing it against `fileHash`), scoping reconciliation to only the diverged subtrees, and cross-checking the identity-matcher's decisions. The first use rested on a category error: `fileHash` hashes a flat string and a tree fingerprint hashes a nested structure — outputs of different functions over different domains, never meaningfully comparable regardless of whether real drift exists. Once that's gone, the DB-ahead-of-file case is better solved by direct on-write signaling instead (§1.B), and the remaining two uses (reconciliation scoping, cross-check) didn't justify a persisted, per-edit-maintained field on their own — reconciliation is rare and artifacts are bounded, human-document-sized trees, so a best-effort matcher can simply run over the whole tree each time without a hash-based pre-filter. Dropped entirely, not kept even as an on-demand technique — leaving two flat file-domain hashes (`fileHash`/`ingestedHash`) as the whole of the fingerprint design, and reconciliation itself resolved at the strategy level as best-effort content matching over the whole tree, with the specific matching algorithm left as an implementation detail.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### G. Separating Artifact/Folder Identity from Location",
    "text": "`ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` were originally Lexical-keyed on `path` — not a deliberated design decision, just the obvious, simple choice made in an early, undesigned prototyping pass, before this document's identity/fingerprint rigor (§1, Appendix F) existed at all.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002E",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002T"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002R",
    "text": "On inspection, while designing reconciliation matching (see `Aperas-reconciliation-matching-design.md`), this turned out to be the same category error Appendix F already fixed once for `BlockNode`, just on a different axis: identity conflated with a mutable property. There, the mutable property was content; here, it's location. A file or folder rename is a routine edit, exactly like a paragraph edit, not an identity change — but `path`-as-`@id` makes every rename destructive by construction, since the `@id` is *recomputed* from the new path, orphaning the old document's identity and everything that ever referenced it directly. The only way to route around that self-inflicted damage is to detect the rename after the fact and manually transplant `root` onto a freshly-identified document — real, working, but entirely unnecessary machinery, built to compensate for the identity scheme rather than because the problem demanded it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002Q",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002S",
    "text": "Resolved the same way as Appendix F: `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` both get a Snowflake-generated identity (§1.A), and `path` becomes an ordinary mutable field. A detected rename (matched by content, same as everything else — see §4.A/B's `title`/`text` and the reconciliation doc's file/folder-matching design) becomes a field update on the same document, not a new identity; anything that referenced the artifact or folder by id keeps working with no transplant needed. `root` only ever needed transplanting because the container around it couldn't survive a move — fix the container, and the transplant machinery becomes unnecessary for this case (still needed for the case where a match genuinely fails and a new identity is warranted).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002Q",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ5Y002T",
    "text": "Two costs were weighed before committing to this: losing the free, deterministic `id`-from-`path` lookup, and losing the database-enforced guarantee that no two documents can claim the same path. The first turned out not to be a cost at all — benchmarked, a `path`-filtered query came back faster than the direct id lookup it would replace (~7.5ms vs ~48ms at 50 documents). The second is real in shape (an application-level check-then-upsert race could in principle create duplicate `path`s) but not practically significant given how this project is actually deployed — one local TerminusDB per machine, reconciled via fetch/push, not a single server multiple machines write into concurrently; worth remembering if that deployment model ever changes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ5Y002Q",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Deep Path Resolution & `kg:resolve` — Design",
    "text": "**Status**: both directions are **implemented and live-verified** — `kg:path` (id→path, §8) and\n`kg:resolve` (path→id, §1-7, including `--base`, `--create-holder`, and the `kg:tree`/`kg:search`\nbackport in §7.5) — via `nodeRef.ts`'s `resolveNodeRefDetail`/`resolveNodeRefOrNull`.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW970000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00K",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01M"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 0. What this is",
    "text": "Path resolution is fundamentally one operation: converting a relative, human-typed path into an\nabsolute node code (a full `BlockNode/ArtifactNode/FolderNode` id) — the \"query-based addressing\"\ntier `nodeRef.ts` already provides (`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md` §2). Today that\nconversion stops at the file/folder boundary: `getArtifactRecord`/`getFolderRecord` resolve a\npath to an `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`, and nothing carries a path further into that artifact's\nown block tree. This extends the same conversion one more tier — `folder/file.md/heading/title`\n— and gives it a command of its own, since nothing currently exposes resolution directly; every\nexisting caller only ever sees it as a side effect of some other command (`kg:assert`, `kg:tree`,\n...).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Command",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG004",
    "text": "```\nkg:resolve [--base <path>] <path> [<path>...]\nkg:resolve [--base <path>] --create-holder <path> --titles <title> [<title>...]\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG003",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG005",
    "text": "The first form resolves each `<path>` through every tier (`Aperas-basic-assertion-skill-design.md`\n§2, extended by §3-4 below) and prints one line per input, in order — id, kind, and title, same\none-line shape `kg:tree --depth 0` already uses. Exits non-zero with a clear reason on a miss\n(unresolvable prefix, or an ambiguous segment — §4).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG006",
    "text": "Multiple `<path>` arguments are accepted specifically so `--base` (§2.1) pulls its weight:\nresolving many paths against the same base in one call, rather than re-resolving the base from\nscratch — and re-typing it — for each one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG007",
    "text": "The second form is `--create-holder` (§7): single-path only, and shaped differently on purpose —\nsee §7.1 for why it needs its own `--titles` list rather than reusing `<path>`'s own segments.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG003",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6D8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6D8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 2. Path grammar",
    "text": "`/`-separated segments, Linux-flavored:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG009",
    "text": "**Leading `/`** → absolute: anchored at the tracked artifacts root, ignoring any `--base`\n  (§2.1) in effect. This is also what every path meant before `--base` existed — the only\n  grammar there was, so nothing about existing (bare, no-`--base`) callers changes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00A",
    "text": "**No leading `/`** → relative to the base in effect: `--base <path>` if given, the implicit\n  base (§2.1) for callers that have one (wikilink extraction), or the artifacts root by default —\n  which is exactly today's behavior, so a bare path with no `--base` in play resolves exactly as\n  it always has.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00B",
    "text": "**`.`** → the base itself. Mostly useful as `kg:resolve --base <path> .` to print what a base\n  resolved to.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00C",
    "text": "**`..`** → the base's own parent, one level up (§2.1's walk mechanics) — chainable (`../..`)\n  like any real filesystem.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00D",
    "text": "No `#`-anchor syntax, no special marker splitting \"file part\" from \"block part\" beyond the above:\nper the unbounded-tree principle (`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §4), there's no structural\nboundary between a folder segment, a file segment, and a heading segment to mark differently in\nthe first place — `/`, `.`, and `..` are the only reserved tokens, and each means the same thing\nat every tier. (A `BlockNode` genuinely titled `.` or `..` becomes unaddressable by title at that\nposition — the same trade a real filesystem already makes.)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6DG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6DG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### 2.1 `--base` and walking `..`",
    "text": "`--base <path>` resolves first, through the full algorithm (§3-4), then each subsequent `<path>`\nargument is resolved relative to that node. `..` moves up one level from wherever the walk\ncurrently stands:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG008",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00F",
    "text": "Within a `BlockNode` tree, `..` reads `.parent` (§8's field) — the same hop `resolveIdToPath`\n  already walks, just in reverse.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00G",
    "text": "At the artifact/folder tier, `BlockNode.parent` doesn't apply — `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`\n  carry no `.parent` field, only a stored `path` string (§3). `..` there trims the last\n  `/`-segment off that string and re-resolves it via `getFolderRecord` — a property of the path,\n  not a field on the node, same as an ordinary filesystem.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00H",
    "text": "`..` past the artifacts root is a miss (\"already at root\"), not a silent no-op.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00J",
    "text": "The base supplies the \"which artifact, which subtree\" that every relative path would otherwise\nhave to repeat: rather than typing `sub/file.md/Heading/Sub A` and `sub/file.md/Heading/Sub B` in\nfull, `kg:resolve --base sub/file.md/Heading \"Sub A\" \"Sub B\"` says the shared prefix once.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00K",
    "text": "Callers that don't go through the CLI at all — wikilink extraction, primarily (`astParser.ts`'s\n`resolveBlockLinks`) — get an *implicit* base: the path of the block containing the\n`[[wikilink]]` text, not the path of that block's owning artifact. A `[[wikilink]]` is written\nfrom a specific place in the tree, and the most-likely-intended target is judged relative to that\nplace — the same way a relative filesystem path is judged relative to the file that contains it,\nnot to some project root; `[[../Sibling Section]]` written inside one heading's subtree to reach\na sibling heading is exactly the case this exists for. Getting \"the containing block's own path\"\nis itself `resolveIdToPath` (§8) — so the wikilink extractor ends up a consumer of both\ndirections of this design, not just the forward one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6E8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6E8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 3. Segmentation algorithm",
    "text": "The stored `path` field on `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` is one exact-match string\n(`artifacts.ts:235`, `folders.ts:141` — `query: { path: ... }`), not resolved segment-by-segment,\nso a multi-segment input like `sub/file.md/Some Heading` can't be split up front — `sub/file.md`\nis itself one atomic stored path. Resolve by longest-prefix search instead:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00N",
    "text": "Split the input on `/` into segments `[s0, s1, ..., sN]`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00P",
    "text": "Try progressively shorter prefixes, longest first: `s0/../sN`, then `s0/../s(N-1)`, ... down to\n   `s0` alone. For each candidate prefix, try `getArtifactRecord` then `getFolderRecord` (same\n   try-both-they-never-collide order every other caller already uses).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00Q",
    "text": "The first prefix that resolves is the base node; every segment *after* it is a slug segment to\n   descend through the base node's block tree, one `BlockNode` child per segment (§4).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00R",
    "text": "No prefix resolves at all → the existing \"not a tracked artifact or folder path\" miss.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CG00S",
    "text": "This is a pure extension: an input with no trailing slug segments behaves exactly as today\n(longest-prefix search immediately finds the whole thing, zero slug segments to descend).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR000",
    "text": "**Confirmed live**: the synthetic `\"Document Root\"` wrapper every artifact's tree starts with\n(astParser.ts's `convertAstNode`, `type: 'root'`) is a real, addressable segment here, not skipped\n— an `ArtifactNode`'s one descendable child (§4) is that wrapper, not the real first heading\nunderneath it. This matches `resolveIdToPath` (§8), which already walks through it as an ordinary\nhop, so a full path from `kg:path` and one typed into `kg:resolve` round-trip consistently — e.g.\n`file.md/document-root/-my-heading`, not `file.md/-my-heading`. A `FolderNode` has no such\nwrapper (its children — README content and nested folders/artifacts alike — sit directly in its\nown `children` list), so this only ever applies one hop deep, right after an `ArtifactNode`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6ER000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6ER001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## 4. Descending slug segments",
    "text": "For each remaining segment, filter the current node's direct `children` to `BlockNode`s whose\n`title`, slugified (§5), matches the segment slugified (§5 applied to both sides, so a caller can\ntype either raw heading text or an already-slugified form and it matches either way). Matching is\ntwo-tier, exact before prefix — the same precedence a short git hash resolves under (a full hash\nthat happens to also be a prefix of some other object's hash still just names the object it\nexactly matches):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR002",
    "text": "**Exact**: candidates whose slugified title equals the segment exactly. Exactly one → descend,\n   done — a longer sibling that happens to start with the same text never interferes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR003",
    "text": "**Prefix**, tried only if step 1 found none: candidates whose slugified title *starts with* the\n   segment. Lets a caller type `setup` for a child titled `Setup Guide` without knowing the rest,\n   the same convenience `git show a1b2c3` gets from a hash prefix — as long as it's the *only*\n   sibling starting that way.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6F0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6F0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR004",
    "text": "At either tier:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR005",
    "text": "**Zero matches** → miss, same as an unresolvable prefix.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR006",
    "text": "**Exactly one match** → descend, continue to the next segment.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR007",
    "text": "**More than one match** → decline rather than guess (the same principle `reconcile.ts`'s\n  matching already follows for ambiguous content) — fail with an error naming the id and title of\n  every sibling that matched, rather than silently picking one. Two siblings with identical titles\n  fail at step 1 without ever falling through to step 2 — prefix matching resolves an\n  *abbreviation*, it never papers over a genuine duplicate.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR008",
    "text": "This matches on *any* `BlockNode.title` uniformly, not just headings — a non-heading block titled\nvia `kg:title` is addressable by that title the same way a heading is, consistent with this\nproject's repeated \"don't special-case by node kind without a real reason\" rule\n(`Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §4's pitfall note; `Aperas-interactive-summarization-design.md`\n§5).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR009",
    "text": "**Real bug found live during implementation**: a heading's stored `title` keeps its raw `#`/`##`\nmarker (astParser.ts's convention), which §5's blanket slugify rule turns into *leading dashes* —\nharmless for exact matching (both sides get the same treatment) but fatal for prefix matching as\nfirst implemented: `## Setup Guide` slugifies to `---setup-guide`, so typing `setup` (a caller has\nno reason to know or type the heading's own depth) never matched via plain `startsWith`, making\nthe whole prefix tier dead on arrival for the dominant addressable-content case. Fixed by stripping\nleading dashes off the *candidate* before the `startsWith` check, prefix tier only — exact matching\nis untouched.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00A",
    "text": "**Reserved tokens apply per-segment, not just to a whole `--base`**: before either tier, a segment\nthat is exactly `.` or `..` is never looked up as a title at all — it's the structural move §2.1\nalready defines (stay, or go to `.parent`), wherever it appears in the path, not only when typed\nalone or as `--base`. `Heading A/../Heading B` mid-path works the same way `../` does at the very\nfront. A real `BlockNode` titled `.` or `..` is simply unreachable via that segment position (§2's\nfootnote) — one more instance of the trade a real filesystem already makes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 5. Slugify",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00D"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00C",
    "text": "```\nslugify(text) = text.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '-')\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00B",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00D",
    "text": "One rule, deliberately not collapsing runs or trimming edges: every individual non-alphanumeric\ncharacter becomes its own `-`, preserving exact length/position rather than folding different\ninputs toward the same output — maximal distinction over cosmetic prettiness. No special-casing\nfor a heading's leading `#`/`##` marker either; under this blanket rule it's just another\ncharacter that becomes a `-` like any other, no AST-awareness needed. Applied to both the\ncandidate `BlockNode.title` and the query segment before comparing, so a `kg:title`-set title\nwith no markdown syntax at all and a raw heading title match on equal footing — e.g. a raw\nheading title (`\"# Interactive Title & Cross-Link Authoring — Design\"`) slugifies to\n`-interactive-title---cross-link-authoring---design`, hyphen-for-character, no cleanup.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00B",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 6. Where this plugs in",
    "text": "**The shared primitive**: `nodeRef.ts`'s `resolveNodeRefOrNull(client, path)` gains an options\nparameter — `resolveNodeRefOrNull(client, path, opts?: { base?: string; createHolder?: boolean;\ntitles?: string[] })` — carrying §2's grammar (`/`, `.`, `..`), §3's longest-prefix search, §4's\nexact-then-prefix descent, and §7's holder creation, all in one place, added to the function's\nexisting tier-3 path fallback.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00J"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00F",
    "text": "Every command that already resolves a `<path>` argument through the plain form —\n`kg:assert`/`kg:assertions`/`kg:unassert`, `kg:tree`, `kg:unfold`/`kg:fold`, `kg:title`, `kg:link`\n— gains deep addressing (§3-4) for free, with no `opts` of their own to supply; none of them need\n`--base` or `--create-holder`, so `opts` simply stays empty for those calls.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00G",
    "text": "**`kg:resolve`** is the new command that exposes this directly, for inspection or scripting,\nrather than only as a side effect of some other operation — the CLI-level home for `--base` and\n`--create-holder` (§1), which are nothing more than `opts.base`/`opts.createHolder`/`opts.titles`\nparsed off `process.argv`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00H",
    "text": "**The wikilink extractor** (`astParser.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks`) calls `resolveNodeRefOrNull`\ndirectly, bypassing the CLI entirely — it already has everything `opts` needs without prompting\nanyone: `opts.base` from the containing block's own path (via `resolveIdToPath`, §8),\n`opts.createHolder: true` unconditionally (§7.3), and `opts.titles` from splitting the wikilink's\nraw text on `/` (§7.1) — one source feeding both the matching path and the holder titles.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00J",
    "text": "**Not touched**: `kg:project` (`kgCli.ts:233-242`) calls `getArtifactRecord`/`getFolderRecord`\ndirectly rather than through `resolveNodeRefOrNull`, because it needs the record itself (to know\nthe on-disk target file path to write), not just a resolved id. No change needed there — it never\naddresses into the block tree, only ever the artifact/folder itself.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 7. Holder nodes — resolving a miss into a placeholder, not just an error",
    "text": "§4's \"decline rather than guess\" covers *ambiguity*. A miss is different: the path is\nunambiguous, there's just nothing there yet. Declining is still the right default — most callers\n(`kg:assert`, a human typing `kg:resolve` at a prompt) want a clear error, not a surprise node\nappearing in the tree. But two real callers want the opposite: a way to *mark* the still-missing\ntarget so something later can find and fill it in, rather than losing the reference entirely.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Y",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR014",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR019",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01C"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.1 What gets created, and why `--titles` is separate from `<path>`",
    "text": "`--create-holder` creates one node per unresolved segment, continuing to descend into each\nnewly-created one for whatever remains — `mkdir -p`, not just the last component. Segments that\nalready resolve are left untouched; only the miss point onward gets created, `holder: true` set\non each. Every holder `BlockNode` created this way is `type: 'heading'` — not a default among\nseveral possible shapes, the only one supported; see §7.4 for why, and for the one thing it rules\nout.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00K",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00X"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00N",
    "text": "`<path>` is still what does the *matching* (§4, prefix included) — but §4's prefix matching means\na segment on the way down may be a deliberate abbreviation (`setup` addressing `Setup Guide`), and\nusing that abbreviation verbatim as a new node's permanent title would be wrong: a holder is new,\nreal, lasting content, not a lookup key. So `--create-holder` requires a parallel `--titles` list —\nbut aligned from the *tail*, not the head, and scoped to only the segments that can even use one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00P",
    "text": "**Which segments need a title at all**: only the heading-tier ones (§4's slug-descent). The\nartifact/folder tier (§3) never does — `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.title` isn't a matching key or\nan authored value anywhere in this codebase, it's a pure derivation from the path segment itself\n(`trackArtifact`, `artifacts.ts:127`: `title: basename(artifactPath)`; the folder-tracking\nequivalent, `folders.ts:44`: `relPath.split('/').pop()!` — both recomputed unconditionally on\nevery track/rebuild; the real match key is `path`, looked up exactly). A holder `ArtifactNode`/\n`FolderNode` gets that same derived title immediately, the moment it's created, with nothing to\nsupply — and when the real file is eventually tracked, `basename` overwrites it again regardless,\nso there was never anything worth preserving there to begin with. `.`/`..` tokens (§2) don't need\none either — they never look anything up.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00Q",
    "text": "**Alignment, tail-first**: `--titles` lines up against the heading-tier segment sequence `S`\n(§4's segments only — base's own trailing heading segments too, if `--base` reaches that far,\n§7.2) from the *end*: the last title pairs with the deepest segment, the second-to-last with the\nnext one up, and so on, for as many titles as given. Segments shallower than the shortest prefix\n`--titles` reaches are assumed to already exist — the caller only supplies titles for as deep as\nit actually expects to be creating, not one for every segment regardless of need. Resolution still\ntries the normal match (§4) at *every* segment first, title-covered or not: a title-covered\nsegment that turns out to already exist just leaves that title unused, same as before; an\nuncovered (shallower) segment that unexpectedly misses is a plain error — \"would need to be\ncreated, no title available for it\" — the same \"decline rather than guess\" answer as any other\nmiss, `--create-holder` or not. `--titles` longer than `S` is a usage error up front (extra titles\nwith nothing left to pair against); shorter is fine by construction — that's the whole point.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00R:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6G0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00R:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6G0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00R",
    "text": "Both callers this exists for already have the full titles on hand for free, so this isn't the\nceremony it looks like at the call site:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00T"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00S",
    "text": "**The wikilink extractor** already has the wikilink's raw, unslugified text — splitting it on\n  `/` gives both `<path>` (via §5's slugify, for matching) and `--titles` (that same split, used\n  as-is) from one source, no separate authoring step.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00T",
    "text": "**A \"vision drawer\"** (§7.3) is, by definition, working from real intended titles — that's the\n  content it's sketching, not an afterthought.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00R",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00V",
    "text": "A direct human `kg:resolve --create-holder` call still has to type both `<path>` and `--titles`,\nbut tail-alignment keeps it to just the segments actually being created — no need to restate\ntitles for a path prefix that already resolves.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00W",
    "text": "**Real bug found live**: `ArtifactNode`'s one \"child\" is its singular `root` field, not a\n`children` List — the first implementation always wrote `children: [...]` regardless of the\ncurrent node's kind, which is fine for `BlockNode`/`FolderNode` but fails schema check\n(`unknown_property_for_type`) the moment a miss needs a holder created directly under an\n`ArtifactNode` (reachable via `..` popping up past a block tree into its owning artifact — §2.1).\nFixed by branching on kind: an `ArtifactNode` with no `root` yet gets the holder set as `root`\ndirectly; one that already has a `root` declines instead (there's no structural slot for a second,\nsibling root — a real edit adds *inside* the existing root, not beside it).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00X",
    "text": "**Output**: one line per segment, not just the final id — §1's plain form only ever prints the\nend result because nothing changed along the way, but `--create-holder` can touch several levels\nat once, and which ones were already there versus newly imagined is exactly what a caller needs to\nsee. Each line carries the same `<id>  [<kind>]  <title>` shape as everywhere else, tagged\n`(existing)` or `(created holder)`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Y",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.2 How far \"imagined\" reaches: the whole tree, not just blocks",
    "text": "Holder creation isn't bounded to the `BlockNode` tier. If §3's longest-prefix search fails\noutright — no tracked `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` matches any prefix at all — `--create-holder`\nextends the same `mkdir -p` logic up through that tier too: a whole path, folders and file and\nheadings alike, can be sketched into existence with nothing on disk yet. This is what lets a\n\"vision drawer\" (§7.3) actually draw a *tree*, not just fill gaps inside artifacts that already\nexist.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00K",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Z",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR010",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR011",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR012",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR013"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Z",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR00Z",
    "text": "Disambiguating which imagined segments are `FolderNode`s versus the one `ArtifactNode` versus\n`BlockNode`s beneath it reuses the convention every real path in this project already follows: the\nsegment carrying this project's file extension (`.md`) is the `ArtifactNode`; segments before it\nare `FolderNode`s; segments after it are `BlockNode`s. No new marker syntax — one already exists\nin every path anyone types here.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Y",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR010",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR010",
    "text": "A holder `ArtifactNode` has no real file on disk and no content hash; it's a placeholder for a\nfile that will eventually be written and tracked (`kg:track`+`kg:ingest`) the normal way. Because\n`FolderNode`/`ArtifactNode` need the same `holder` marker `BlockNode` does, it belongs on\n`BaseNode` rather than repeated per concrete kind — the same \"don't special-case by node kind\"\ndefault §4 already follows for title-addressing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Y",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR011",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR011",
    "text": "`--base` composes with `--create-holder` the same way as any other segment: if the base path\nitself doesn't resolve, it's imagined too, root included — being \"the base\" doesn't make it any\nless eligible than a segment reached by descending into it. No separate title list for it either:\n§7.1's tail-aligned `S` is base's own heading-tier segments followed by `<path>`'s, one combined\nsequence in resolution order — `--titles` aligns against the tail of *that*, so the common case\n(base already fully exists, only `<path>` needs creating) costs nothing extra, and the rare case\n(base itself needs imagining too) is just more of the same sequence, not a different mechanism.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Y",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR012",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR012",
    "text": "**Schema: a field, not a `Prop`.** `holder: { \"@type\": \"Optional\", \"@class\": \"xsd:boolean\" }` on\n`BaseNode` — not an entry in the existing `props: Set<Prop>` (`props.ts`'s `StringProp` et al.).\n`props` is reserved for heterogeneous, mdast/frontmatter-derived metadata read back as an opaque\n`{key, value}` bag — a list's `ordered`/`startIndex`, frontmatter serialized as one whole entry\n(`astParser.ts`, `artifacts.ts:316-318`) — content the parser happens to carry, not core ontology\nstate. `holder` is the opposite: a first-class concept needed on every node of every kind, checked\non essentially every resolve and worth checking on every traversal that wants to honor it — the\nsame reasoning `parent` (§8) already settled for this schema: a real typed field beats a value\nburied in a generic collection once the concept is universal rather than incidental per-node data.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Y",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR013",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR013",
    "text": "`kg:tree`/`kg:search` mark and can filter holders — settled in §7.5, not left open.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00Y",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR014",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.3 The two callers this exists for",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00K",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR015"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR015",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR015:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6H0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR015:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6H0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR015",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR014",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR016",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR018"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR016",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR016",
    "text": "**The wikilink extractor** (`artifacts.ts`'s `resolveBlockLinks` — moved there from `astParser.ts`\n  during implementation, since resolution needs DB access a pure parser doesn't have; §2.1's\n  implicit base): today a `[[wikilink]]` to a heading that hasn't been written yet has nowhere to\n  point and the reference is simply dropped. With `--create-holder` (passed automatically here — a\n  forward reference is exactly the case this exists for, not a per-call user choice), it resolves\n  to a holder instead, and the `[[wikilink]]`-predicate `Link`\n  (`Aperas-interactive-summarization-design.md`) points at something real.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR015",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR017"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR017",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR017",
    "text": "**Real bug found live, more fundamental than a typo**: resolving links *before* the tree's own\n  write (the original ordering) is a genuine chicken-and-egg failure, not just a style choice —\n  the implicit base (`resolveIdToPath` on the containing block) only works on an already-persisted\n  `.parent` chain, so anything inside the artifact currently being ingested would still be resolved\n  against the *previous* ingestion's stale tree, missing whatever's brand new in this very edit.\n  Fixed by moving link resolution to a separate pass *after* the big write (extracting and\n  stripping each block's raw `linkCodes` beforehand — schema.json has no such field, so leaving it\n  in for the write itself also fails schema check — then resolving and patching each affected\n  block by id once the tree is queryable), at the cost of one extra write per block that actually\n  has a wikilink. A second, smaller bug in the same area: building `--titles` as a raw\n  `code.split('/')` overshoots §7.1's tail-alignment count whenever the code contains `..`/`.`\n  tokens (§2) — fixed by filtering those out before building the titles list, since only name\n  segments ever consume one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR016",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR018",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR018",
    "text": "**A \"vision drawer\"**: a not-yet-built, not otherwise specified tool for sketching an intended\n  tree shape — folders, files, and headings with no real content yet — ahead of writing any of it.\n  `--create-holder` is the primitive such a tool would need, all the way from the artifacts root\n  (§7.2) down to a single heading: resolve/create the intended structure first, populate it later.\n  Whether that tool ends up an interactive `kg:` command (in the spirit of `kg:title`/`kg:link`)\n  or something else is out of scope here; the point is the primitive it would need already falls\n  out of this flag without a separate design.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR015",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.4 Why holders are heading-only",
    "text": "A holder `BlockNode` is always created with `type: 'heading'` (§7.1) — that's what makes\nreconciliation need no new design at all: `reconcile.ts`'s `leafKey` — its Stage A match key — is\n`node.title` for a heading and `node.text` for anything else (`reconcile.ts:96-98`). A holder's\n`.title` is already the real, intended title (§7.1's whole reason `--titles` exists), so when\nreal content finally arrives with a matching heading, Stage A's Gestalt match keys on title\nequality and matches the holder exactly like it would match any unedited heading being carried\nforward — same key, same code path; `carryForwardFields` (`reconcile.ts:112`) reuses the holder's\n`blockId` on the incoming real node with zero extra logic. Empty `text` never enters into it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00K",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01A",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01A",
    "text": "A non-heading titled block (the `kg:title` style of addressable-but-not-a-heading block) keys on\n`text` instead, and an empty `text` won't Gestalt-match a real populated one automatically —\nthere's no working reconciliation path for that case, so `--create-holder` doesn't offer it: a\nsegment that would need a non-heading holder still declines, same as any miss without the flag.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR019",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01B",
    "text": "This restriction sits only at the bottom of the descent (§4's slug tier) — the artifact/folder\ntier above it (§7.2) is unrestricted, root included. `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` reconcile by\npath/abstract similarity (`matchLeftoverByAbstract`, `reconcile.ts:368`), not by this\nheading-vs-text key distinction, so nothing about imagining a whole tree from the root down\ndepends on this choice — only what a leaf holder is allowed to become.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR019",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### 7.5 Marking and filtering in `kg:tree` / `kg:search`",
    "text": "§7.2 left this open; settling it now, since both commands already have a natural place for it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR00K",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01D",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01G",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01D:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6H8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01D:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6HG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01D",
    "text": "**The mark**: one more trailing field on the line, the same convention `--create-holder`'s own\noutput (§7.1) already uses for exactly this distinction:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01F"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01E",
    "text": "`kg:tree`: `<id>  [<kind>]  <title>  (holder)` — `printTree` (`kgCli.ts:63-79`) already fetches\n  the full doc to read `.title`; reading `.holder` off that same fetch costs nothing new.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01F",
    "text": "`kg:search`: `<id>  [<kind>]  <field>  <value>  (holder)` — `searchNodes` (`woql.ts:120`)\n  returns a bare `{id, field, value}` straight from a WOQL triple query, no full document read.\n  The print loop (`kgCli.ts:320-327`) already does one `getNode` fetch per match, but today only\n  conditionally, for `BlockNode`'s `type` label (`if (label === 'BlockNode')`) — reading `.holder`\n  needs that same fetch unconditionally, for every match kind, not only `BlockNode`. Accepted\n  cost: one extra `getNode` round trip per `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` match that previously\n  skipped it entirely (`BlockNode` matches already paid this).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6HG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01G:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6HG002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01G",
    "text": "**The filter**: `--no-holders` on both commands, print-time only — neither command's underlying\nquery changes, since a match or a tree node still has to be fetched/visited to know whether it's\na holder in the first place.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01H",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01J"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01H",
    "text": "`kg:search --no-holders`: matches are a flat list, so this just drops holder matches before\n  printing — nothing else to reconcile.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01J",
    "text": "`kg:tree --no-holders`: matches are a tree, so dropping is per-node, not per-subtree. §7.2\n  already established holders exist at per-node granularity, not all-or-nothing (a still-holder\n  `FolderNode` can have a real `ArtifactNode` underneath it once that one piece gets tracked), so\n  hiding a holder's own line can't also hide what's beneath it. `printTree` recurses into a\n  filtered node's children at the *same* depth it would have used for the hidden node itself — the\n  holder becomes transparent for indentation purposes, not a pruning point. A holder whose entire\n  subtree is also holders (the common case: one `mkdir -p` chain, nothing real underneath it yet)\n  simply produces no output for that whole branch, one hidden line at a time — reading the same as\n  pruning, without a separate subtree-level check to get wrong.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01G",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01K",
    "text": "Without `--create-holder` (the default), a miss behaves exactly as §3/§4 already specify — no\nnode is created, resolution simply declines.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01C",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01M",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 8. `kg:path` — the reverse direction (id→path) — **implemented and live-verified**",
    "text": "Checked first whether `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` §3.A's \"native backlinks\" claim already\ngave this for free: it doesn't. Confirmed live: `t(X, 'links', <a real Link id>)` correctly\nreturns the owning `BlockNode`, but `t(X, 'children', <a real child id>)` returns **nothing**,\neven though that id is genuinely in its parent's `children`. `Set`-typed fields produce a direct\ntriple; `List`-typed ones (`BlockNode.children`, `ArtifactNode.root`, `FolderNode.children` — the\ntree's actual containment, `List` rather than `Set` because child order is semantically\nmeaningful) don't — TerminusDB represents `List` membership through cons-cell/RDF-list\nindirection, not a direct `Parent predicate Child` triple. §3.A has been corrected to state this\nprecisely rather than leave the disproven generalization standing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01V",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01W",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01X"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6J0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6J0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01N",
    "text": "**The fix**: a direct `parent: Optional<BaseNode>` field on `BlockNode` (`schema.json`) — not a\n`parentId: xsd:string` requiring kind-guessing to become useful, a real typed reference read\nstraight off the document, same as `Link`/`Assertion`'s `target`/`source` already work. Points at\nthe direct container's full id — another `BlockNode`, or the owning `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`\nfor a tree's own root.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01P",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01Q",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01R",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01S",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01T"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01P",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01P",
    "text": "**`astParser.ts`**: `stampParents`, a post-pass over the *finished* tree (not threaded through\n  construction) setting `child.parent` for every direct child. Exported, not auto-run only once —\n  see the next bug.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01Q",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01Q",
    "text": "**`artifacts.ts`**: the tree's own root has no parent from parsing alone (nothing inside the\n  parser knows the owning `ArtifactNode`) — stamped externally: `finalRoot.parent =\n  ArtifactNode/<artifactId>`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01R",
    "text": "**`folders.ts`**: README absorption relocates top-level parsed blocks directly into\n  `FolderNode.children`, bypassing a root block entirely — those get re-stamped to\n  `FolderNode/<folderId>`, overriding what `astParser.ts` set before the splice.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01S",
    "text": "**`nodeRef.ts`**: `resolveIdToPath` walks `.parent` up to the first `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`,\n  collecting each hop's slugified `title`, then prepends that node's own `path` (already the full\n  nested path from the artifacts root — no further upward walk needed once reached). `slugify`\n  (§5) lives here too, shared with the not-yet-built `kg:resolve` side.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01T",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01T",
    "text": "**`kgCli.ts`**: `kg:path <id>`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01N",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01V",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01V",
    "text": "**Real bug found and fixed before this worked at all**: `stampParents` originally ran once,\npre-reconciliation, inside `parseMarkdownTree`. `reconcile.ts`'s `carryForwardFields` then\nreassigns `blockId` for every matched pair (the fresh parse's transient id → the old carried-\nforward one) — so every already-stamped child's `.parent` pointed at its container's *discarded*\npre-reassignment id, a reference to a document that never actually gets written. Confirmed live as\n`references_untyped_object` (`SchemaCheckFailure`) on the very next real re-ingest of any existing\nartifact — not a hypothetical. Fixed by calling `stampParents` a second time in `artifacts.ts`,\n*after* reconciliation, on the final tree.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01W",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01W",
    "text": "**Second real bug, found by `verify:phase0 -- --db`'s own cleanup step failing**: `parent` makes\n`ArtifactNode`↔root-`BlockNode` a genuine reference cycle for the first time (root points down via\n`children`/`root`, now also points up via `parent`) — the same class of cycle\n`findLinkIdsTargeting`'s doc comment already covers for `Link`↔`BlockNode`, with the same fix:\ndelete everything in the cycle together, in one combined batch, not the `ArtifactNode` separately\nand first. `verifyPhase0.ts`'s `resetDemoState` did exactly the latter; fixed to match.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01X",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6CR01X",
    "text": "**Migration caveat, same pattern as every prior schema addition in this project**: `parent` is\n`Optional`, so existing content isn't broken, but a `BlockNode` ingested before this field existed\nhas no `parent` set until its artifact is actually re-ingested (content-hash-unchanged artifacts\nare correctly skipped by `kg:ingest`, so this doesn't happen automatically). `kg:path` reports a\nclear, specific miss for this case rather than crashing — confirmed live against both a freshly\nre-ingested block (full path resolved correctly, multiple levels deep) and a stale one (correct,\nactionable miss message).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6CR01M",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# The Aperas Extensibility Model: Apeiron to Perata",
    "text": "Aperas is not a monolithic application with a single, closed database; it is an engine for growing forms (Perata) out of a formless core (Apeiron).",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW978000",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR002",
    "text": "Because there will be many different Domain KGs (e.g., `ArchitectureKG`, `PersonalKG`, `ProjectX_KG`), the boundary between the Core and the Domain must support deep **extension and refinability**.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 1. Core vs. Community Governance",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6M0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR004:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6M0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR004",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR003",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR006"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR005",
    "text": "**Aperas (The Apeiron Core):** Managed by the Core Dev Team. It provides the universal potential: the database driver, the base universal schemas (`BaseNode`, `BaseLink`, `BaseEdge`, `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`), and the infrastructure tooling (like `restore.sh`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR006",
    "text": "**A Domain KG (The Perata):** Managed by the Community. A specific KG repository (like `AperasKG`) is a specific materialization. It contains the raw content (`artifacts/`), **and** the specific logic that defines its shape (its Schemas, Ontologies, and Transducers).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "## 2. Mechanisms of Extension (OOP & Composition)",
    "text": "The technical implementation of this extensibility leverages Object-Oriented polymorphism in TerminusDB and functional composition in TypeScript.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00H"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR008:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6M8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR008:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6M8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### A. Schema Extension (`@inherits`)",
    "text": "Domain KGs can define custom node shapes that natively inherit properties from the Aperas Core nodes using the `@inherits` JSON-LD keyword.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR007",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR009",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR009",
    "text": "**Property Inheritance**: A domain `RequirementNode` inheriting from `BlockNode` automatically gains `title`, `text`, and `children`, plus its own specific fields like `priority`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR00A",
    "text": "**Subsumption (Polymorphism)**: TerminusDB natively understands this inheritance Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG). A query for `BlockNode` will automatically return all `RequirementNode` instances. *(See the official [TerminusDB Schema Reference Guide](https://terminusdb.org/docs/schema-reference-guide/#inherits) for details).*",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### B. Ontology Extension (Edge Subclassing)",
    "text": "Aperas adopts **Edge Subclassing (OOP)** for defining predicates and relationships. \nInstead of relying on fragile string fields, relationships are modeled as abstract schema classes (e.g., `BaseEdge`). A Domain KG can define specific relationships that inherit from these bases:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR007",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00D"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR00C",
    "text": "```json\n{ \"@id\": \"AffectsEdge\", \"@type\": \"Class\", \"@inherits\": [\"BaseEdge\"], \"source\": \"BlockNode\", \"target\": \"BlockNode\" }\n{ \"@id\": \"BlocksEdge\", \"@type\": \"Class\", \"@inherits\": [\"AffectsEdge\"] }\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00B",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00D:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6MG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00D:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6MG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR00D",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00B",
    "type": "list",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00E"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR00E",
    "text": "**WOQL Subsumption Query**: Because the edge is an object in a hierarchy, you query it using `isa()`, which natively understands subsumption. *(See the [WOQL Class Reference Guide](https://terminusdb.org/docs/woql-class-reference-guide/#is-a) for details on `isa`)*.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00F",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00G"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00F",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR00F",
    "text": "```javascript\n    WOQL.isa(\"v:EdgeId\", \"AffectsEdge\")\n        .triple(\"v:EdgeId\", \"source\", \"v:NodeA\")\n        .triple(\"v:EdgeId\", \"target\", \"v:NodeB\")\n    ```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00E",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00G",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR00G",
    "text": "This single query automatically fetches all `AffectsEdge` instances **and** all domain-specific `BlocksEdge` instances seamlessly, with strict typing constraints.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00H",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00H:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6MR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00H:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6N0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "### C. Transducer Extension (Pipeline & Class Overrides)",
    "text": "Because transducers are TypeScript logic, they extend via standard programming patterns:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR007",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00J",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00K"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00J",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR00J",
    "text": "**Pipeline Composition**: Using tools like `unified.js`, the core provides a base pipeline (`unified().use(remarkParse)`). A Domain KG injects its own specific plugins (`.use(domainSpecificYamlPlugin)`) into the chain.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00H",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00K",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6KR00K",
    "text": "**OOP Class Inheritance**: A domain script can extend a core parser class (`class ArchitectureTransducer extends BaseMarkdownTransducer`) to override specific parsing behaviors.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6KR00H",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "# Aperas Fractal Ontology - Implementation Plan",
    "text": "This plan outlines the steps to refactor the Aperas Knowledge Graph codebase to implement the fractal block-tree ontology designed in `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md`. Stage 1 is fully implemented and live-verified; Stage 2 is implemented except items 4 (Link Extraction) and 5 (Title vs. Text Split), which remain open; Stage 3 (Database & UI Alignment) hasn't started. See `Aperas-dev-status.md` for current status and `Aperas-architecture.md` for the as-built reference. Node identity evolved past what Stage 2 anticipated (content-addressed hashing was tried and superseded by Snowflake-style ids — see `Aperas-core-ontology-design.md` Appendix F); this plan is kept as a historical record, not a live checklist.",
    "parent": "ArtifactNode:00CBFDW978001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00E",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00M"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6PR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "true"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6PR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "## Stage 1: Core Schema Overhaul",
    "text": "**Target Files**: Create `web/src/lib/schema.json` and delete `web/src/lib/schema.ts`",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG003",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG004",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG005",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG006",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00A"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG003",
    "text": "**Eliminate `schema.ts`**: The current `schema.ts` file contains useless TypeScript interfaces that type the TerminusDB schema metadata (e.g., `docId: \"xsd:string\"`). This is a double-maintenance anti-pattern. We will delete `schema.ts` entirely.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG004",
    "text": "**Create `schema.json`**: Create a pure, static JSON array file representing the raw JSON-LD schema required by TerminusDB.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG005",
    "text": "**Clean Slate**: Do not port over the legacy `DocumentNode`, `SpanNode`, or `TripleAssertion` definitions to the new JSON file.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG006:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6Q0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG006:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6Q0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG006",
    "text": "**Implement Lineage (in `schema.json`)**:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG007",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG008",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG009"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG007",
    "text": "Define `BaseNode` (Abstract class, `links: Set<BaseLink>`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG008",
    "text": "Define `BaseLink` (Inherits `BaseNode`, adds `target: BaseNode`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG009",
    "text": "Define `BaseEdge` (Inherits `BaseLink`, adds `source: BaseNode`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00A:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6Q8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00A:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6Q8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG00A",
    "text": "**Implement Topology (in `schema.json`)**:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00B",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00C",
      "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00D"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG00B",
    "text": "Define `FolderNode` (Inherits `BaseNode`, adds `title`, `path`, `text`, `children: List<BaseNode>`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG00C",
    "text": "Define `BlockNode` (Inherits `BaseNode`, adds `title`, `text`, `children: List<BlockNode>`, `unfolded: boolean`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CBFDZ6PG00D",
    "text": "Define `ArtifactNode` (adds `path`, `contentHash`, `root: BlockNode`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CBFDZ6PG00E:props:StringProp:00CBFDZ6QG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
